      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES.</head>
        <p>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES.</p>
        <p>By Veronica.</p>
        <p>A NEW PRINTING PAPER.</p>
        <p>Ensyna "is the name that has been given to a new printing paper, sad it appears likely to create a new revolution in the methods of printing. It is something totally different from the papers that have hitherto been used. It can be handled in subdued light in the same way as the so-called gaslight papers, but its com- position is entirely different. By its aid, prints having all the appearance of gold-toned P. 0. P. can he pro- duced and finished only with the exception of drying in the course of seven cr eight minutes. An astonishing fact in connection with this paper is the great latitude that is possible in the way of exposure. You may expose to gaslight and get a good print in bairn minute. You may also expose the same negative for ten minutes, and you get an equally good result, the only difference between the two prints is the colour. Short exposure give purple tones, and long exposures give a much N‘arrner shade. In fact, there is a range of tones obtain- able from purple to sepia. METHODS OF WORKING.</p>
        <p>Have ready a jug of water and three clean dishes one of them for developing, one for fixing, and one for washing. Fill the frame by placing the sensitive side which is hollow against the film of the negetive, taking care, of ccurse that no direct rays of bright light fall upon the paper. The exposure may be made by holding the frame to the window, but not in direct sunlight, or close up to any artificial light. 5, ith an average nega- tive, the exposure may be made to an ordinary incandescent gas flaree,holding the flame twelve inches away in about thirty seconds. Taking the paper out of the frame, and place it face upwards in the developing dish. Pour clean water over the paper and let it remain until the paper is quite Lrnp. This will take about half a minute. The pour this washing water away. You then pour on the special developer, flowing it over the paper, and keeping the dish gently rocking until the image attains its correct depth. The picture does not appear quickly as in the use of ordinary gaslight papers, hut takes some half a minute or so to appear, and development is usually complete in about two or three minutes. You want to pour the developer away before the image hr's fully developed. Then pour clean water out of the jug over the print directiy the developing solution is removed, and gently rock until the development,which continues in the water, is quite complete. You then pick the print out and immerse it into the hypo solntion, face upwards. Great care must be taken so AS not to touch the hypo with your fingers, as this will cause great trouble in handling fresh pieces of paper. A good thing is to get a flat, wooden ruler or a celluloid paper knife, and by this means lift the print out of the hypo as soon PS it is fixed, which is the case in the short epece of time of 34 seconds. Theprint is then to be put into the washing water and can be allowed to wash for at least two minutes. Longer washing will not affect the print, but it is not necessary. This short 'washing seems to upset all our theories of the past 011 the nece=sity of thorough washing after a print has been in the hypo, but the composition of the image on " Ensyna" paper is totally different, the method of development being a physical one. The image is built up by small particles of pure silver in various states of division, giving the different tones. The image thus formed is practically permanent, so that even if a trace of hypo still remain in the paper, it would have no deleterious effect on the print. THE DEVELOPER.</p>
        <p>The developer is a special one, called " Fnsynoid," and it is or.ly vvith this developer that it is any use to Rttemp t to develop the prints. The developer gradually increases in strength during the time of development. It is therefore recommended to only use the developer .once, otherwise you are using a developer of increasing etrength each time, vvhich would lead to trouble. VARIETIES OF NEGATIVES.</p>
        <p>It is remarkable what regular prints are obtainable 'when using negatives which are very different in character. I made some experiments with negatives that were soft and very full of detail, and also with one that was rather under expr•sed, with many strong con- trasts, and which with P.O.P. printing, in the latter IMO I could never get a really good print, but on this paper, I got cqually good results from Loth class of xtegative.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CINEMATOGRAPH FIRES.</head>
        <p>CINEMATOGRAPH FIRES.</p>
        <p>Owing to the disastrous critastrophies which have occurred on several occasions in connection with the exhibition of cinematographic pictures, where the film which is made of celluloid has caught fire, has resulted in certain manufacturers turning their attention to the supply of a film-support other than celluloid. This has now been successfully accomplished Ly the well-known firm of Lumiere and Co. They have produced a film which is non-inflammable, composed of gelatine. At a recent demonstration, various experiments were made to set fire to this film, but it absolutely refused to burn v%ith a flame. It only fizzled up and destroyed the picture, but no more.</p>
      </div>
