      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>VELVETEEN AND BRAID..</head>
        <p>VELVETEEN AND BRAID..</p>
        <p>Velveteen, once a despised material, has now reached such perfection in manufacture that it is worn by the wealthiest. A race costume achieved in prune-coloured velveteen has the whole of the front strapped across with black silk braid and buttons. The half- length coat is trimmed to match down the fronts ancl.down the backs of the long, tight sleeves. Another, in a lovely shade of bluish grey, is intended for wear at races, and is modified Directoire •in shape, with a wida plastron down the whole of the back, this being furnished with buttons and loops on either side, making it look almost as though it were nailed on to the gown. The collar, vest, and sleeves are in white transparent tucked Ninon, a chilly fashion which still prevals, the idea being that under a warm fur cat the arms vill be supplementarily protected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>PRETTY DRESS ACCESSORIES.</head>
        <p>PRETTY DRESS ACCESSORIES.</p>
        <p>Our sketch shews some dainty accessories of lace so useful at this time of the year for smartening up a day or evening gown. The pelerine is of point d'esprit net, and can bo worn over any simply-made evening gown. It can be realised either in black or ivory net. It consists of two long pieces of net arranged in tucks on the shoulders, and the fulness caught in by the belt at the waist. At the</p>
        <p>back the two edges meet at the waist-line, and the whole is bordered with filet lace in- sertion, and edged with a very tiny cream silk ban fringe, the belt or girdle being of gold tinsel. The yoke and sleeves," for wearing with a low-cut -bodice, are composed of wide lace. The yoke is mounted on to a slip of cambric or silk, and the lace arranged to form a square, with the edge towagds the centre, the square opening being filled in with fine lace or net, and crossed with little straps of velvet to match the colour of the costume with which it is to be worm THE LATEST EXPLORER.</p>
        <p>. Mies Charlotte Mansfield, who sails for South Africa about December 20th, intends to start on the journey overland from the Cape to Cairo. If she succeeds, she will, M. A. /'. says, be the first woman ta go a10n4,, this par- ticular route under the same conditions. She will train from the Cape to beyond the Vie- toria Falls, and then .set out on a march • by caravan to Lake Tanganyika, and thence overland to the source of the Nile and Cairo. Her party .will consist of some thirty porters and bearers, and four personal attendants, till natives, who will come from Nairobi, and she will, of course a st,De; 4^ea. onn•irt</p>
