      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>MusHnoom TIMBALES. Mushroom tim- bales form a delicious entree, and are also suitable for breakfast. For these we require six ounces of mushrooms, four ounces of tomato or cooked beet, two ounces of bread- crumbs, one once of butter, one egg, the juice of half a lemon, and a little stock or milk. Beat the butter to a cream, add the yolk of egg, and beat for a few minutes longer. Chop up the mushrooms and tomato, and add them, also the breaderumbs, and enough stock' or milk to moisten the whole. Season to taste. Whip the white till quite stiff, then mix in very liehtly. Pour the mixture into small but- tered cups or moulds, which might be deco- rated with tiny strips of beet, and steam very' gently for one hour. Turn out carefully; and serve with hot tomato sauce.</p>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>ELECTRIC CLEANSING COMPOUND</p>
        <p>The following is the recipe or prescription for making a good spot - removing prepara- tion: To 500 parts of water add 30 parts of glycerine, 7 parts of the strongest caustic am- monia, and 30 parts of ether; mix all thoroughly, then add 500 parts of water and 30 parts of white olive oil soap, shaking thoroughly until everything is Jlissolved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Changed by a Chill.</head>
        <p>Changed by a Chill.</p>
        <p>11 A busy man exhuasted and broken in 1 health. Restored to full strength by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>In spite of an apparently sfund constitution and being accustomed to an outdoor life, Mr. Joseph Bradley, the well-known proprietor of the Coal Depot, Holden Street, Radford, Nottingham, dis- covered that he could no more afford to disregard the dangers of a chill than any other man with responsi- bilities.</p>
        <p>"A neglected chill," said Mr. Bradley, " reduced me to a physical wreck, and, from the first, con- siderably interfered with my business. Further, my associat.ton with a friendly society whose influence extends throughout the country, means that I have to attend many evening meetings, and often leave heated rooms to come out into the cold damp air. Through the chill my health gave way rapidly, and I lost all energy, being fagged by the least exertion. After only a light meal I would become dull and drowsy, and when taking a few mouthfuls of food I experienced acute pains across my chest and back. Sick head- aches often seized me, and although I felt tired and `ifeless during the daytime, the moment I went to bed - sufferings increased and could get no rest. "Although' roar- ing fires were kept up in my room, I constantly shi- vered with the clotld ; indeed, my blood felt like ice- w ater running through my veins. ;hose who knew me could not help remarking h o w pale and wasted I was. My eyes were heavy and dull and I was most woe- 'u II y depressed. Iy heart felt weak md palpitated to such an extent that .is increased and I ould get no rest." I really thought it would burst. "I took a great deal of so-callal tonic medicines but my strength failed me more and mire, and every effort to swallow nourishment resulted in sickness. The least noise irritated me, and I felt that I should have to give up business and all responsibility. "But one day a customer begged of me to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Well, I did and after taking these pills for a time I certainly experienced a decided change for the better, and found my appetite again. I felt more cheerful every day and as I continued taking the pills I steadily got strong and well. " My blood was warmed and nourished by Dr. Williams' Pink I'ills, and I no longer wanted to be constantly near a fire. I regained my lost weight, felt comfoi table and well nourished. My nerves became as steady as a rock, and I was thoroughly fitted for the active life required of me." TII; an. WILLIAMS' WAT.</p>
        <p>By making the Blood Rich, Red and Nourishing, and thus invigorating the entire system, all the evils ,of Chills may be prevented as well ns cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, which have direct action on the blood. They have cured not only Chills and the Aftereffects of Influenza' Bronchitis, etc., but also the many forms of Nervous Disorders, Anemia, Indiges- tion, Rheumatism, Sciatica, Neuralgia and the aches and ills of women. Obtainable of all dealers, or direct from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, post free, 2a. 9d. for one box, or 13s. 9d. for six boxes. Substitutes will not cure you ; the genuine pills always bear the full name, seven words on the wrapper, thus—Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People.</p>
