        <head>MR. SNOWDEN REFUSED A HEARING.</head>
        <p>MR. SNOWDEN REFUSED A HEARING.</p>
        <p>pith of their are of considerable geological importance. In Rev. H. Donnie° Presides at Rowdy meeting. various parts of the world may be found de. Tumultuous scenes were witnessed at the Picton posits of their silic.eous frustules, which have Hall, Liverpool last night week in connection with a meeting held under the, auspices of the Independent Labour Party on the subject of unemployment,the Bev. or lakes Thus Oran in Algiers, ancient seas . , accumulated in the past on the floors of Herbert Dormice being in the chair. There were about is noted for its beautiful fossil forms. In seven or eight hundred persons present, and from the t Bermuda' there is a famous deposit, while the beginning it was evident that a strong minority were , city of Eichmond, in Virginia, is practically determined to prevent Mr. Philip Snowden, M.P., and ; built upon a bed of fossil diatoms no less than Mrs. Brace Giesler, the two chief speakers announced, 18 feet in thickness. Fresh-water deposits from making their speeches. In the interval of waiting which have yielded many species exist for the chairman to open the meeting, a large section sing. of the assemblage amused themselves by in Norway and Sweden, South Mourne, Ireland, in the island of Mull, Scotland, and lug Socialist hymns, interspered with the, at Dolgelly, North Wales. In Iceland and parodied sons, " Dare to be a Grayson " and , • We'll hang Philip Snow Lapland, a. Snowdon en a Sour Apple Tree." and in the plains of the Amazon f When the Rev. Herbert Dtinuico rose to speak he thick beds formed and in Min, Bohemia, are was greeted by a storm of hissing and booing, which !of countless millions of fossil diatoms. The lasted at least a quarter of an hour. His words were well-known Tripoli powder so much used for not audible even to those of the audience who were , polishing is almost entirely composed of the seated near the platform. The Chairman shouted, frustules of one or two species. In many of "Wilt you make a bargain with tee ?" but his solo these-deposits we find the same genera that answer was "Dare to be a Grayson." The remaining exist in the lakes, rivers, and oceans of our forty-five minutes might be described as pandemonium, own times. . the only quiet moments being when the rowdy portion of the audience paused to gather breath. Then the I storm ,as renewed; with the accompaniment of groans for Keir Hardie and Snowden, cheers for Grayson, and hisses for the Socialist soup van. Suddenly a respectably-dressed man rose and called for silence. After three minutes he succeeded in j obtaining it. " Mr. Chairman," he cried, " will you ask Mr. Snowden to substantiate. or withdraw, at once his remark about Mr. Grayson's dinners ?" Mr. Dunitico ' announced that seven minutes would be given at the tend of Mr. Snowden's address for the answering of questions, but this only created a rew storm more j siolent than any which preceedecl it. Loud cries arose ; fuom the galleries, where the "Monument unemployed" ; teemed to be strongly represented, among other senti- , ments heard being Wile lives on labour ? Philip ; Snowden !" " Who is a blood-sucker ? Philip Snowden." " Who is a traitor to the workers ? Philip Snowden. Finally the Chairman asked that these in favour of ; Mr. Snowden should stand up. At least three-fourths ! of the audience responded to thia appeal, yet when be ! attempted to pave the way for Mr. Snowden,the rowdi- ness became even more pronounced. At last in despair ' the Chairmen announced that the meeting was closed, and Mr. Snowden and Mrs. Giesler left the platform. , The Chairman then said that he had made many sacri- fices for the Socialist cruise, but if he thought that the men who had broken up the meeting were representatives of the Socialist movement he would immediately abandon Socialism. This was the signal for another outburst, and the meeting finally broke up in great disorder.</p>
        <p>WEATHER PREDICTION.</p>
        <p>The art of forecasting seasons, more or less definitely, will, writes Mr. Alex. B. Mac- . Dowall, in S ymon Meteorological Maga- zine be acquired ir quie ; after many at- tempts, and many stumbles. It seems a legi- ' timate question, discussable at any time: Here is a piepe of knowledge available before a given seaeon ; is it, or 16 it not, a sufficient reason for • expecting the season to be so-mid- st:if In the course of these studies one, comes tierces many s..iqueaces or tendencies with ap- parently high probability. It might be a good thing to tabulate such for practical. use. For example, when the first three months of the year (at Greenwich) are all dry, we have gonvrally a dry summer (11 cases out of 13) ; when we have 20 or more days with 80deg. or more in the hot season the autumn is gene- rally warm • (hi cases • out of 19). Among other helpful facts is a relation which the writer has noted between Pothesay summers and Greenwich winters following— both regarded as typical for a region. What- ever the explanation- (perhaps sun-spot in- fluence manifest in - both), it appears that after a very wet summer at Rothceay we -may look pretty confidently for a mild winter fi.- 6on at Greenwich .(one -with less cold than the average), and after •a " very dry summer a severe winter. Corresponding inferences may be drawn from very severe or very mild Greenwich winters to Rothesay summers. It seems, the writer adds, quite possible on scientific grounds to predict " wetness " or " dryness " for a Greenwich summer (in rela- tion to average).</p>
        <p>THE HEW 6EE EA-R-1118. Prepared solely from Beet and Vegetable. Makes delicious rich gravies or a cup of strong Beef Tea for a penny. Ask your grocer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Cuto- 0</head>
        <p>Cuto- 0</p>
        <p>ACID-RESISTING CEMENT.</p>
        <p>The Brass World gives the following formula for an acid-resisting cement, for tanks, floors, ex. Silicate of soda (water glass), 6 parts; glycerine, 1 part; red lead, 81 parts; fine cinders, 10 parts. The silicate of soda and glycerine are mixed and then the red lead and cinders added to make a mass resembling putty. This cement soon sets or hardens, and when heated to the temperature of boiling water' unites with brick or Port- land cement to form a strong joint.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>GENIUS AND BRAIN DISEASE.</head>
        <p>GENIUS AND BRAIN DISEASE.</p>
        <p>Professor von Hanseman, of the Berlin ;University, has examined the brain of Theo- dore Mommsen, the great historian. He states that he, like Hermann von Helmholtz, the physiologist, and Adolph Menzel, the painter, was the subject of hydrocephalus (an accumu- lation of fluid in the ventricles of the brain). He suggested that on this basis an explana- tion may be offered of Mommsen's eccentri- city, Menzel's extreme irritability and occa- sional fainting fits, and the spasmodic seizures from which Helmholtz suffered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>EXPLOSIONS WITHOUT FLAME.</head>
        <p>EXPLOSIONS WITHOUT FLAME.</p>
        <p>M.— Dantriche has announced to the Academie des Sciences of France that if to ordinary powder a little more potash of soda is added at the moment of explosion a cloud of dust is formed which prevents the com- bustion of gas. Thus- it would be possible to suppress the flame which is produced at • the mouth of firearms, and which often betrays at a great distance the position of a battery or group of riflemen. On the other hand, an explosive without flame would be especially valuable in the working of mines, since it would be without effect on many com- bustibles, such as firedamp and coal dust.</p>
        <p>TAPPING THE EARTH FOR ELECTRICITY.</p>
        <p>As pure water, Mineral waters, petroleum, gas, and heat can be obtained from di ep borings, it appears worth while, remarks , a writer in the Scientific American, to make an Attempt to obtain electrical energy from the interior of the earth by the same means. I have already published numerous instances of the magnetisation of driven well tubes. Some tubes, driven less than I,oooft. into thg earth, become magnetised strongly enough to sus- tain the weight of large iron keys placed in contact with the protruding tops of the tubes. We know, furthermore, that a measurable electric current is generated when two elec- trodes, connected by a wire, are immersed in solutions of different temperatures or dif- ferent degrees of concentration. Now the earth contains various liquids, the pressure and temperature of which increase according to their depth below the surface. This dis- tribution must produce electric currents, and these may possibly be the most available subterranean source of electric energy, for to every depth corresponds a certain constant pressure, and when this pressure is removed (as by a boring) the temperature immediately riaes.</p>
        <p>, INVISIBLE LIGHT. The dictum of the French savant, M. Gus- tave le Bon, that there are in nature no dark bodies, but only imperfect eyes, seems to have been anticipated in some remarkable re- searches carried out more than half a century ago by Baron von Reichenbach. Every body, even in a perfectly darkened room, according to this view is giving out light vibrations of some sort. Only a small proportion of the light emitted, however, is perceptible to the human eye. And it is probable, remarks the Globe, that as some human ears can perceive sounds too shrill for others, so there are eyes which can perceive light invisible to others. It is also probable that the same eye may differ in its powers of perception under differ- ent conditions of health, Sze.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>LIGHT FROM MAGNETS.</head>
        <p>LIGHT FROM MAGNETS.</p>
        <p>It is a well-known fact that in certain pathological states the senses become more acute. Von Reichenbach's experiments were largely concerned with testing the powers of perception of those whoae senses were ren- dered more acute by disease. One of the most remarkable results was that such per- sons saw streams of light proceeding from the poles of a magnet in a darkened room. When the keeper was placed on the magnet the light disappeared, reappearing when it was again removed. When the patients after- wards made drawings of the appearances these bore a certain resemblance to the familiar lines of magnetic force.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>LIGHT FROM CRYSTALS.</head>
        <p>LIGHT FROM CRYSTALS.</p>
        <p>Further experiments were tried with vari- ous crystals. It was found that the same in- dividuals perceived light emanating from these also in a darkened room. In some cases also light was perceived _proceeding from the fingers of a hand held up in the same. Most of the individuals experimented with were those whose senses were rendered abnormally acute by disease, but a few in normal health were found sufficiently sensitive.</p>
        <p>FOSSIL DIATOMS. " Diatoms are minute creatures which are found both in fresh, and salt water, and are distinguished from the diamids, their near relations, by the po3secision of a firm coating of silica, so hard that it will resist fire or acid. In spite of their minuteness, diatoms</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>FATHER WHITTLE'S JUBILEE.</head>
        <p>FATHER WHITTLE'S JUBILEE.</p>
        <p>Piesentallon at the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>The Very Rev. Father Whittle, 0.5. R., rector of St. Alban', Warrington, celebrated the fiftieth anniversary of his monastic profession on Tuesday and occasion was taken by tho members of his flock and numerous other friends and admirers to express in tangible form their deep respect for him. In the morning Father Whittle sang the High Mass of Thanksgiving at St. Alban's Church, assisted by the Deacon and Sob-deacon, Father Swarbreck and McLoughlin respectively. A choir of priests sang the mein of the Mass, and the Right Rev. Abbot of Ampleforth delivered an address, in which he congratulated Father Whittle on his jubilee and referred particularly to his early life and to his membership of the Order of St. Benedict. . . . . . . .</p>
        <p>Prior to he service, the bells of St. Mary's Church, Buttermarket-street, rang out a joyous peal in honour of the occasion.</p>
        <p>The Parr Hall was crowded in the evening when the' publics presentation of an iiluminsted address and purse of gold was made. The Right Rev. the Abbott of Ampleforth presided and the other clergy present were the Revs. Prior Burge, Fathers I Wilson, Bugging, Feeney, Worden, Clarkson, Darby, Prinsavesi, Jackson, Dr. Mercer, Rylance and Mc Loughlin. Amongst those present on the platform were the Mayor (Alderman Forsbaw), the ex-Moyor (Alderman Smethurst), Rev. W. Bracecamp (vicar of, St. Paul's), Councillor- Toole and Mr. Peter Delaney (secretary). The ABBOT OF AMPLEFORTH gave a sketch of Father Whittle's career as a priest, pointing out that his first curacy was at St. Alban's, Warrington, where he began i work n 1869. He had, however, only been in Warring- ton for three weeks when he was stricken down with smallpox. From Warrington he went to St. Mary's, Liverpool, and from thence to St. Mary's, Wootton. In 18R0 he became Prior of Ampleforth, and ruled over the destinies of that great establishment for three years. He was then appointed rector of the large parish of St. Anne's, Liverpool, and there he carried out a large scheme of enlargement and beautification, making the church a centre of true liturgy in the diocese of Liver- pool. After this strennons work in Liverpool he went to recuperate in the bracing climate of Maryport, and having spent three years there he came back as rector to St. Alban's, Warrington. (Applause.) At all the places where he laboured the love of the beauty of the House of God was upon him, and p great distinguishing feature of his work had been the way in which be had carried out schemes of improvement. At St. Alban's, Warrington, be had beautified the windows in a way which was quite a wonder to those who knew the Church before. He bad not finished yet, for he hoped very shortly to make the exterior of the church more befitting the beauty of the interior. (Applause.) Wherever be had gone, Father Whittle had attracted friends to him and he still had the power of attracting friends from all denominations and classes. (Applause.) It must be a proud moment for him now to see that he was supported by so many *whom he had known for so many years in Warrington. (Applause.) The reason of that attraction that vras in him was that he had exhibited to his brethren and to the congregations that he had been set over the true priestly spirit in his care for the I people, his devotion to the sick, his regularity and , punctuality in the work of the church, his kindly and I sympathetic manner,and sterling good sense. (Applause) Zl r. Preen DELANEY then read the address as follows: "To the Very Reverend J, P. Whittle, Cathedral Prior of Rochester.—The jubilee of the day of your monastic profession has given us an opportunity, long desired, to express to you our senthnents of gratitude and affection. It is not our English custom under ordinary circum- stances to speak of such things. We do not make public display of our feelings ; the best and holiest we keep concealed in our hearts. The bond of sympathy between a pastor and his flock is one of these. It is a sacred sympathy, and, therefore, a silent one. The priest does his duty, and oftentimes more than his duty, without a thought of recognition. His people respond • to his care by their silent obedience and reverence. Both are content to leave reward and praise unmen- tioned in this life. But there are occasions when this rule of silence is haptily and gracefully broken. There are times of unusual joys or grief. Then all reserve is at an end, and we count it a privilege openly to share in the rejoicing and mourning of our friends. In suoh a communion of spirit we ask, deer father, to be per- mitted to take a public part in your rejoicing. It is a glad day to you and we wish to add to your joy by an open expression of our sympathy. We desire, at the same time, to thank you for your labonrs in our service, and your unflagging devotion to the interests of St. Alban's. We hope you will accept the gift we have brought you as a testimony of sincere friendship and , gratitude. Signed on behalf of the Committee, chair. I man, John Richardson ; treasurer, Alfred B. Marsh ; secretary, Peter Delaney." Father BUCIOINS stated that the purse contained a cheque for £1.40. Other subscriptions had yet to come in: and the Confraternity of the Children of Mary had raised from £2O to £3O. The school children were making a presentation, and the total subscription would ultimately reach about .UOO. (Applause.) The Amen OF AMPLEFORTH then handed the purse to Father Whittle, amid cheers. The MAYOR, who had a very cordial reception, 'Aid he was pleased to be present as Mayor to pay his respects to their good old veteran Father Whittle and to congratulate him upon having attained his jubilee. They all very much admired Father Whittle and he (the Mayor) was sure that all his flock and everybody con- nected with him were proud of him. (Applause.) Father Whittle was a gentleman who was given to good works and he was constantly dispensing charity amongst the poor. Ile was pleased that they had that night paid honour to whom honour was due and be hoped Father Whittle would live for many years to continue his good and useful work. (Applause.) Father Wurrrr.e was cheered again and again on rising to respond. He said he was 70 years of age, but he had never found himself in such a position as that before. It was impossible to reply to all the nice things which the Abbott and the Mayor had said about him. On a recent occasion that veteran Cabinet Minister, Lord Ripon, in reply to the toast of his health, said " I suppose I must allow for the exaggeration of a friend," and be (Father Whittle) supposed ho must allow for the exaggeration with regard to himself, because it was ell exaggeration. (Cries of "No, no.") He had only done what he could, and if he lived long enough would do it again. (Cheers.) He could not deny that during his forty years as a priest he had loved the glory and beauty of the House of God, lie hoped before the end of 1609 to make the front of St. Alban's Church a little more in keeping with the inside. (Applause.) He thanked all who bad contributed to the testimonial, and was thankful for the text of the address, which bad gone to his heart because it spoke of the sympathy that had ee'er existed and should exist between a priest and his people—the great mutual love and respect too. He respected end loved his people, and he knew they respected and loved him. He thanked the Mayor, Deputy-Mayor and Mr. Brececamp for their presence. lie and Mr. Bracecnmp might differ on certain points, but they agreed to respect each other and work together for good. Father Whittle concluded by thanking everybody for their great kindness to him and especially mentioned his "darling children" of St. Alban's. He prayed God to heats His graces and bless- ings upon every family in Warrington. (Applause.) On the motion of Father Brooms, seconded by Councillor Toosesthe Abbot was thanked for presiding. Father WHITTLE said be had forgotten to thank Father Buggies for his work in connection with the testimonial. The success which had been achieved was greatly due to his exertions. .(Applaase.) During the evening an admirable, entertainment was given, consisting of costume and action songs by the infants and an operetta entitled" Snow White and the Seven Drawfs "by the senior childsen. The characters were taken by the following :—Princess Snow White, Miss Maggie Kilgallon ; Queen, Miss Annie Delaney ; Prince, Master NVilliam Burke; Carl, the Huntsman, Master Leo Delaney. Special features were a bunters' dance by Masters °Leo and Cyril Delaney and Jack lies!ey, and a skirt dance by Miss Maggie Holland. he children bed been splendidly trained and the per- formence did them and their teachers infinite credit. Miss D. Dillon mt.s an able accompanist.</p>
        <p>All renders who are victims of Chills and dangerous ofter-flects should write protni tly to the Dr. Williams' ?didicine Co., 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, for information that may be of considerable assistance in disrelling their ailments. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WARRINGTON TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TEACHERS' ASSOCIATION</p>
        <p>The School Games.</p>
        <p>A general meeting of the \Carrington and District Teachers' Association was held in the \\ryeliffe Memorial Building on Saturday morning under the presidency of Mr. T. Bowen (president). There was a large attend- ance.—An interesting report on the athletic undertakings of the past year was submitted by Mr. Bowen who is the secretary of the Sports Committee. He stated that it was his pleasing duty to again report success all along the line in the various sports fostered and controlled by the Sports Committee. Dealing first with the football season, ending on April 30th of this year, interest was well sustained to the end. It was especially fitting that St. Mary's team should head the League, as Mr. Price, the indefatigable football secretary, would feel repaid in some measure for his arduous labours. His team were now the proud possessors of the Dodds challenge shield, until the end of the present season. In the spring the Warrington Schoolboys' team tried conclusions with the Bolton team, but they bad yet to secure their first victory against an outside team. However, it was gratifying to notice that whilst the Warrington boys had not yet won their opponents had had to strain every nerve to secure victory. In the rounders competition the girls again displayed unbounded enthusiasm in their games, and so even was the play that three teams—St. Alban's, ' St. Barnabas', and Silver-street--tied for the first place in the League. The committee decided that these three should play off for the championship. St. Alban's deservedly earned the title by their brilliant victories over their rivals, and carried off the handsome rose bowl which was handed to them by the donor, Councillor Fulton-Smith. This trophy they would hold until the end of next summer. The thanks of the Com- mittee were due to Miss Orton, of Wycliffe, who so ably discharged the onerous duties of rounders secretary. The Sports Committee decided not to form a cricket league chiefly owing to the fact that so many schools experienced a difficulty in securing suitable playing grounds. Nevertheless several enjoyable friendly matches were played, and It was hoped that next year arrangements might be made whereby a successful schools' cricket league might be established. Reference was made to the success of the athletic festival and tbe swimming gala, which was due in a great measure to the work of Nil% Kilburn, as secretary. With repard to the gala, Arploy-street team again secured the Walker challenge shield, with Silver-street as runners up. The general character of the swimming walla revelation to the spectators, and was proof positive of the good work done by the Education Committee in sending boys to the baths for instruction during school hours. There was no doubt that the instruction would Le equally beneficial to the girls if the same privilege were extended to them, and it was very encouraging that the education authority was doing everything everything possible to achieve this desirable object. Football matters this season were again flourishing, due largely to the admirable work done by M.. Price as secretary. In the English Shield Competition the Warrington boys , were decidedly unfortunate to lose in the first round against the Bootle team, and very little would have turned the balance in their favour. The adoption of the report was moved by Mr. W. 11. STANSPIELD, who paid Mr. Bowen a special compliment for the ardent and enthusiastic manner in which he had worked for the benefit of the children.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOHN CANN, local secretary to the Benevolent and Orphan Fund, referred to the special efforts being made by the teachers in Lancashire elementary schools to augment their subscription to this worthy object, and stated that about £77 would be raised in Warrington. It was then decided to add £lO from the balance in hand from the Sports Committee account, and further to supplement this by voting a sum from the funds of the association to complete a grand total of £lOO. It was decided to support the eledidatures of Miss Cleghorn (Sheffield) for vice-pnsident, and Messrs. Sharpies, Pearson, Sidobottom, and Allcott as members of the executive representing Lancashire.—The local association will be represented at the next conference , of National Union of Teachers at MoreoaMbe by Messrs. John Upson, T. Bowen, F. 11. Bowen, F. 11. Longshaw, W. IT. Stansfield, J. Cann and G. Newburn. The Director of Education, Mr. J. Moore Murray, attended the meeting for the purpose of talking over with the teachers the question of the supervision of the children during the meals which are to be provided Iby the Education Commiteee. Ile was very favourably received and the general feeling seemed to be that the assistance required would be readily forthcoming.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>PARENTS WHO PRAISE PEPS.</head>
