      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>St. Peter's Church. SPeeial sormons pleading the foreign missionary ~..anee were preached at St. Peter's Church on Sunday, "":".eing and evening by the Rev. Canon Plummer, rector of Halewood. There were large congregations and the addresses proved very instructive and inspiring. The offertories were on behalf of the Church Mission- all Society, and, a satisfactOry sum was realized.</p>
        <p>Orford Old Boys at Church. _ On Sunday evening about a hundred members of the Orford Old Boys' Reunion attended service at the new Church of St. Margaret and All Hallows, Orford. They Tuarched from the schools in processional order, and Ivere accommodated with seats on the North side. There was a crowded congregation. The service was conducted by the Rev. G. Vale Owen, and the lessons ;ere read by Mr. John Cann (president of the ,eunion) and Councillor Steel (hon. treasurer). The II". G. Vale Owen preached from the words " Watch- ."ao, what of the night." Tie referred to the kindness of the Old Boys 'who had decided to purchase and pre-) sent to the church an oak lectern, and said he bad had ;I.letter from the Bishop in which his lordship wished turn (dr. Vale Owen) to convey to them his very great thanks for their kindness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Church Lads' Brigade.</head>
        <p>Church Lads' Brigade.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning the combined companies of St. Ilr,hires, St:. Clement's, and Stockton Heath Church Brigade attended divine service at the Parish '-hurch. About 70 officers and lids were on parade, accompanied by the band of the Stockton Heath Con- any. In charge of the Stockton Heath Company were NlPtain Wage and Lieutenant Hepherd ; with ?rgeant-Insnector 1-Tobin ; St. Clement's Company, eutenant Sankey and Lieutenant Gibson ; and St. Phin's. Captain -Brookes and Lieutenant Coates. The T. AV. Barber, chaplain of St. Clement's Company, preached from the Gospel of the day, and exhorted the ads to be loyal and true to the cause they represented, and with steady purpose, ready and cheerful obedience t° uphold and carry out the objects for which they were enrolled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Wyciffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wyciffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>slThere was a gond attendance at this meeting on 'day last, presided over by Mr. Joseph Owen, who 8110ke upon the" Divinity of Christ." Mr. R. J. Peake, 13!' Grappenhall, gave the address upon "Sampson and 18 Position with God,"--his great strength and his 12s.rfect wealatess. His strength was conditional on /MU:fulness and obedience; commands broken made him ;eat% So we had our commands from God, and His if we faithfully 'kept it, would make us strong to e_ndure and to resist any temptation, but if we allowed Delilahs to come in and deceive us, then we became teak na other men. Let us be careful, guarded, and •rue, then we should he safe. Mr. Smiley also took part the meeting. Miss Amour sang, "The Man of " and "My Father Knows," which were well eceived , . Iwo resolutions was passed unanimously : Ist, "That "e resent the nction of the tfouse of Lords, in rejecting e Licensintt Bill, and thank the Prime Minister and Government for their efforts to pass the 13111 into ; 2nd, "That we implore the Government at an ,irIV date to brina about such measures as will relieve rgreat sn fferiniof the nnernployed and their families." zad "that these resolutions be forwarded at once to Ord Lansdowne, the leader of the opposition in the °use of Commons, the Prime Minister and the Member °,r the Borough, Mr. Crosfleld." ulhe resolutions were proposed by Mr. B. Farrar, Tneonded by Mr. Bradley, and carried unanimously. ,a Secretary on behalf of Mr. Gardner and family, urned thaiiks for the letter of sympathy sent last '"eek to them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Institution of the New Vicar of Orford.</head>
        <p>Institution of the New Vicar of Orford.</p>
        <p>t The institution of the Rev. George Vale Owen to the elletice of St. Margaret and All Hallows, Orford, was 'formed on Monday evening by the Bishop of Liver- .l):ol. There was a good congregation, and the service 89 most impressive. The clergy present, in addition " the Rev G. Vale Owen, were the Re's. Canon Willis, 1, W. Dickinson, H. R. Wild, T. A. J. Ridpath, W. (Ashton-in-Makerfield), and L. W. Thomas 1S st, Alban', Broadheath). 05The Bishop took for his text "Let a man so account II:Ile, as of the ministers of Christ, and stewards of mysteries of God. Moreover it is required in '," leWards that a man be found faithful,"and urged upon e clergy and laity to do the best they could in the ri:rishes they represented. He pointed out that the trY required them (the clergy) to be so busy about (',ir parishes that they had not sufficient time to read q mediate upon Holy Scripture, and much of the tllverty of the pulpit was due to that fact. In cen- t, "ion. his Lordship impressed upon the laity the recessity of giving every assistance in their power to Oe clergy, "Too often "he said "the laity think they ye their duty if they have done part of the secular We are most grateful to them fordoing that, :,rld they can very often do it a great deal better than can, but we want others, whom God has given :ifrerent, gifts, to help us in the more spiritual part of ..I,llr Work, to help us not only with their prayers, hut °l' their personal services in spiritual things." The ;e•remony of instituting the Rev. G. Vale Owen was proceeded with,and at the conclusion the offertory 089 take in aid of the Ordination Candidates' '4llibition Fund (Bishop of Liverpool's Fund).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>' The Messiah" at Legh-street.</head>
        <p>' The Messiah" at Legh-street.</p>
        <p>t Selections from "The Messiah " were given at egh-street Primitive Church on Sunday evening to a °,vded congregation, forms having to be put down aisles to accommodate the people. The service was fened by sineing "All hail the power " to the well- )sanwn tune " Diadem," which was sung very heartily ali„ Miss A. Bridge, who was in very rood voice, tZlf? sweetly and with clear enunciation " Tnero were oOherds," "Come unto Him." and "1 know that my t., ,-",eemer liveth." Miss L. Plinston, who has a con- voice of pore quality, was heard to advantage in thou that tellest," " He shall feed His flock," and Att`le was despised." Mr. W. Booth in big first tarn, Pt at "Comfort ye " and " Every valley " was tl-,Y successful, and the way he realised the difficult 1123 was most praiseworthy. He also sane " Thy "and "Thou didst not leave. Mr. E. H. Pearce u,alt.." For behold " and " The people that walked " 'Y do the Nation " and "The trumpet shall tend." the florid passages of which be seemed to fairly in. The difflcult trumpet part in the latter item 'oil taken by Mr. J. Hodgkinson. The choir an9g the Ihl, aa'lng choruses on the whole in a creditable peeer :—" And the Glory," " Oh, Thor: that tellest, heiva,r unto us," "All we like sheep," "Lift up your 48," "Worthy is the Lamb " and " Hallelujah." rittet,Sonng organist Mr. A. W. Clatworthp accompanied the ae items in a highly successful Nray, and also played aPening Overture. Mr. A. Spencer conducted, and ttey, J. 13. Buglass presided.:</p>
