      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>Sermon on John Milton.</head>
        <p>Sermon on John Milton.</p>
        <p>1 John Milton " was the subject of a sermon by the ff,ev. R. E. Letheren at Golborne-street Baptist Church on Sunday evening. The preacher said he wished to take advantage of the tercentenary of Milton's birth to try to give them an irnprerFion of the man who by the splendour of his genius and the sublimity of his faith for ever redeemed Nonconformity from being a by-word and a reproach, by enshrining its inmost spirit in literature that must live as lenges the English language lasted. Mr. Letheren briefly sketched John Milton's career. He lived through those tremendous years when England was rent in twain and her green and pleasant land dyed with English blood that English hands must needs shed to preserve her liberty, when a perfidious king paid upon the scaffold the price of his treachery of a national trust. Milton was secretary to the Protector when Cromwell made England's name to be feared all over Europe. From his earliest manhood, even from his youth, he knew himself called by a supreme election to the production of a great poem that should he the glory of the English tongue and there were few thing in literary history more striking than this predetermination of Milton in his early manhood to the subject of the greatest work of his litter life. At last when the cause of Puritanism seemed lost, Belial and Molock were enthroned in Britain and the poet had given the best years of life, light and liberty to the service of failure, then in his blindness and adversity, gigantic came the vision- splendid, nnd out of the wreck of things seen and temporal, he laid bare the imperishable foundations of faith in that poem which had been called the epic of a fallen cause, bnt was rather the building of it anew in the hearts and consciences of men. Here then was the first message of Milton,the message of poet the and not merely of the poetry. It was the message of a life of lofty purpose, of thorough preparation by toil and pains for its fulfilment, of persistence and perseverance that kept him faithful to it even though for long years duty led him into other fields of service, of prayer that inspired and sustained him as a dedicated, spirit to do what work God called him to. Milton washy no means one of those who thought that the best way to the Father's House MS by the swine trough. If by Puritanism one understood a life kept unspotted from fleshly lusts' certainly Milton was a Puritan and wore from his youth the white flower of a blameless life, Being " Church outed " by prelates. Milton took up his pen and in five pamphlets pleaded for the freedom of the church from episcopal tyranny. " Never," says Dr. Garnett, "was diction so magnificent called forth by a theme so unpromising." Though he did not ally him- self with any particular Church, partly perhaps on account of his blindness and partly because none quite suited Lim, yet by his noble enunciation of the abiding principles of Nonconformity he was worthy of lemembrance as a great Nonconformist. Ile (Mr. Letheren) should have liked to illustrate tho blending of the Puritan and the poet in Milton. Some- times the Puritan almost startled us in the poetry, but he was not the less a poet. He loved music too, an inheritance perhaps from his father, and both father and eon were accomplished musicians. The puritan ' need not be a Philistine. The Free Churchmen of to-day were the children of the Puritans. They won for them the rich inheritance of freedom that they had entered into. It might be that their times needed a. certain narrowness to„secure the force their con- victions called for. Those convictions wore their sacred trust. Better far to have their narrowness than lose their convictions. They built in battle the broad basis of our freedom, they gave their lives for our liberties. We must prize what they purchased at such a price, we must improve the inheritance they won and hand on its gains ; we must use our liberties and not abuse them, we must be worthy sons of worthy sires. Puritanism meant purity of thought, word and deed. It meant the noblest culture of mind and soul ; It meant pleasures that helped, and not those that hindered the highest life ; it meant that we must do for those who came after us su3h services in our day as they did in their's. The preacher briefly referred to Milton as a patriot, observing that he saw EnglantiN servitu'e and fought for her freedom. " GoMs Englishmen "—it was Milton's golden phrase, it WAS his glory to have taught us its meaning, it must he our task to make it true.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>CONCLUSIVE WARRINGTON EYIDENCE.</head>
        <p>CONCLUSIVE WARRINGTON EYIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Conclusive evidence is evidence that puts an end to all doubt. Here is Warrington evidence that must put an end to any doubt you may have about the permanency of the cures made by Doan's Pills. Mr. E. Oakley, I, Bond-street, Sankey, Warrington, says :—" I can truthfully say that Doan 's pills have done me a lot of good, and I have recommended them to all my mates. " I used to be a sufferer from dropsy and kidney trouble, my limbs swelled very much. I was subiect to severe pains in the small of my back, hips, and loins. I consulted a doctor, and he advised me to go into an Infirmary. I was in there for a time, and I got a bit better. Altogether I was laid up for ten weeks, an I got no real benefit until I tried Doan's pills. "Doan's pills are a splendid medicine ; they have made me well again, and I shall take pleasure in speak- ing about them. (Signed) E. Oakley." After a lapse of more than eighteen months, one of our representatives had the pleasure of seeing Mr. Oakley, who said :—" I have not needed to use Doan's pills since they cured me so thorouFhly. It was dropsy that I had, my body swelled considerably, and I was , unable to pass the kidneY secretions properly, which were unnatural in colour, painful to pass, and contained ' sediment ; Doan's pills. however, caused them to pass naturally. I was trolled for kidney trouble at the I hospital, where I remained a month, twenty-one days in bed.</p>
        <p>"Doan's pills tire' indeed splended. Numbers have asked rue about my ease,and 11 always give the medicine high praise. I can't do otherwise, four boxes of the pills cured me." Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and niuepenco per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence.) Of all chemists and stores, or post free, direct from Poster-NlcClellan Co., 8, Wells•street, Oxford.street. London, W. Doan's are the pills that cured Mr. Oakley.</p>
        <p>Ragged Sokol Soup Kitchen. The above kitchen was opened for four days during this week. 'Ph Committee have given over 60 gall.,ns of soup and 2001 b. of bread to hundreds of poor people each day. Sincere thanks are tendered 'IrY the Committee to all those tradespeople who have so liberally helped during the week, and they appeal for more articles to enable them to contince the work (luring the next week. Threugh the generosity of Messrs. Atkinson and Jarman, who have given 3 tons of coal, the Committee have been enabled to brighten up GO poor homes. Meat has been (,:ven by Messrs. Cooper, G. and J. flowcoek, Peet, Nelson, Ltd., Hall, Tunstall, R. 0. Twist:di, Cross, Walsh, Owen and Myrekyme ; vegetables by Messrs. Lythgoe, Moulding, McDermott, Holcroft, holland, Sutton, Bate, Lindley, Birch, Lawrenson, McElroy and A Fiend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <p>THE LORDS and the LICENSING BILL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Protest Meeting at Warrington.</head>
        <p>Protest Meeting at Warrington.</p>
        <p>Speeches by Mr. Stephen Walsh, M.P. and others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>The Prime IliniAer's emphatic declaration that the question of the veto of the House of Lirds is to he the dominating isstu in politics was enthusiastically welcomed at the public meeting held in the Parr Hall on Saturday evening, under the auspices of the Warrington Citizens' Temperance League, to protest against the summary rejection of the Licensing Bill by the House of Lords. Considering the shlrt notice given of the meeting, the attendance was most encouraging, and showed that local temperance and social reformers are thoroughly in earnest on this far- reaching ipsue. Hr. James Dodds presided, and was supported on the platform by Mr. Stephen Walsh, M.P. (Ince Division), who was accompanied by his son Master Arthur Walsh, Councillor Max Muspratt, (Liverpool), Mr. Gilbert J. Tickle (Liverpool), Revs. A. H. Mitchell, E. Blanshard Keeling, F. Vaeghan, C. Harvey Cook, J. F. Matthews, and J. B. Bug lass ; Mrs. Simpson, Miss Rowlands, Mrs. nin widdie. Councillor Skelland, Messrs. C. E. Parker, J. Morton, D. Hodge, G. Weaver, Thomas Leech, J. Proffit, J. L. Fryer, W. Fryer, J. T. Rowlands, Dinwiddie, H. Wardman, E. Wynne-Jones, Alfred ,Tons, Twiss, A. Mounaeld, J. Griffiths, A. H. Dunn, Felix Maginn. Muir, Fellowes, Pharaoh, W, Ashton, George Leach (secretary) and others.</p>
        <p>in their history books, formed the governing part of the British nation. The King—he did not think even the most devoted Radical would mention his name without a feeling of respect and evan of reverence—(applause) —which grew from day to day. Could they say so much of the second part of the Constitution ? ("No.") It was said in the earlier days of Mr. Disraeli that the House of Lords was simply a</p>
        <p>HIGH COURT OF REGISTRY for the edicts of the Commons, and there was a great deal of truth in that statement. The House of Commons in those days was composed almost entirely of the same claSs as the House of Lords itself. There was inevitably a great deal of sympathy and agreement in the points of view that obtained in both Houses. Bat from the moment the great Reform Bill of 1832 came into operation, it was not only a necessary but an absolutely inevitable thing that the House of Commons should be composed of people who would come more and moro into clear touch,contact and agreemmt with the large mass of opinion which hitherto had found no representation. (Applause.) If the Second Chamber was to maintain its character unchanged, in defiance of all change in public °pillion, in dcflance of all growth of the imperative necessity of the public well-being, and in defiance of all the economic or moral oonsiderltious that arose,then surely the situati es was one that called for the most steadfast determination and courage on the part of every man and woman who wished for the well-being of the country. (Applause.) The Licensing Bill seemed to him from the very first moment he read it, to be one that contained the essential principles upon which clear thinking in this nation could be based. It was elways , held by every man and woman with whom he had come , into contact in his life that when a wrong decision , upon anything had been arrived it, it was best to appeal , from Philip drunk to Philip sober. (Laughter.) But ' the Lords said they would rather have the opinion of Philip druuk—(laughter and applause)—and not only that, but they intended to have no other opinion. Ile did not think that in a nation like this, composed, as he , believed from the bottom of his heart, of people who sometimes with apathy, often with indifference yet ! always truly in the4ong run desired the well-being of the commonalty, that was a state of mind on the part I of the House of Lords with which the nation would , long remain content. (Applause.) They gave in the ' House of Commons very long and detailed considera- tion to the measure. rt contained, admittedly, many very debatable and highly contentious provisions, but on the other hand he believed any Government, of I whatever denomination,—Liberal, Conservative, or if they conld imagine the possibility even of a Labour j Government coming into force—would be compelled : by the logic of circumstance to put a time limit to the monopoly value and, if they desired to handle the problem effectively, to give to the people the power of j local veto. (Applause.) Even Mr. Balfour admitted there must boa time lia.it, although he ratber disagreed about the method of assessing the value that was to be paid to erect that time limit. Every man of influence in the Conservative party agreed that it was a proper moral and social consideration that there should be a time limit. If that be the case, any Government mind- ful of that first proposition would have to proceed to the second and prevent further abuses being built up in defiance of the popular will and therefore local veto was necessary. He was glad to think that when It had been freely bandied about that there was no difference in parties, and that politics were a dirty thing that any self-respecting citizen ought not to touch, the time had come when a great political party was</p>
        <p>TEIE CHAIRAIAN AND THE LORDS. The CIIAIRMtN s.tid they were assembled on the morrow of an historic ,occasion. A few days ago the Licensing Bill was the main issue before the country, so far as the people knew, but to-day the issue was the veto of the House of Lords—(applause)—for the Prime Minister the previous day invited his great part, to agree that that question should be the dominant issue at the next election which was to come on very iShortly, thoue h they did not know exactly when. Of course, it would not do to let the other side know when it would suit them to have an election. (Laughter.) In War- rington their blood boiled when they beard of the way in which the Lords treated the Licensing Bill. He admitted that many of the members of the Second Chamber were splendid men—and leadiog men in the country—and they prattically all supported the Bill. But the great majority, the men unknown—nonentities, so to speak—decided at a meeting held in a private house, before the Bill 'came before them, that the measure should be killed. (" Shame.") If the Bill was dead, the cause remained. (Applause.). The cause was alke—(applanse)--and from what the Prime Minister , said on Friday they knew it would not be abandoned until a beneficent measure—if not the very measure which had been before the country and carried by the House of Lords, was placed upon the Statute book. (Applause.) They knew that the principles of the Bill were safe. (Applause.) Looking at the position of the men, whom he had termed nonentities, when Mr. Balfour brought in Bills as he did in 1902 and 1904, for which he had no mandate from the election of 1900, they rushed the measures through without any con- sideration simply because they were brought in by the leader of the party to which they were attached. But when Mr. Asqui h brought in Bills on the same subjects —Licensing and Education—these men threw them out without any kind of consideration whatever. That was a state of things which we could not allow to continue in this country—(applause)—and he was sure the people of England would not stand it. lie was told by a I leader of the other side in Warrington that when the election came on the Government would be beaten worse than the Conservatives were defeated in the last F fight. He did not believe that. On the contrary, be WILLING TO STAKE ITS ORTUNES was of opinion that with such moral forces behind upon a measure like the Licensing Bill—(applause)—to ; the Bill—all that was hest in the Church of lift men considerations of social progress and public ; England, the whole of the Nonconformists, the best I morality far above the mere paltry and temporary con- of the Catholics and last but not least the siderations of party success. (Applause.) Mr. Walsh ! unanimous support of the leaders of the Labour party I then alluded to a speech on the Bill by Mr. F. E. In Parliament—(applause)—they need have no fear. Smith, M.P., who was an extremely able man and could always (111 a gap very effectively. (Laughter.) " Like They were sure they would triumph—(applanse)—but Fuzzy Wuzzy in the Soudan, he's a first-class fighting they felt that the triumph ought not to be long- man.' (Laughter.) He (Mr. Walsh) thought the exhibi- delayed. They knew what the drink traffic caused— there.weuld perhaps he thousands of livms lost because I Lion Mr. Smith gave—and he said it in a perfectly 'the Bill had not already assed—and they hoped the friendly manner—on the very last night of the debate p I was hardly worthy of his undoubted ability.He said—and Prime Minister would decide as early as 'possible to he chortle.d. in his glee—that he wasattending a funeral, take the country's decision, once for all, upon the veto and that he had come to bury Cassar, nat to praise power of the House of Lords. Let them get rid of him," and he added that he had never attended a funeral that and then carry the Licensing Bill, as he was sure they easily would. (Applause.) The House of Lords