he belieeed was destined . laymen were in favour of such a measure. (Applause.) to become a memorable meeting, the Prime Minister ; comas MEI" CJNTENIPLATE DISASTER enlarged upon matters which were not within the pur- uptn the Bill? If they could, be should simply view of the resolntieh cad he (Ate Weise) supposed I despair of the thought, of the democracy of this that as that was a citizens' meeting called almost for country, He should despair of the possibility of clear the express purpose of protesting against the action of I thinking. But surely they could not rest content and the House of Lords on one particular matter, it might ellotv such a feeling of pessimism to enter their minds. he held to be somewhat outside the scope of the There was really for most great measures of progress special object of the meeting if one were to enlaree I placed over the portals of the second Chamber upon other affairs. lie was in complete sympathy with that inscription which was said by Dante to the protest, although he hardly liked nsiue that expres- Ihe placed over the doorway of the inferno : sion " sympathy " ; it sounded se lugubrious. They I " All hope abandon ye who enter here." did not not want sympathy at the present moment, brit I They had been able to do a bit and they were not courage, backbone, determination and clear-sighted- I going to abandon hope. (Applause.) They believed I ness upon the issues on which they intended to fight. ; means could and would be found within the Constitu- -1 (Applause.) Clear-sightedness ought to come first ; tion to force the measure upon the Statute book. 'the other finalities would follow later. The Lorde met I (Applause.) Bat a very great deal rested with audiences at a particular !mese which he thought had received such as that. Probably there was no more fickle a crest deal more advertiseioent than it was entited to I multitude, search the world round, than the En; lish —(langhter)—end decided upon a certain course of ; electorate. They talked about" tilonian in her hours of ; action. Did anybody believe that they decided upon ' ease is uncertain, coy and liar I to please." that action ? (Cries of" No.") Not at all. Any such (Laughter.) Was there any, woman so fickle, uncertain, thought as that was beneath contempt. They never thought for one moment at Lansdowne House what their action was to be. They had RECEIVED THEIR ORDERS al rearly--(applaime) —from the lender of the Opposition in the House of Commons, and it, would have been great deal more than their place was worth—(langhter) —much as that value might be appraised—had they 'dared to go against the declared yet secret will of Mr. A rthur Balfour. If that was to he the action of a Chamber which for many hundreds of years had been an integral part of the British Constitution, then all free, fair and indep trident exercise of the rights of citizen- ship.vres practically redneed to a mockery. (Applause.) The Hine, the Lords and the Commons, they were told</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>4/d, bottle make.. 4 – 2 gallons of dellcioushomemade Lemonade. Equaity good as A HOT DRINK for Supper. N :lif ..1111A, r . ~_ Try. pc. di e</head>
        <p>A 4/d, bottle make.. 4 - 2 gallons of dellcioushomemade Lemonade. Equaity good as A HOT DRINK for Supper. N :lif ..1111A, r . ~_ Try. pc. di e</p>
        <p>coy and touchy and in many cases so treacherous as the English workingman electorate ? (Laughter.) Therefore they protested against the action of the House of Lords because of the women of the nation who had never been given an effective chance of a say in the matter. (Applause.) They had no definite and direct means of influencing the legislative Chambers, but upon them was cast the burden of the broken-up homes:lt NM they who knew the heart-break of the child; it was they who waited the drunkard's curse and blow; it was they who had to pat up with wages cut iu two or three ; upon them all the bitterness, terror and torment of the conditions under which we were , struggling were really impased, and they ought to have ' the greatest say in a mattEr like this. (Applause.) Then they must also think of the children. Continuing, Mr. Walsh said that if they were to enter into a high Constitutional struggle, the r ink and file ought to be quite assured of the earnestness of their leaders. Disraeli in a farnone speech accusing Sir Robert Peel 1 of desertion of his party upon the question of the Corn I LAWS referred to a Turkish admiral who had had orders to bombard the enemy's ports, but, to the great , surprise of the Sultan hs surrendered without firing a shot. His excuse was that he thought of putting the ; orders into execution bet when he saw the gigantic forces arrayed against him he concluded that prudence I was the better part of valour and surrendered in order to save the lives of the Sultan's subjects. That was an act of the grossest treachery, apart altogether from 'cowardice. They wanted to know whether the political leaders of the people were in earnest. lie was in , Parliament two years ago and heard the pronouncement ' of the late Sir Henry Campbell-Rmnerman, whore they always remembered with veneration and respect,that the will of the Commons must prevail, and suggesting a method. He (fr. Walsh) thought at the time that it resembled a famous duel, which never took place, between the son of the great Chatham and Sir Richard , Strachan who were always threatening each other but ' managed to keep at a tolerably safe distance. " Great Glatham with his sabre drawn Stands waiting for Sir Richard Strachan ; Sir Richard, longing to be at him, Sits waiting for the Earl of Chatham." (Laughter.) He did not want to see re-enacted once more that • eighteenth century duel that never took place. The , conduct of the second Ceamber, non-elected, in no ' sense representative except of their own private interests, dependent entirely for whatever degree of argument that could be put forth upon the argument of heredity—which was probably the worst argument of all, because the best part of it was beneath the ground—(laughter)—this conduct raised a Constitu- tional question of vast importance to every ruan,woman and child in the coentry, and there was no party, how- ever Conservative, however willing to let things stand as they were, but must recognise that the situatiod was fraught with infinite</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>DANGER TO TUE CONSTITUTION</head>
        <p>DANGER TO TUE CONSTITUTION</p>
        <p>land to the country and could not be allr;%—ved to remain las it was. They were engaged in a peaceful crusade at present but there was no telling how soon it might I develop into something quite different. There were , too many vast social questions awaiting if not settle- ment, at least decent and efficient treatment, to allow the question of the relations of the two Chambers to ,be very much long.r delayed. He was a citizen who loved from the bottom of his heart the history of his country and he admired the orderly and peaceful way in which the development of the nation had gone on, but they could not shut their eyes to the terrible wretchedness which prevailed. Whatever the causes might be they had a right seriously to consider them with a view to their removal, and the people would not be content to have this implacable barrier placed in their path when they were aiming for the improvement in social and moral conditions which was their undoubted right. (Applause.) He did not want there to enter into any mock heroics or stage comicalities.fle wanted the fight to be a real Constitutional fight—a definite and straight- forward fight deali.ig with the great Constitutioeal problems with which the nation was confronted. He hoped no promptings of feebleness or despair would enter their minds. The great moral forces, all those elements which in the past had made for progress, were with them. The ! i louse of Lords was engaged n the old impossible task of grs.Partington with the Atlantic ocean.—(Laughter.)—The old lady was defeated and he was certain that would be the fate of the House of Lords. (Loud applause.) HOUSE OF LORDS AS TORY INIACEHNERY.</p>
        <p>Councillor MAX MUSYRATT, of Liverpool, said that before the Liberal Government was returned to power, the interests of the people of this country and of the I Empire had for nearly twenty years been in charge of Conservative Ministers. Daring that time great social, industrial and financial problems were corning up. Were they dealt with or even faced ? He said that for twenty years the progress of this nation was stayed and stopped because the Conservative Governtnent was in power and did not chose to take action in these great I problems. The House of Lords made no attempt to stimulate the House of Commons to hasten the pace a little, to see what was coming if these social reforms were neglected. The House of Commons under the I Conservative Government passed many Acts of various I kinds, many of them bad Acts. Did the House of Lords I attempt to remedy or to improve those Acts ? No. They were simply a registering assembly of the actions ! lof Mr. Balfour's Cabinet. (Applause.) The speaker particularly instanced Mr. Balfour's Education and Licensing Bills and in regard to the latter remarked that it came as near endowing the brewer as possible, and multiplied tho dielculties of dealing with this terribly strong vested interest. The Lords, however, instead of dealing with it in a statesmanlike manner and looking ahead, let it go through. Then they came to the General Election of 1900, and he claimed that in that election it was not any particular subject that received more attention than another, but it was fought upon the broad necessity of changing the Government in order that the great Questions of social reform might be dealt with. That was the mandate of 1900, and that was the mandate the Gcivernment had attempted to act up,to. (Applause.) The whole principle and theory of our party Govern- ment was that whatever Government the electors returned should nominally for seven years, at any rate for five or six years, take control of the fortunes of the country. Underlying our English constitution it was firmly established that when the people seat a Govern- ment to power, particularly with such a tremendous majority as the present Government, that Government was to have a chance to show how it could deal with social reform. (Applause.) The House of Lords had undoubtedly become simply</p>
        <p>AN INSTRUMENT OF THF: PARTY MACHINE ' of the Government which was turned out with ignominy in 190 G. Particularly in the presence of Mr. Walsh, he was only too glad to point out that even where they had done right, they had acted as part of the political machine. When any subject that was definitely labour legislation was sent up, they pasSed it because they dare not in a frontal attacl,—(applause)—stand against the great forces of organized Labour. (Applause). But the other part of the machine was working, and throughout the length and breadth of this country through the Conservative organizations there bad been a steady flaw of calumny and slander against the. Liberal Government for what was called Socialistic legislation. If Labour legislation should not have been passed by the Liberals because it was Socialistic, what about the House of Lords, the bulwarks of the Constitution, letting that legislation go through ? (Applause.) The Licensing Bill dealt with all the horrors and terrors of drunkenness in a fair and states- manlike manner and with the utmost regard to the interests involved. When the subject came to the House of Lords,—lhese benevolent patricians—was not that the kind of Bill that should have appealed to them as the guardians of the people's interests? Should not they have received it with open arms and have corrected here and there but, broadly speaking, they could indeed have claimed to have represented the people at large and the hest opinion of the nation instead of our tickle and hurried opinion, hy giving the Bill a second reading and, if necessary, amending it in committee. In allowing themselves to be influenced entirely by the brewer and the bond holder and not by the great moral influences of the nation, they had shed the last shred of decency which enveloped the [louse of Lords before. Thee stood now politically naked and unashamed before the people at large. They stood as the friends of corruption and of corrupt influences in our politics. They bad deliberately and in spite of the warnings of all their hest men, independent of party,put C ASH. BEFORE CONSCIENCE.</p>
        <p>They had pot money before morals end the brewery share- holder h.fore the betterment of the people. (Applause.) That was a hig indictment, holt alas, it was true and the people of this country had to realize that they were et the parting of the ways.qe was a member of the Church of England end he was also a progressive. In years past he bad sornetims,s not thought that the Church of linOand was steliciently progressive or took a suffi- ciently sympathetic view of progressive causes. To-day he was proud of his Church—!annlausel—and proud of her stand. The old taunt of "Beer and Bible " had, thank God, for ever been stilled. (Applause.) The Bishops had taken a dilicult, brave and noble stand and all that was best in the Church, although many of the clergy and laity were slow at falling into line at once, was hound to follow those bishops and the Church would become purer, creator and nobler. (Applause.) They had a big and difficult fight before them but the moral forces were on their side, and though Englishmen were often thoughtless and apathetic and sometimes even influenced by the corrupt vested interests, in the long run when the moral forces had been on the side of any issue, that issue had always gone from strength to strength until it reached the attainment of its end. They had a long light before them, a fight second in , importance and perhaps in violence to none in the past. ' it was the great fight of PRTVII,P,GE VPRSITS PROGRESS, of peers versus people. They had not to enter the fight with any light heart or with any undue excitement or violence of expression. They had to approach it as patriotic men, imbued firmly with the conviction that it was necessary for the progress of the country that the difficulties to progress should be removed end not increased and that a Government when it was sent with a mandate by the electors of the country, should for five or six years have a decent chance of showing what it could do. (Applause.) In that fight they would draw nll moral end social reformers to their side. Possibly many of the old divisions of party might he obliterated. Ile did not care whether it was a Liberal, Labour or Progressive party or even a reformed Ccoservative patty that carried out what they</p>
        <p>had to do, but it had got to be done in the interests of their wives and children and of the great Empire of ; which they were proud. Social reform had to be dealt t with in no niggardly spirit and the first step in social reform was to deal with the vast interest which had done so much to corrupt and keep the people down. (Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>THE CORRUPTION OF' THE LIQUOR TRAITIC. Mr. G. J. TICKLE, of Liverpool, said he spoke as a member of the rank and file of the temperance party. While a great mandate was given to the Government to carry forward great measures of reform, especially was the mandate given to pass a measure to help the people againt " the trade." Mr. George Wyndham in a speech the other day said no nation could afford to have its legislators and officials grinding away for months at futile tasks and certainly this nation could not afford it at a mo.nent which was historic and fraught with consequences to generations to come. No doubt Mr. Wyndham thought the proper course to take when legislation was hindered and the men seeking to carry it forward had to grind away without results to their labour, was for the Government to clear out of the way and let those who could carry the work forward take their place. He (the speaker) was pleased that the great leader of the Liberal party did not take that view of the qeestion. (Applause.) He was going to take his own time and just at the moment when he felt he could carry these great reforms forward he would appeal to the country. (Applause.) lie was not going to establish a precedent that the House of Lords should dictate to the House of Commons and to the country,the moment at which a Government should resign. Much as they deplored the amendment carried by Lord Lansdowne and his friends, deep as their indignation was, what impressed him (the speaker) more deeply and aroused more intense anger in his mind was the manner in which the amendment was carried. The amendment was one of the most cl-SICAL AND lIEARTLESS things he ever read. The Lords declared that their arms were open to receive a measure of temperance reform shown by experience to be necessary. If ever a measure was sent from the House of Commons to the House of Lords backed by the experience of experts, it was this Licensing Bill. It was discussed as seriously as any measure had been discussed in the memory of living man and as men understood the measure better it gained an increasing and more earnest support. They were suspicious of the kind of temperance the Lords would, support and receive. The kind of temperance they wanted was that which would not stop drinking, because they were shareholders in the breweries. (Applause.) They said they rejected the measure because it would cause grave inconvenience to many of his Majesty's subjects. It would interfere with the dividend of these men. (Applause,) When the Bill was thrown out it was received with cruel laughter. However bad the action was, the way in which it was done made it ten-fold worse. (Applause.) But there were noble men engaged in the fight in the House of Lords, and the speeches of Lord Crewe, the Arch- bishop of Canterbury and especially that noble oration of Lord Rosebery, should not be passed over without careful attention from the thinking people of this country. The speaker quoted Lord Itosebery's words about the danger of corrupt influence by the liquor traffic in pubhc life. This corruption, continued Mr. Ticicle, beginning in municipal life and extending to Parliamentary life, had reached the House of Lords and it was because of</p>
        <p>THE COREUPEION OF THE LIQUOR TRArFIC that the Bill was thrown out. The appeal was made to all that was sordid, base and cru,4 in their nature. As temperance men and women, they must think of the source from which the incomes of some of these men arose. He was sick of the talk about monopoly value and time limit as it affected pounds, shillings and pence. It was all that, hut it was much deeper. It was not a question of dollars and cent,,,but of the happiness of homes, the parity of our women, the safety of our little children, and it seemed an abuse of reason and of time to be talking about dollars when they ought to be talking about morals. (Applause.) They were fighting for truth, purity and righteousness, and they must have the enthusiasm of good men behind them. They thanked the Primo Minister for his declara- tion. (Applause.) They thanked him that there was going to be no hesitation in the matter. As a humble man of the temperance party,'ae (gr. Tickle) responded to that appeal. He had always consecrated his vote to the temperance movement and had voted for Conserva- tives, Unionists and Liberals, for any men whom he thought would help to free us from the liquor traffic. Ha promised Mr. Asq&amp;th that from that night the only question he would ask a candidate for his suffrage in the days that were left to him, or at least until the work was done, would be " tVill you help to deliver us from the bondage of the Lords ? " (Applause.) They were not going to let the Bill drop. They were going on with it as ihthey bad not been defeated, and they were going to win. (Applause). The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>REV. J. F. MATTEIEWS' STRONG VIEW.</head>
        <p>REV. J. F. MATTEIEWS' STRONG VIEW.</p>
        <p>_ The Rev. J. F. MATrltEws had a most enthusiastic reception on ri.rinr, to propose a vote of thanks to the speakers. He said 's people were making it much harder for him to leave Warrington than he really thought they would. (Laughter.) With regard to the veto of the House of Lords, it was becoming a serious question. It was not merely a Constitutional question, hut a ques- tion involving the widest ethical and moral issues, and he thought with all his heart that as the days went by they must not lose sight of this one thinff—ttat come what might, the House of Lords had got to go. (Loud applause.) He did not care whether the means were Constitutional or unconstitutional. (Applause.) They could not at this time of day allow legislation of national and even of international importance, which had to do with the best interests of the boys and girls to say nothing of the interests of the women—and he hoped they would soon have the vote--(applause)—to be tampered with by such an irresponsible body of men as those who were found in the gilded chamber (Applause.) The Free Church parsons and a great mans" of the Anglican parsons like his friend Mr. Mitchell would support Mr. Walsh and all like him who were prepared to take up the cudgels in defence of the women and children as against the brewing lords and the peers at large. Whatever Government was in power, the drying force tested with the people. Their leaders would not go one inch further than they could send them. They [mist strike once, for ever, and for victory. 1 (Loud appause.) THE DOMINANT ISSUE.</p>
        <p>_ The Rev. J. B. BeGLass in seconding said he had never been a House of Lords man and never would be. It was time that House was dispensed with in some way or that sori3 drastic reform should be brought about. The question was : Were the people of this country or the House of Lords to rule ? He was sure that audience had indicated by the repeated applause that they meant that the people should govern. There would never be political peace in this country until they had waged a successful war with the Lords, lie protested against the House of Lords as at present constituted, because it was incompatible with the democracy of the country, it lacked moral passion, it was a party organization,and it cared taore about property than about humanity. They had had an inspiring lead from Mr. Asqiiith. l'hey had to proclaim war against the House of Lords—that was the dominant issue. Let all the forces of progress concentrate upon that point. They would have a good fight but they bail no need to be timid, for he believed that the fort of the liquor tut&amp; could be taken. (A plain use.) The resolution was heartily carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>LORDS AND TilE TRADE.</head>
        <p>THE LORDS AND TilE TRADE.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said a question had boon sent up to him to the effect that \fr. Hobert Pierpoint was reported es havipg said that n-hen the House of Lords rejected the Licensing Bill, not one man in 3 hundred in that Chamber was connected with the drink trade. Was the statement true? If not, would the Chairman take steps to let them know how many of the Lords were connected with the trade ? _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN sail be believed about 16) out of the GOO mena')ers of the house of Lords, exclusive of debenture holders, were interested in the trade. When Pierpoint addressed another meeting they would he able to ask him bow he arrived at his figures. Continsing Mr. Dodds said the late John 13right said force was no remedy. Honcst John Morley, now Lord Morley, said with regard to the House of Lords that force was the oily remedy. lie (Mr. Dodds) was told that the only remedy that could be thought of was that Mr. Asquith should create 300 or 400 new peers. If Mr. Asquith intended to du that, he would probably come down to Warrington for a few. (Laughter.) "Rut" concluded Mr. Dodds "I have made up my mind that if I am to be made a peer, it must be a Duke or noth!ng. (Laughter.) I am rather afraid it will be nothing." (Laughter.</p>
      </div>
