        <p>The House of Lords on THE COAL Thursday took up the</p>
        <p>SCUTTLE CRY. Mines Eight Hours Bill at the Committee stage, and proceeded so to amend it as to make it unrecog- nisable. We have never been very eager about this measure, but we cannot regard without astonishment the sort of argument that is employed against it. The Coal Consumers' League, which has been particularly active and virulent in all recent bye-elections, has apparently convinced itself that if the measure is passed into law it will be the cause of a considerable addition to the price of coals. This view of the case is that which is put 'forward by such unsparing opponents of the Bill as Lord New Torr, who moved its rejection. He puts the probable increase of price at a shilling a ton—a sum which, we may inform him, comes within easy measurable distance of the actual present cost of hewing the coal. How in the world is the restriction of the working day to eight hours to double, almost, the cost of hewing coal? The history of the progress made by the coal miner—a quite recent history, by the way—entirely discourages the idea that a further restriction in the hours of working will add anything appreciable to the cost of coal. There are men still living who have worked in the coal pit for twice as many hours as it is now customary to work. They never saw daylight except on Sundays, or days on which the pits were not working. They went down before the sun arose, and did not come up again until after it had set. Their wages were low. There was no Truck Act to protect them against employers who made a second profit out of them by selling them their food :and tbeir clothes. There was immensely less expenditure on applicances for securing the safety of life, and on officiate whose first duty is to assure them- selves that the pits are fit to work in. Hours have been greatly shortened since those days. But if the price of coals has gone up, it has been on the ground of causes quite distinct from the length of the miner's working day. The case of Northumberland and Durham may be cited in proof that the n umber of working hours has no a ctual controlling effect on coal prices. As was frequently pointed out, the miners in those couutries do no work eight hours a day, but usually only five. If the price of coal went up in consequence of the shortening of hours, North- umberland and Durham would simply have ruined themselves as a coal-producing district before this time. As a matter of fact, they are as prosperous as they ever were, and:they produce and sell coal in quantities that would cot have been dreamed of half a century since. It might possibly have been better and wiser if the miners had ros olved on shortening their hours without resort to Parlia- ment, as was done by the pitmen of the North. If they had been content to bring pressure to bear, through their unions, we should not now be , talking about the probable e fleet of a shortening of hours or the price of coal. The hours would have been shortened long ago. The result of a long working day has too often been a shortening of the week's work. The pit has worked two or three day's a week, instead of five and a half, and the miner has in consequence—in some districts more than others—been kept constantly on the verge of starvation. The Peers who oppose the Eight Hours Bill are possibly sincere in believing that the price of coal would be raised if the measure were passed into law. We do not feel so sure about the Coal Consumers' League. That body came in existence in circurrstances little likely to breed confidence in the earnestness of its conviction, and it agita- tion has been carried on in a style which inspires doubt. The coal consumer need be under no apprehension that such a Bill as the present will raise the price of coal, otherwise than temporarily, even if it does so much as that. The effort of shortening hours in all industries up to the present time has been a greater productiveness.</p>
      </div>
