      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RAILWAY CHRISMIAS ARRANGENIENTS</head>
        <p>RAILWAY CHRISMIAS ARRANGENIENTS</p>
        <p>The Great Central Raiiway Company announce special excursions during the Christmas and New Year Holidays to Londan, Nottingham, Leicester,Rugby, Stratford-on Avon, Sheffield, Lincoln, Hull, Newcastle-on-Tyne Filey, Scarboro' and other North East Coast resorts - Full particulars appear in our advertising columns. CURRANTS AND BANANAS HOME COOKERY COMPETI- TION.—List of Prizes for the County of Lancashire :- ISt prize, 15955-301, 1920-7072, 101E3 ; 2nd prize 18142-10903-2918 ; 3rd prize, 2105.</p>
        <p>In March last, when the SIR GILBERT AND Licensing Bill was intro- THE BELvora. duced, Sir Gilbert Greeuall, Bart., took the opportunity of resigning the Mastership of the Belvoir Hounds. Matters of this kind are usually arranged privately and without undue fuss. But Sir Gilbert resigned as loudly and as publicly as he could. He wanted to make it clear—mostly to those not concerned—tnat if "this revolutionary bill " became law hs would be unable to continue the expense of leading the Belvoir Hunt. We pitied the poor millionaire at the time, and we pitied him as publicly as we could. The Bill is now dead so far as it concerns the present session of Parliament, and Sir Gilbert has talon the opportunity of at once emerging from his retirement. He announces—or at least our neutral contemporary announces—" that he has reconsidered his decision and will continue in command." The Belvoir Hunt will doubtless be relieved. We are sure Sir Gilbert will. But it must be quite obvious to thinking people that if Sir Gilbert really feared financial loss from the passing of the Licensing Bill,he has come out into the open too soon. The Government's Budget schemes are expected to touch the pocket of the Trade much more heavily than the Licensing Bill would have done, so that if the Knight of Walton had good reasons for resigning the mastership of the Belvoir,he should have the same strong inducements to continue practising so estimable a virtue as economy. aimeM It is quite conceivable that MAKING HISTORY Saturday night's protest IN WARRINGTON, meeting in Warrington may eventually take the form of one of those milestones which measure and mark the pathway'of history. The Premier only sounded , the battle-cry on Friday evening, when he pro- nounced the veto of the House of Lords to be 1" the dominating issue in politics," and expressed the national resentment at the callous treatment of the Licensing Bill and other Liberal measures, by the hereditary peers. The following evening the fight had commenced in Warrington. The good old town of Warrington is the first, so far as we know, to raise the flag of defiance against the House of Lords, and the fact should not be forgotten. There may, and probably will, come a time when we shall be proud to recollect that Warrington led the van in the historic struggle which culminated in the abolition of the Lords' veto and the establishment of the principle that the democracy shall prevail in its desire and right to legislate for the national well-being. Probably the august assembly at Westminster will not tremble very fearsomely either at the words of the Premier on Friday, or the action of the Warrington Citizens on Saturday, but they would do well to remember that the vastest streams have small beginnings. The spirit of the people—who were not assembled in Parr Hall under any political auspices—was deep and sincere. They realised that Government by the hangman, and the hangman in the pay of one party, was a brutal and accomplished fact. And what Warrington thinks to-day, England may think to-morrow!</p>
        <p>-- We are glad to note that a "THE NEW sane and responsible journal Mrismartism." like the "Westminster Gazette " has turned its attention to " the new militarism " and criticises strongly the policy which consists in promoting scepticism about the defensive qualities of the Navy and raising alarms about the alleged danger of invasion. "Thus, in the last few days, we have seen Lord Middleton, who may be supposed to have the military conscience of the Unionist Party in his keeping, associating himself with Lord Roberts in challenging our competence to resist an invading force of 100,000 men, with our exist- ing army, and in attacking the scheme of military re-organisation which has been undertaken by Mr. Haldane. If this attack succeeds, the alterna- tive policy of universal military service, leading as we believe, by sure stages, to the conscript system of Continental countries, will be upon us.' The italics are ours. It has always been one of our strongest objections to any system of com- pulsory military training, whether advocated on the lines of the National Service League or not, that it must inevitably result in some degree of Conscription. The word is as hateful, almost, to the National Service League enthusiast as it is to the voluntary service man, but for a different reason. The latter objects to anything of the kind with all force of his nature- and his inherit- Gnce of freedom, but the former protests against the use of the word because it savours too much of the naked truth. We have never heard any argument to rebut the contention that the militant militarists will not be satisfied with an apology for compulsory training, once they get the thin end of the wedge well inserted. Much always wanted more, and it is only reasonable to Ilassume that the advanced members of the 1 National Service League would, once they had been dazzled by the glitter of three months under arms, rapidly convince themselves that the benefit would be correspondingly great if the period were extended to three years. The " Westminster" analyses Lord Midleton's figures, and points out that we have at the present moment 517,000 men in various forms of military service at home and ridicules the talk of an invasion by 100,000 foreigners. It concludes : "The Army we have . . . . is adequate and suitable to the defensive problem that con- fronts us, and there is not the slightest necessity to alarm the public about our defenceless condition. Let our young men come forward, as they are doing, and take their place in the Territorial Army, and the problem will be solved. Those who scoff at the Territorials, decry the Navy, and call for universal service must be thinking not of this problem but of a new policy as well as s new Army—a policy which, we believe, represents a mistaken ideal for this country."</p>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, December 19, 1908.</p>
        <p>His mighty champion, strong above compare, Whose drink was only from the liquid brook ! To this almost topical allusion may be called the attention of those who favour the view that beer is "the food of the people." * * * To hear sturdy Stephen Walsh giving the exotic Mr. F. E. Smith a lesson in the classics was a treat to those who attended the protest meeting on Saturday evening. Ur. Smith was not happy in his choice of a metaphor in the House of Comm ms when on the death of the Licensing Bill, he said " he had come to bury Cabear, not to praise him." Mr. Walsh was quite right in pointing out that like the lamented Bill, great Caesar was the victim of a premeditated and cold- blooded murder. As one of the murderers, Ur. F. E. Smith had a valid reason in adding that " he never attended a funeral with greater glee in his life." But what a state of mind in which to attend funerals generally ! * * * Considering the hasty arrangements, there was a very representative gathering at the meeting on Saturday evening to protest against the House of Lords' treatment of the Licensing Bill. The speaking was of a high order, and notable for calm, dispassionate reasoning in an hour of considerable national excitement. Yet the conviction was strongly affirmed that there must be a fight to the finish on the con- stitutional deadlock brought about by the House of Lords. To most people in the hall, Mr. Stephen Walsh, M.P., was a stranger, but he quickly made himself a favourite. We have rarely heard a more earnest, eloquent and inspiring speech than the one he delivered. For a self-made, self-taught man, it was a wonderful effort. Mr. Walsh is certainly an ornament of the party to which he -belongs, and of the constituency he represents.* * Is London a favourable place for literary labour? The Bookman has been making biquiries on the subject, and p iblishes a number of replies from well-known men and women writers. Mr. H. G. Wells thinks a man should write just as well in London as anywhere else. Mr. Pett Ridge can write anywhere, but " for perfect comfort give me my own rooms in the heart of London, with traffic and move- ment near." Mr. E.F. Benson finds the quiet of town an excellent environment for work. Mr. H. A. Vachell works in the country because be likes to live in the country, but he would work as well" in London or Timbuctoo "if fate constrained him to dwell in either of those cities. Mr. W. E. Norris says he would find it difficult to write in any large city, because be has always been accustomed to the quiet of the country.</p>
        <p>* With respect to Tariff Revision in the United States the American has taken the trouble to conduct a little referendum amongst manufacturers, who have been asked whether they want the tariff on their products iacreased or reduced. According to the New York correspondent of the Times : The answers indicate, as might be expected, that these gentlemen favour a reduction of duty on the " raw material "of their particular industry, but are not particularly anxious for any reduction of duty on the manufactured article. For example, the boot and shoe manufacturers unanimously favour a repeal of the duty on hides and a sub- stantial reduction of the duty on leather, but would like to retain the present tariff on boots and shoes. Was it not Cobden who said that every man was a Free Trader except in his own trade ? Here the boot and shoe manufacturer wants cheap hides and cheap leather, though from one point of view, both these are manufactured articles, just as from another they are with equal certainty raw material.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <p>For Children's tanking Cough at night, Wood's Great Peppsitmint Cure. Is. li. 2s. 9cl. Advt.</p>
