      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>It is reported that there may be a settlement of the pending petition against the return of Councillor P. Peacock for Orford Ward on the ground of alleged bribery and corruption. We hope the rumour is true, for actions of this kind are usually unsavoury and of doubtful benefit to any of the parties concerned, what- ever the issue.</p>
        <p>_ * * * Referring to the treatment meted out to certain Suffragettes at the Albert Hall, one of their number states : "We will not suffer these things in silence." We have sometimes wondered whether the notoriously quiet methods of the Suffragettes are really advisable, and we are interested to hear that there is now to be a change of policy.—" Punch." * * * Father Whittle perpetrated an amusing slip of the tongue in acknowledging the handsome jubilee pre- sentation made to him at the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening. Referring to an early curacy, he described Liverpool as the place " where I began my great career." The audience laughed heartily and Father Whittle smilingly admitted that he had put his foot into it," but his many friends will freely acknowledge that he has laboured unselfishly and with distinction for the Church to which _he is so devotedly attached.</p>
        <p>* * The " Guardian" Year Book comes from an office whence emanates much that is described as " neutral in all matters political and religious." Seeing, how- ever, that this annual does not bear the usual hall- mark of neutrality, our surprise was somewhat lessened when we read, under the chronology of local events, the following : " Oct. 19. Citizens' Temperance League: Demonstration against the Licensing Bill." This is a somewhat bold construction to place on the objects of the meeting in question and hardly coincides with the terms of the resolution ' carried unanimously at the time.</p>
        <p>In " Samson Agonistes," which was given before the members of the British Academy on Tuesday evening, it will be recognised that Milton, with regard to the Licensing Bill, would have probably been in its favour, for Samson's strength is attributed to his abstention from intoxicants, as the following quota- tion will show : 0 madness to think use of strongest wines, And strongest drinks, our chief support of health, When God, with these forbidden, made choice to rear</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Christmas Choral Services To-morrow. • 'P Christmas choral services will be held at Wycliffe Congregational Church and Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church to-morrow (Sunday) evening when selections from "The Messiah " will be rendered. Full particulars are advertised. Musical Soziety's Concert. We would remind our readers that the second subscription concert of the season in connection with the Warrington Musical Society will be held in the Parr Hall on Boxing Day. A clpital miscellaneous programme will be submitted, including Elgar's Banner of St. George."</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.—U.NDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST RBOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Ragged School Soup K 1 chen. On Thursday evening sixty unemployed from Sheffield tramped into Warrington and, as the Workhouse accommodation is at present severely taxed the Ragged School authorities were asked by the police if they could find lodgings for the men. Accordingly they were accommodated for the night at the Labour Home, and were provided with a supper of soup and a breakfast of bread and cheese. They left yesterday morning for St. Helens._</p>
        <p>_ PUBLIC T3F,BEFIT.—UNDER TETE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Whist Drive at the Liberal Club. _ - -</p>
        <p>A whist drive arranged by the members of the Liberal Club took place at the Club on Thursday evening and proved a complete success. The prize winners were :—Ladies : 1, Mrs. Lindley ; 2, Mrs. Eastwood ; booby prize, Miss Whitfield • Gentlemen : 1, Mr. J. Howes, junior; 2, Mr. A. ilynekyme ; booby prize, Mir. Mynekyme, junior. The prizes were pre- sented to the succesful players by Mrs. Locker and Councillor Wilkinson. The games took place in the reading room and refreshments were served in the billiard room upstairs, which had been very tastefully decorated. The catering was in the hands of Mr. J. J. Randle, manager of the club. The arrangements for the function were admirably carried out by a sub- committee, consisting of Messrs. J. J. Pritchard, x. 1.7. Bibby, J. Wilkinson and T. Locker. For Influenza and Bronchial Coughs,Woods' Great L Peppermint Cure. 15. lid., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>CHRISTAAS TREE AND FANCY FAIR.</p>
        <p>The annual Christmas tree and fancy fair promoted successfully for many years past by Mr. and Airs Martindale,assisted by the teachers, to aid the funds of St. John's Sunday 'school, was held in ihe Mission tiall, Legh-street, Earlestown, on Saturdiy last. The central feature of the decorations was the Christmas tree, which was in charge of several ladies in turn. At the opening ceremony the Rev. S. Devenning presided, and explained the object in view. The Sunday School Fund was about £2O in debt, and he was sure all present would empty their pockets and give the treasurer a good balance, for he was worthy of it. The Vicar was not present owing to his indisposition. lie had to introduce to them Mrs. Collinge, wife of the head- master of the Manor School. Sue had been identified with parochial work at St. Peter's Newton, and would declare the sale open.—Mrs. Collinge briefly declared the sale open and wished the promoters every success. Mr. Pierpoint moved a vote oh thanks to Mr. and Mrs. Collinge for their presence and assistance.—Captain liaslain (C.A.) seconded.—Mr. Collinge replied for his wife, and expressed the appreciation of the work being done in the Sunday schools, lie said the day schools as well as the Sunday school was the place to train character. They had heard a lot about the Education Bill, but he hoped that Parliament would never say that religion must not be taught in the day schools.— The sale of work realized the sum of £lO.</p>
        <p>LORD NEWrON AND TOE AIINRRS' EIGLIT</p>
        <p>DARING TREFT AT Sr. HELENS. ' A daring act of shopbreaking was carried out at St. Helens during Ssturday night, when the main window in Mr. Grundy's pawnshop, in Clauroh-street, Was smashed, and a large number of watches and articles of jewellery were stolen, the thief or thieves getting clear away. . Oaly fifteen minutes before the robbery was discovered a policeman had passed the spot, Willt everything was quite in order. lialf a brick was thrown inside the shop, showing that this had been the menus employed to break the window. It is only a week: ago since Mr. Grundy's other shop, in Townsead-street, was treated in the same fashion, aad A number of watches and other articles stolen.</p>
        <p>IVESLEVAN PRESIDENTS visur. On Sunday the Rev. J. Scott Lidgett, the president of the IVeslapin Conference, paid a visit to Widnes. Mr. Lidgett is an alderman of the London County Council, and the mayor and corporation of Widnes recognised the visitor's civic service by officially attending at the Victoria-road Wesleyan Church. la the course of his sermon Mr. Lidgett said that civio life was of religion, just as religion was of civic life. Though some people sighed for the Old English village life it was the struggle of the city that brought out the character in man and made for the fatherhood of God and the brotherhood of man. This is the first time a Wesleyan president has visited Widnes.</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>Page. 2. Father Whittle's Jubilee. Warrington Teachers' Association. Photographic and Science Notes. Rev. H. llunnico's Rowdy Meeting. 3. The Lords and the Licensing Bill. Big Protest Meeting. Our L.,cal Churches. G. Stockton Heath News. Savings Bank Meeting. Cross-Country Running. Hippodrome and Theatre Items. 7. Politics of the Week. Notes on Games and Ltbletics. Football. A _Star Pantomime.</p>
        <p>We have little doubt that the pantomime of " Jack and the Beanstalk " which opens at the Shakespeare Theatre, Liver').3ol, on Boxing Day (two perform- ances), will soon earn the reputation of being the best pantomime in Lancashire. Shakespeare shows are always br.lliantly staged, and while this may be relied up.m. as usual, the great draw will certainly be Ada Reeve, one of the most fascinating musical comedy and pantomime artistes of the day. The com- pany is exceptionally strong in other respects, and records bookings are sure t) be the order in Fraser- street, London-road.</p>
        <p>New Appointment for Mr. George Carruthers. Mr. George Carruthers, 8.A., second master of the Warrington Grammar School, has this week beers appointed by the Manchester Education Committee mathematical master in the Municipal Secondary School, Whitworth-street, Manchester, where about 500 boys receive instruction. Mr. Carruthers, whca was a Hasting's Exhibitioner at Queen's College, Oxford, has been at Warrington nearly twelve years and we are pleased to know that his new appointment will not necessitate his leaving Latchford. LINSELW COUPOIIND 'for Coughs and Colds. Of proven efficacy. Did. &amp; ls. lid. Of Chemists only.—Advt. Gollxirne-street Christmas Tree ana Social. A Christmts tree and social were held in the Golborne-street Baptist schoolroom on Wednesday afternoon. There was a large attendance and every- thing passed off successfully. An excellent pro- gramme was submitted at the social, including solos by Miss Jackson and Miss Hamer. Mr. Muir gave a humorous Scotch reading which created much amuse- ment. The following were responsible for arrange- ments :—Miss Williams, Miss Bate, Miss Longshaw, Mr. Hunt, Mr. and Miss Muir, Miss Sharplea, and Miss Mitchell. A satisfactory sum was realized fcr the church funds. War,lngton Law Clerks Society. it is evident th.it the Law Clerks of Warrington do not intend always to Lake their pleasures seriously, and the smoking concert on Wednesday evening last at the Winmarleigh Cafe was a decided success.— His Worship the Mayor (Alderman Forshaw) as President of the Society, occupied the chair, and was accorded a very hearty reception from the members and their friends.— The artistes, Messrs. J. W. Bibby, F. Fieldbouse, G. H. Smith, R. Lyon, C. Orlando, E. A. Sanders, lt. Wood and Noden were all that could be desired ; and Mr. W. J. Churchill, from the Hippodrome, paid a flying visit and kept his audience in roars of laugh 'er for a quarter of an hour.</p>
