      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>HOURS BILL. MOVES irs REJECIION.</head>
        <p>HOURS BILL. MOVES irs REJECIION.</p>
        <p>The !diners' Eight Hours Bill came up for its second reading in the House of Lords on Tuesday.—Lord Newton, in moving the rejection of the measure, said it was extremely unpopular in the country. He might be doing the Government a good turn if he procured the rejection of the Bill, and he might be doing a service to the people on the other side who desired to see the House of Lords embroiled with the Labour party ; and he might also be assisting in the ungrateful task of filling up the cup, which up to now had been pursued with so little success. But !airier, more regard for principles than for tactics,he proposed to go to a division. He denied that the reduction of hours would lead to the miners working harder and producing more coal. The Departmental Committee had found that there would he a reduction in out-put and increase in price, and had added that in no other country were the manu- facturing authorities so completely dependent on the coal produced within its borders. Lie would be studi- ously moderate in his figures, and estimate that the increase in the price would be only Is. per ton. That would mean to the shipping trade an extra expense of £1,000,000 a year, to the cotton trade £200,0u0, to gas £1,000,010, and to the iron and steel trade £d,500,0u0. rhe present Government was elected to look after the interests of the consumer, and the way this Free Trade Government proposed to safeguard the interest of the consumer was by placing an enormous tax on coal, a raw product, a thing which even the most ardent Tariff Reformer had never even suggested. (Laughter.) liners were highly organised and were led by capable men, who had obtained extreme proficiency, who could get the last ounce out of a Government, a member of Parliament, or a Parliamentary candidate. He had repreaented,a mining constituency, and the miners' leaders had come to him and told him that they would turn him out if he would not do NI hat they wanted in regard to that Bill. Even In those days, however, he had had sufficient sense to realise that if anyone wanted to vote against him he would do so whether he voted for an Eight Hours Bill or not, and though he was naturally of a meek disposi- tion he had turned his back on them and had never regretted it. (Laughter.) To him this proposal that there should be special protection for miners was almost equal to giving a special income tax in favour of dukes. (Laughter.) No solid argument had been adduced in favour of the Bill, and it was only put forward on the ground of political expediency. The house had auflicient sense to distinguish between what was for the general interest of the community and what was not, and it was because the Bill, which was unsupported by either serious facts or serious arguments, proposed to penalise the whole community in order to give a special advan- tage to one class that he asked the house to reject it. The division resulted as follows For the second reading Against 4l Government majority</p>
        <p>BANKRUPT APPLIES FOR. lIIS DISCHARGE. At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before His Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., Francis Joseph Holt, of Earlestown, applied for his discharge from bankruptcy, Mr. E. A. IVnite, solicitor, Warrington appeared for the bankrupt.—The Official Receiver (Hr. J. Grant Gibson) read a report slating that the pantie examination was concluded on December 7th, 1906. Aocording to the bankrupt's statement of affairs the liabilities to rank for dividend were estimated at £575 us. 7d. but proofs actually admitted at this date (gill 18s. Id.) and the probable claims not yet proved .£2l 75. 41) amounted to £536 ss. 53. The assets were estimated to produce £262 3s. id. but only realised .£l3l 3s. 3d., the difference being accounted for maimly by the fact that the bankrupt over-estimated the value of his property mortgaged to the fully secured creditors. The balance available for costs and for distribution among unsecured creditors was £124 13s. 63. instead of £258 12s. Id. us estimated by the bankrupt. A first and final dividend of 3s. 30. in the £ was paid on proofs for £514 lfis. Id. Insolvency was due to a single circum- stance. Bankrupt, who was 52 years of age had been brought up as a litter. He commenced business at the end of 1896 as a cycle manufacturer at a workshop adjoining 100, Wargrave-road, Earlestown, with £3Ol capital. He appeared to have been able to make a living out of the business, but be was not apparently able to put any money by. Three years before the receiving order was made he commissioned a Mr. Mitchell to build for him a house and workshop. Difficulties between the parties ended in litergation in which Mr. Mitchell claimed £650. The matter was referred by the High Court to an arbitrator who awarded Mr. Mitchell £ll5, dismissed the bankrupt's counter-claim and ordered him to pay costs. Of the £575 scheduled as liabilities, £425 represented the judgment and costs. There could be no doubt that the bankrupt would have been able to pay without difficulty his other liabilities which amounted to only £l5O, out of his business and personal assets. Mitchell's claim showed an increasing tendency to go up from the time it was first preferred. The matter passed through the hands of five separate firms or individuals and the claim was increased from a comparatively small sum until it had attained the formidable dimensions mentioned above. Bankrupt kept no books of account except the day book, no balance sheet was prepared and stock was never taken. Although the business was a small one and elaborate books would not be required, the Official Receiver submitted that; the bankrupt had not duly complied with the requirements of the Bank- ruptcy Act.—Mr. White submitted that his Honour would be justified in finding that the fact that the bankrupt was not able to pay 10s. in the £ did not arise from a cause for which he could be held justly responsible. Having regard to the limited nature of the business, he contended that the day book which was kept well posted up, was sufficient to show the position of the concern. His Honour said he did not feel justified in going out of the ordinary course. He made an order for discharge, but suspended it for two years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS ALDERMAN AS CHINESE CON SUL-GENERAL. A great amount of interest was taken on Saturday in the announcement that Alderman John Forster, J.P., of St. Helens, has received the appointment of Consul- General for China in England. Mr. Forster has been a member of the St. Hetens Town Council since 1884. Ile is an ex-Mayor, and on one occasion contested the borough in the Liberal interest. Ile was chairman for a great many years of the Health Committee, but last month abandoned that position to take up the chairmanship of the premier committee —the Parliamentary Committee—in succession to the late Sir Christopher Gamble. For some years past Mr. Forster has 6een interested in large trading Con- cerns in South Africa and China. He has made several prolonged visits to these countries, aact made the acquaintance of many influential persons in Pekin and the 'Various treaty ports of China. The appointment of chief Chinese Consul for Great Britain is a new and most Important one, and Mr. Forster will play an Important part in the commercial development in China where English influences and capital ars being used to develop and found many important industries. The office of the Consul-General will be at 88, Fenchurch. street, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
