      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>WESLEYAN QUARTERLY MEETING.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN QUARTERLY MEETING.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Warrington Wesleyan Methodist Circuit was held at Bald-street Church on Thursday. Prior to the meeting a company of about seventy sat down to tea and a vote of thanks was accorded to the Bold-street friends for their hospitality on the proposition of the Rev. E. BLASSIIARD KEELING (superintendent minister). Mr. F. A. HARRIS responded. The business meeting was presided over by the Rev. B. B. Keeling, who was supported by the Revs. T. Miller T. Gladding, F. W. Kernick and J. Gunnell. Thelinancial returns were considered highly satisfac- tory. During the year the ministers' homes had been re-decorated and largely re-furnished, and a sum of £1.30 was raised for the purpose. The debt on the circuit had been reduced from £79 to .C 22. The membership returns were also satisfactory. There was an interesting discussion on the question of prayer meetings, the Rev. T. MILLER introducing it. There was a general feeling that a powerful attempt should be made to revive prayer meetings where they had been allowed to lapse. Mr. IL IL Heidrun' and Dr. James Bennett were unanimously re-elected circuit stewards for the ensuing year. Mr. WILLIAM SILCOCK, after many years service, regretted that he was obliged to resign the post of secretary to the quarterly meeting. A vote of thanks was passed to him for his untiring work and Mr. F. A. Harris was unanimously appointed his successor. The report of the Circuit Guild Council was presented and recorded the fact that that organization united with the Citizens' League and distributed handbills in about one-third of the borough advertising the demon- stration in favour of the Licensing Bill. The annual Guild Rally had been held and proved a pronounced success. Mr. W. E. Plinston was elected Circuit Guild Secretary. It was reported that a site for a new chapel in the Orford district had been secured and a large amount of the cost of the land had been raised. Foreign missions and temperance work were dis- cussed and it was considered that things were in a very hopeful condition in regard to Methodist work generally in the Warrington Circuit. The next meeting will he held at Bewsey-road on Thursday, March 18th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>PENI(ETH.</head>
        <p>PENI(ETH.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN . G THLD.—A general meeting of the Wesleyan Guild was held on Tuesday night in the Wesleyan Sunday School. The Rev. F. W. Kernick occupied the chair, and in his opening remarks said the reason for the delay of the general meeting had been brought about by the school not being finished. He remarked that the schools were now adapted for there meeting and hoped that the guild would make up the time lost. After reports had been given by the secretaries of the various committees, which were of a satisfactory nature, the business of electing committees and officers for the coming session were gone through. The Rev. F. W. Remick was elected, president, Mr. E. A. Burphy, general secretary, and Mr. Thipnts Bell, treasurer.</p>
        <p>Hockey. Latchford first eleven beat Waterloo last week on the latter's ground by 3-2. This was a capital win, as Waterloo are very strong. The work of the visiting forwards was very good, Scott keeping both wings going all the time by judicious passes which were well utilized. Spensley and Banks on the left were exceptionally tricky and Ryder the county back had a lively time. Parker and Trovis on the other wing were also solid. The Latchford defence was quite up to dick, Parker scored two goals and Banks one.</p>
        <p>Manchester Y.M.C.A. received their usual beating at Latchford at the hands of the Second Eleven by 7—h In spite of the big score, the game was a good one. Booth for Latchford was brilliant on the right wing, scoring a fine goal and putting across many telling centres. Jones in the centre distributed the play nicely. Minards scored four times and Jones and Btarkey once each.</p>
        <p>To-day the first eleven against Eccles at home is as follows :—W. Unswortb, goal ; C. Plinston, T. Dixon barks ; F. Newell, J. B. Wilkinson, J. Albinson, halves D. Travis, H. Parker, H. Scott, E. Banks, W. Spensley, forwards. Referee: Mr. W. B. Cobb. Bully-off, 2.45.</p>
        <p>The Second Elevens meet at Eccles. Latchford's team si—J. Appleton, goAl ; J. Wilkinson, W. H. Pickles, ecks ; L Kenworthy, A. Knight, F. Tole, halves ; H. Booth, A. Starkey, H. Jones, W. H. Minards, C. Hackney, forwards. Referee : Mr. H. Warburton. Train from Bank Quay (high-level) at 1.15, to Eccles.</p>
        <p>Take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure, for Coughs and Golds; it never fails. Is. lad., 2s. Dd.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
