        <p>Mr. T. Ridgway, distric'c coroner, held an inquiry at the Hall Cafe on Thursday, touching the death of Ann ,McC.e, of Kimberley Drive, Stockton Heath, whose ; body was recovered from the Bridgewater Canal, the ' previous morning.—Deceased's husband Richard alcCue, a bricksetter, employed by Mr. Themes Williams, builder, said his wife was forty years of age. She had been under the doctor since last July, suffering from "I suffered from bilious headaches more or less chronic indigestion and nervous prostration, and had from boyhood," Says Mr. William Clark, of GO, Russel suffered terribly at times. last Tuesday night she was I Street, St. Neots, Hunts, " and lam now over 50. A suffering severely, and the next morning when witness very severe pain used to settle over my left eye, and worknhgertobew oul? eas:,ileedkhsillno rttol stayv lie saw her ;La st glodi this was accompanied by sickness and an indescribable at 7 minutes to 9 on Wednesday Morning. She was headache. Faintness, giddiness, loss of strength and then in bed. While in agony she had told him she appetite followed, and I was invariably compelled to would do something to hers elf but be dissuaded her keep to my bed for several days. Attacks of this kind He had no suspicion that she would take her life. On would occur perhaps every fortnight —sometimes some occasions she would be bright and cheerful, and on oftener. While the attack lasted I could not sight bear others she would be in great agony. of the food. Eating was quite out of the question,as I Mary McCue, daughter of the deceased, said that she , was sent out by deceased for a shilling's worth of was unable to keep anything down. whisky. Deceased was upstairs when. Witness went out "My eyes became aull and blurred and I felt but was downstairs when she returned, witness gave ; thoroughly exhausted. I had a doctor several times, deceased the whisky, and on missing her a little later but received no benefit from his treatment, nor from she th