fs and Strays Society. I to finding the body near Lamb Brook Bridge,and sending BAND OF HerE CoscEnT.—On Monday evening a for a constah'e. concert in connection with the Hill Cliffe and Stockton Police Constable Clarke, stationed at Stockton Heath, Heath United Band of Hope was held in the Wesleyan stated that be resorted to artificial respiration but Schoolroom. Mrs. James Dodds, president of the without avail. He searched the body but found nothing Warrington Band of Hope Union, occupied the chair, with the exception of a wedding ring. The jury returned and expressed her delight in visiting the Stockton a verdict of "Found drowned,"therenot being snlficient Heath lined of Hope. Beferring to the rejection of evidence to show how deceased got into the water. The the Licensing Bill by the House of Lords, she said coroner expressed his sympathy with the hoshand and that she believed that this action would stimulate the ' loss. various temperance workers to greater effort to. secure i family in their control over the drink traffic. A capital programme was gone through in a very creditable manner, and consisted of recitations by Edward Phillips, G. Nicholls and Maud Kirkland. Miss Minnie Mounfield sane very effectively "My daddy." " The very worst girl' in school " was sung by Miss Elsie Mountield. Miss Edith Hamblett gave a capital rendering of the song "Come, deck the hall with holly." Miss May Isherwood and Miss Lily Powell delighted the audience with the duet " Hark ! the goat belts ringing," their voices blenoing very sweetly. An action song "The Wakenftiffen Band by six boys created much amuse- ment. whilst the humorous sung " Ida, ha, ha," by Mast.er lq, Nurse, was encored. he principal event of the evening ,Alis the performance of the cantata " Santa Clans by a special choir trained by Mr. J. H. Dutton. This seasonatie item created great interest amongst the little folk, and when Father Chtistruas appeared and proceeded to fill the stockings with the usual toys and presents the excitement was intense. The choir sang the various pieces in good style, and the following boys and girls took the principal characters in a most praiseworthy manner. Reggie ,Grounds, Ernest Whittield, Ernest Clarke, Dolly Cain' Elsie Whitfield, Mabel Cain, Elsie Mountield, Minnie Mona- field and Emma Harablett and Mr. G. Gould. The closing item was a dialogue entitled " Peter Sqiall's Downfall, which was well given, the various characters being represented by Misses M. Isherwood, L. and F. Powell, M. Earle, M. Kirkland, and Messrs. J.Whitfield, %V. Earle and A. 'l'. Hughes. Mr. U. A. Whitfield officiated as accompanist and Miss Lena Powell gave a well-rendered pianoforte solo. STocKToN HEATH EVENING CLASSF.S.—At a meeting of the Cheshire County Education Committee on Monday, it was recorded on the minutes of the Runcorn Rural Area Sub-committee that a letter was received from the managers of the Stockton Heath evening classes, asking that the cookery, wood-carving and dressmaking classes be allowed to continue, notwith- standing that the number of pupils attending such class* s ware 10,6, and 6 respectively. It was resolved (a) That it be an instruction to the Stockton Heath evening classes local committee that in view of the county evening schools regulations, the cookery, wood- carving, and dressmaking classes must be at once dis- continued, and they be informed tht further expenses in connection with such classes will not he acknow- ledged ; (b) that in view of the fact that the existing students in such classes have shown their desire for instruction, they be informed that, should they be able to arrange for attendance at similar classes in IVarring- ton, they will he allowed tram fares to and from War- riogton on production of authoritative proof of satis- factory attendances ; (c) that the lees of the students concerned be returned.—This action was confirmed.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH PAROCHIAL COMMITTEE. A meeting of the above committee was held in the flail Cafe on Tuesday evening. Mr. G. Pilling presided and there were also present :—Messrs. J. 11. Clark, J. Whitley, T. R. Westbrook, H. J, Westbrook, F. Pe.uson J. M.:Dermid, J. W. Bouchicr, J. Mynekyrue, W. Mc Intyre, T. Baxter,G. J. Brown,F. Warburton, J. Hallow, J. Swinton (surveyor), and G. F. Ashton (clerk).</p>
        <p>LIWITING (IF GRAPPLNIIALL ROAD. The clerk read a letter received from the Warrington Gas Department, with regard to the cost of lighting the Lumb-brook end WO rappeuhall-road.They were prepared to extend the main to Lurubbrook, and erect two lamps at an extra charge of Is. per lamp per annum. They were however unable to get any cousumers on that length of main. Tic..NDEILS FOR 'NIGHT SOIL COLLECTION. The clerk reported that he had received two tenders for the collection of night soil and the emptying of ash- tubs for the next twelve months. The tender of Mr. Miller the present contractor, was for £3OO end that Of Mr. Spence!), for the same amount. There was, how- ever, a difference for extras. After much discussion the telider of Mr. Spencely as accepted.</p>
