        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>THE BEST COE IN ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>"The wife," the one woman in the world that cannot be spared, is, at this season, on the look-out for little hints and suggestions that shall help her through the winter's housekeeping. Host opportune, then, is the appearance at this time, of a quite new e.iition of the fa n')n little currant cookery book which his been for some years past so invaluable an all to the housewife.</p>
        <p>Your Grocer is luny supp'ied with thii new edition, and will gladly give you a free copy if you ask him. Here is a sAnaple recipe : -- TEA SCONES.</p>
        <p>llalf-a-poond of self-raising fhor, laz castor sugar, 1 egz, 2oz. butter, 2az. cut rat, aad sofficient milk to make it soft clongla METIOD. —Rub the butter into the floor, add the sugar and currants, ao,l mix with the egg (well beaten) and milk. Knead well, and roll out rather thickly. Stamp out into rounds with a pa,ite-cutter, and place on to a greased baking tin, brush over with egg, and bake in a fairly hot oven for about 15 minutes.</p>
