        <p>THE WESLEYANS AT MANCFIESTNII.—The Wesleyans journeyed to Manchester, List Saturday, to fulfil their annual fixture with the Y.NI.C.A. Harriers. Buxton Harriers also sent a strong team and a close race was anticipated. Commencing at th.: Red Lion Inn at Blackley, trail was laid over 6 miles of very hilly country, the recent rains malting the going very heavy. Neary 80 runners toed the mark and at the start, Gme, of Man- chester took the lead,closely followed by Massey of the Wesleyans. A race, brimful of interest, ended in a fine win for the Warringtonians, who packed splendidly and will take some beating on this form. Placing as follows : Wgton. Y.NI.C.A. Buxton. Massey 4 Farrand 1 Askew.— 3 Thornley... 7 Core Berton 8 Hampton ... 10 Holmes ... 13 Gill Willett ll Slack l5 Walker ... 30</p>
        <p>30 41 75 A Tea was provided at the Y.NI.C.A. for Ivinnors and friends, an enjoyable day ending at 10,30.</p>
        <p>(By PEDO). Warriugton A.C. visited Crewe Harriers from whose headquarters trail was laid by Bandies, Marshall and Beckett of Crewe and Jeffries Of Werrington, over a fairly stiff course of about nine miles. The pack followed at a Bretz,- pace, Bermion, Holbrook, Jepson, Watruough, Ford and Antrobus being in the van. After about a mile and a half had been covered Holbrook lost considerable ground owing to encountering a loose fence rail •, at the end of five miles the order was Bennion, Watmougb, Ford, Antrobus, George Jepson and Holbrook, and about 103 yards off Shaw and Millington were the only others in sight. Bennion was pressing hard to get away but without avail until within a mile and a half from the finish, at which point Holbrook and Jepson got off the trail, and lost nearly 30 Seconds marling. Bennion maintained his position to the finish and won by about 60 yards from Antrobus who was followed at similar intervals by Ford and Watmough ; Millington was nearly 100 yards off and after a desperate finish be gained the fifth position by inches only from J. George who beat Shaw by about 40 yards ; Jepson and Holbrook Came next nearly a minute in front of Howyer and Bradley. Result : Crewe : Bennion 1, Millington 5, Shaw 7, Bowyer 10, Bradley 11—total 31 points. Warrington : Antrobus 2. Ford 3, Watmough 4, George 6, Jepson B—total 23. Warrington thus achieved a meritorious performance in winning by eleven points as Crewe are seldom beaten on their home course. The visitors were congratulated arid entertained by Crewe, a very enjoyable evening being spent. A. Millington, W. Litherland and R. Whittaker were the most prominent of the IVarrington novices who have a race to-day from the Bridge Inn.</p>
        <p>EIIPTYINC. 07 PRIVIES. Mr. Brown raised the matter of emptying privies, and stated it was the gereral impression or the people of :the people of Stockton Heath, that notice had to be Igiven in order for their privies to be emptied. There fought to be a definite rule, so that they could be ' i.attended to without having to give notice. There ehould be a time limit. 1 Mr. 11. J. Westbrook objected to time limits. Laughter.) i , Mr. Clarke said he knew of one privy not being ' emptied for three years. 1 s It was decided that privies should be emptied as often :as occasion required but in no instance blaould the period be longer than three months. t hagiiiallt Father. . • SEWERAGE. I At the Borough Police Court on Monday, John ' Mr. Swinton (surveyor) reported that a committee 'Mm ques- tion of the Walton sewer and came to the conclusion i 'Moms Connor, last from the Union, was charged of the aural District Council bad considered the with neglecting to maintain his family, thereby leaving : *flat it would considerably relieve the ktibitefield-road them chargeable to the Warrington Union. Mr. . and Walton-road sewers it the Welton sewer were Shearer prosecuted on behalf of the Guardians, and extended for another 16 yards 50 as to join the Stockton stated that defendant's wife and two cliildreu were 'Beath sewer. I admitted to the Workhouse on the 25th of last • it was decided to offer Lower Walton £lO towards month. Defendant, who was a puddler by trade, left 'the costs of the work. A SUGGESTION'. i his employment through no cause whatever, and A letter WES read from Mr. it. Shacklady, suggesting refused to work, and so Iris fernily had nothing to live upon and were in consequence admitted to the Work- Out as New Pike-street was to be planted with Hawthorn bushes instead of common privets the name house. Ile(llr.Shearer)had been instructed to press these 'ehould be altered to Hawthorn Grove. 'cases. —The defendant stated he had no regular job . This was agreed