      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday December 19, 1908</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>YEAR'S THRIFT. Continued Progress of the Warrington Savings Bank.</head>
        <p>A YEAR'S THRIFT. Continued Progress of the Warrington Savings Bank.</p>
        <p>Another progressive year was under review at the annual meeting of trustees and managers of the War- rington Savings Bank held at the bank premises, Buttermarket-street, on Tuesday morning. Mr. William Long (chairman of the bank) presided and there were also present :—Mr. John Geddes (vice-chairman), the Revs. 11. V. Pigot, W. Ifracecamp, G. Vale Omen, John Yonge, C. Harvey Cook, and J. F. Matthews ; Alderman Smethurst and Messrs. Charles White, Henry Roberts, W. Richardson, C. Jackson Holmes, Edwin Woods, Arthur Down, J. S. HULL. R. Rowson, J. Longland, W. Tinsley, E. Wallington, and William A. Proud (actuary.) _ TIIE REPORT.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF THE DAY SCHOOL BANKS AND</p>
        <p>HOME SAFES.</p>
        <p>The ACTVARY read the 91st annual report of the committee, which stated that in the ordinary depart- reent 811 accounts had been opened or renewed, and 573 closed, an increase of 238. In the investment department there was an increase of 69, together 307, and making a grand total of 15,772 open accounts. The funds of the bank were as follows :—Ordinary depart- ment assets exclusive of freehold bank premises, &amp;c., valued at £1,300, £353,272 2s. 7d. ; Government stock, £17,001 Os. 6d.; investment department., £47,940 Is. 3d.; liabilities, ordinary department, £319,968 14s. 7d. ; Government stock, £17,001 Os. 6d. ; investment depart- ment, £17,537 9s. 6d. ; surplus, ordinary department, £6,303 Be. : investment department, £lO2 lls. 93. The surpine assets over liabilities, inclusive of the value of Bank premises, amounted to £8,005 19s. 93.—an increase of £lO5 Is. Bd. for the year. The summary of the deposits and payments was as follows :—Deposits ordinary department, number 19,793, amount £76,085 19s. Si. ; investment department, number 448, amount £13,370 7s. 33. ; interest credited, ordinary department, £3,319 12s. 23.; investment department, £1,258 Is. Si.; payments, ordinary department, 11,0 te, amount, £87,353 7s. 33. ; Government stock, £930 ; investment department, number 102, amount £3,923 ; showing a net increase of £6,823 13s. for the I year. The balance due to depositors now stood at £114,510 Is. 73. The branches continued to make good progress. At Earlestown there had been an increase in each department, and the total funds now exceeded £lO,OOO. At Bank Quay there was a large number of transactions, but the total amount deposited had not increased proportionately. That was accounted for by the fact that some of the depositors preferred to have their accounts kept at the head office, and only used the branch for making deposits, which of course, were credited to the head office. The home safes had now been in use about eighteen months and provided the means of accumulating small sums which would not otherwise be saved. Dining the year £5BO 7s. id. had been received through them chiefly in capper and small silver coins. During the year a number of banks had been inaugurated in some of the day schools, and the results had been most gratifying. The committee took that opportunity of thinking all those who had asso- ciated with the movement, and desired to place on record their high appreciation of the cervices they had rendered in the cause of thrift. The committee had decided to give greater facilities to depositors. With the advent of the new year the head office would be open duly from ten to three, except on Thursday and Saturday when it would close as at present at one o'clock. On Wednesday the bank would be open until 4 p.m., and on Saturday evening from 6to 8 p.m. It was hoped that with extended hours the business would be largely increased. The committee regretted the loss the institution haul sustained by the death of Mr. R. W. Francomb, a trustee and manager for a number of years.</p>
        <p>THE CEIAIR.MAN'S SPEECIL The CrtratamaN moved :—" That the statement of accounts prepared by the Actuary, and examined by the Auditor, to approved, and that copies be transmitted to the National Debt Commissioners and the Committee of Inspection, and that the duplicate statements be publicly affixed in the bank." Mr. Long said the bank had done a great amount of good in the town, and they who were in charge of it had been anxious to extend its facilities to keep pace with the development of the borough. It was on that ground they proposed to open the bank according to the usual bank hours, except in one or two instances which were named in the report of the committee. The home safes which were an American invention, were well protected, and were sent out on hire, and it was hoped that when the husband came in from has work he would put the odd coppers he hid in his packet into one of them instead of spending them in a less prAtable manaer. (Hear, hear.) It was most encouraging that £330 had been accumulated in the safes during the past year. The branches were doing well, and the penny banks at the schools they all set great store by, as they would be a great help in the future. Mr. Moore Murray, the director of education, had taken a personal interest in the matter. It had not been simply an official duty to him, but he had worked con smote* lie hoped that the habits of thrift which. were being formed among the children, would . .</p>
        <p>stick to them thrOugh life. (Hear, hear.) The work of I Walker's Brewery Band Concert. the Institution was very often best shown by the fact 1 that it had gone on quietly and smoothly, end it was A pleasant musical evening was provided for the hoped that its usefulness would be increased with all employs of Messrs. Peter Walker and Son, Ltd., on these additional facilities that were being afforded.' Monday when the annual concert in connection with (Heart hear). the Brewery Band was held in the Parr Hall. There was Mr. OEDDES seconded, add the motion was carried, a very large and appreciative audience and the enter- THANRING THE COMMITTEE. , tainment proved most interesting throughout. The Alderman SmETtionsx moved :—" That the report of : Brewery Band continues to make good progress and the Committee of Management be approved, and that ! its interpretations of the " Tannhauser March " this meeting acknowledge the valuable service rendered ! (Wagner), ce "Tancredi" overture (Ross/ ta), "The Pirates to the Institution by the gentlemen acting on the com- I Penzance" (Sullivan) and " Punchinello" (Rimmer) mittee daring the past year, and tenders its best thanks of ' I deserving of the generous applause accorded. to them." He said he regarded it as a great honour to . were - • propose such a resolution, because there was no doubt ; Mr. W. Winstanley ably conducted. The vocalists that the members of the committee had rendered vain- were the Misses Dudley and Hargreaves and Messrs. able services to the Institution. He was pleased to : Cottrell and Coleman who acquitted themselves axlmir- find that the Bank had made such progress during the , ably tiith individually and collectively. The opening past year, and it was his earnest hope that that progress Q would be maintained in the years to come. The home 1 1 uartette " Over the bills " (Geibel) was beautifully , •• safes were serving a most useful purpose, and with the• sung and later that fascinating number from the Chairman, he trusted that they would find their way 1 "Gondoliers"—"A Regular Royal Queen"--vt as given into the homes of the people, as they were an excellent with rare zest and delighted . the audience. Miss means of inducing tho people to save. (Hear, bear.) I Myra Dudley, a soprano with a flexible voice of The Bev. C. flostvEv Corm seconded. He said he had E good range, sang " Cartnena " (Lane Wilson) and read the report quietly at borne, and there were items i o Good-bye" (Tot/) with taste and erpression,though in it which he considered very satisfactory. Consider-y the effect of the latter item was spoiled bs the lusty ing the condition of trade during the past twelve A months for the Bank to have accumulated the sum it , and cries of an infant in thegallery. had, cr had, showed how careful the Committee of Management , However, the artiste took it all good-humouredly and were. He liked to know what area of population the ! was rewarded with a cordial encore. Miss Annie bank covered. Taking the population of Warrington at , Hargreaves gave sympathetic renderings of " When about 70,000, he found that the bank had at least one ' a little while has flown "(Lloyd) and " A pretty little depositor in every four of that ponulation. That seemed flower " (Sullivan) and, had time pormitted, the to him to redound to the glory of the Institution. The . audience would have liked another a ,ng from her. whole report struck him as being the outcome of very i Mr. William Coleman achieved considerable success careful and economical management and wise counsels in carrying on the work of the Institution. (Hear, hear.) by the spirited style in which. he song " Peg away " The resolution WAS carried. I (Bevan) and " The Cooper's song " (Suppe) both of VIE CHAIRMAN AND \TECH-CHAIR MAN. I which went with a capital swing. Mr. J. W. Cottrell's Mr. CnAnt.as Wrirre moved" that Nit.. Witihm Long pure toned voice was heard to distinct advantage in he re-elected chairman and Mr. John