        <p>' only once been absent and that was owing to a dense fog, which delayed the train. The Rev. Joipt YONGE seconded, and said they all shared in the gratification in having the services of two such exceedingly able gentlemen. All connected , with the institution knew how much Mr. Long and Mr. Ceddes did for the comfortable working of the business by their urbanity of temper and thorough business capacity. The resolution having been heartily agreed to, Mr. LONG, in reply, sad be thought the time was coming when the trustees and managers must look out. 4r younger men to take the positions which had just been filled up. Tho Bank was growing and they hoped it would continue to grow ; also that they would all try to do something to stem the folly and ignorance of some working men in sponding their money in such foolish ways. (Hear, hear.) It. was a fearful thing to see what an immense amount of money was wasted when it ought to be applied to useful purposes. Mr. GEDDES said that as long as he was able to do anything for the Bank he should be willing to give his services. OTHER APPOIFTTNIENCS. Mr. C. JACKSON iloi.mF.s moved :—That the follow- ing managers be elected to join the trustees on the Committee of Management for the ensoins year, viz. : The tier. John Yonge, and Messrs. T. B. Carter, J. S. Hill, Richard Fairclough and Lewis ILltowson. Mr. J. LONGLAND seconded, and the motion was carried. The Rev. W. BR tCECAMP moved :—That the fol. lowing gentlemen be added to the list of managers,viz.: —Councillor Fulton-Smith, Mr. J. E. Richardson, Mr. W. Mason, the Rev. 0. W. Dickinson, Dr. Joseph, and Rev. R. FL Lathe ren.</p>
        <p>J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-streit,Wa;rington.—Advt. Concert by Bold-street Choir. The members of Bold-street Wesleyan Church choir proved their capabilities as eutertatuers on Monday evening when they gave a highly enjoyable concert in the schoolroom. The Rev. T. Miller presided over a good attendance. It will be remembered that the choir succeeded in winning the cup in the church and chapel choir contest at the Warrington Musical 'Festival, and this trophy was en view: Under Mr. Frank Hickman's able conductorship the part songs " The River " (Rogers) and " The Storm ' (Rogers) were expressively given an 1 called forth hearty appreciation The duet " Hark ! the g at bells" (S;mart) was snag with good taste by the Misses Lawton and Ashworth. Miss Carrie Hastings greatly pleased the audience with the favourite " Bid me discourse." Mrs. Arthur Booth fully maintained her reputation in "The Throstle " (Maud V. White) and " Caro Nome " (Verdi) which were artistically interpreted and on each occasion she was constrained to respond to an encore. "On the banks of Allan Water," with which she responded to one recall, was sung "pith much feeling. Miss E. Taylor aas eminently successful in the " Waltz Song" (German) and" April Song," and she, too, was vigorously endored. Miss F. Bennett sang " The River of Years " very pleasingly. Mr. Herbert Plumpton sang " The jolly sailor " with rare gusto, and another sea-song "Three for Jack" by Mr. Noden had a good reception. Messrs. Plurnpton and i Noden were associated n the daet "Proper men " _</p>
        <p>The motion was soconded by Mr. ViTILLIA M TJNSLEY, end carried. Mr. W. RICHARDSON moved :—That the names of the officersand their sareties, as read by the Actuary, be approved, and that two trustees and three managers sign the certificate to be sent to the National Debt Office. Mr. 'DLL seconded, and this motion was also agreed to.</p>
        <p>! and the ever popular quartette "Dear little shamrock" ; was feelingly sung by Miss Warburton,lliss Plumpton, I Messrs. Warburton and Davis, whose voices blended beautifully. Mr. Li. Wood sang " Come into the I garden Maud " with great acceptance; Mr. Davies interpreted the martial song "The King's own" in spirited style, and Mr. Warburton did well in " Lighterman Tom." A duet" When the wind blows •_</p>
        <p>in fr—om the sea. " was tastefully sung by Mr. and Mrs. Warburton. Mr. H. M. Ashton's humorous reading from Mark Twain's " Innocents Abroad " created much merriment and the laughable sketches by Air. Alfred Grounds added greatly to the success of the concert. Ur. Frank Hickman played the pianoforte accompaniments. The Rev. T. Miller proposed a vote of thanks to the choir and Mr. Frank Hickman, in responding said he than ked them for their support. Ile was proud of the choir he bad to lead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>TrIANKING THE CILURMAN.</head>
        <p>TrIANKING THE CILURMAN.</p>
        <p>The Rev. If. V. PEGOT proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Long for presiding. ,The Rev. J. F. NIATTIIEWS seconded, and said Mr. Long never seemed more at home than when presiding over a meeting of that kind. The vote was cordially passed. The CnAIRAI AN, in reply, said that wonld be the last time they woold see their friend Mr. Matthews at these meetings. His departure from Warrington would be a loss to them but a gain to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Warrington Wounding Case. At the Liverpool Sessions, before Sir W. B. Forwood and other magistrates, Joseph Marratt (61) joiner, who surrendered to his bail, was charged with maliciously wounding Isabella Mannion, at War- rington, on August 3rd.-Mr. Madden and Mr. Singleton prosecuted and Mr Riley defended. 'The case for the prosecution was that on the date named -Bank Holiday-there was a general row in Foundry-street, Warrington. The prisoner, who had been drinking, began wrangling with several neighbours, and producing a large pocket-knife he stabbed Mrs. Mailmen under the breast, causing such 'grave injuries that the woman had to undergo two operations, and was not even now out of danger.-The defence was that the prisoner was cleaning his pipe when the quarrel arose, and that the wounding of the woman was purely accidental.-The prisoner was found guilty, and was sentenced to nine month's imprisonment. Table, Floor, and Hanging LAmes. Latestdesigns now showing. See windows.-J. S. GARLAND, 51- 55 Bridge-street, IVarrington.-Advt. Thin ROM the Cricket Pavilion. John Toohey, 9, Oldham-street, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday with stealing from the Wan ington Cricket Club Pavikion some time between 1.30 and 845 p.m. on Saturday, November 27th, twenty-seven teaspoons and one hair brush, valued at 135., the property of Sydney Baxter and others. -He p/eaded not guilty.- The Chief Constable stated that about six weeks ago a man named Pestle- thwaite was sentenced to a term of imprisonment for stealing a number of spoons from th 3 Stockton Heath Hockey Club. On Monday it came to the knowledge of Detective Clough that prisoner had been disposing of some spoons, and was arrested.-Alic3 Baxter, wife of James Baxter, 69, Gaskell-street, Stockton Reath, stated that her husband was the caretaker of the pavilion. He saw the spoons (produced) safe in the cupboard at 6.39 p.m. on Saturday, November 27th, and did not see them again until Monday at the Police Court. -Detective Clough stated that acting on information received abut the prisoner selling spoons at 3d. per half-dozen, he arrested To3hey near the Royal Ciurt Theatre on Monday morning. Prisoner told witness he had found the spoons iu an old house on Town Hill in some boxes, but on being questioned at the Police Station he said " I will say nothing."- Pris oner, who had been before the Bench once before was fined 10s. and costs or 11 days. LINSEED COMPOUND ' for Coughs and Colls. Of proven efficacy. Of Chemists only. 91.,18.1Ad.,26.9d.-Advt Modern Vandalism in Churches. Mr. Hubert Congreve, of Moore, give a most interesting lecture on "Some Devonshire Church Screens ' before the merrthers of the Warrington Literary and Philosphical Society on Monday evcning. Mr. A. J. Jolley (president), presided over a large attendance. The lecturer sketched in a very instruc- tive way the origin and development of the church screens, and particularly dea't with Devonshire church screens, which were, with a few exceptions, constructed of oak. He described their design and construction, and enlarged on the wonderful richness of the Rarving and tracery work. There was sufficient evidence to show that this work was done; not by foreign crafts- men, who wandered from church to' church, but by the village carpenter and the villagers themselves. I Mr- Congreve re erred to the organized attacks made on the screens in the relens of Edward VI. and !Elizabeth and during the Commonwealth. He observed ' that the destruction of those beautiful samples of medieval craftsrnanship in molly instances still went on. Several of them bristled with nail, and tacks, ' driven in by young I:odic's and curates, who, if they tried to decorate the parson's drawing room furniture in like manner would aeon hear some home truths from the parson's wife, but on the priceless old oak in the I . I,,Pansh Church it did not matter. The era of whole- isale destruction had passed, ho trusted, for ever; and , that period was being succeeded by one of construction ' and new screens on old lines had heel erected in i several pariehes.-Ahoat fifty lantern views of some notable Dev mehire screens were exhibited, and ' admired by everyone. Mr. Congreve was heartily thanked for his entertaining and instructive lecture' on the proposition of Mr John Fairclough, seconded by Mr. Sileack, and supported by several other members. A Pair of " DOULTON " ORNAMENTS make a choice and acceptable present. Stock ranges from ss. to £5 per pair.-J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.-Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>PALAU AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALAU AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>An infinitely varitd, laughable and entertaining programme is submitted for the patrons of the Hippodrome this week. Astley's American Circus is a big draw. Not only are the animals remarkably well trained, but the competitions for the public are arousing great interest. Competitors can enter for either the revolving table, or for riding the pony or mule. Whichever they attempt their efforts are usually screamingly funny to the onlooker, and the laughter is loud and long. The most successful competitors will appear to night and receive money prizes for their circus endeavours. NV. J. Churchill, the famous comedian known as Uncle Willie, and a familiar personality at Llandudno in the summer, has nightly received an enthusiastic encore for his songs and jokes. Churchill is a bit above the ordinary comedian, and knows how to heartily amuse the audi- ence without descending to vulgarity. Another popular item is given by Margaret Radcliffe, described as an illustrated songstress. She sings "The moon has his eyes on you,' and other sentimental ditties in a sweet soprano voice, each song being illustrated by magnificent coloured slides. Finally the chorous is thrown upon the screen, and then the "gods" enjoy themselves t toroughly. The Donatos, the one-legged clowns, are a really wonderful pair of acrobats. Though eachlacks his right leg, he displays agility which would earn cheers for a pedant:ter endowed as nature intended. To see the Donatos jutup over stacks of Chairs —on one leg —is sufficient proof of their pluck and skill in overcoming a serious handicap in life. Daos presents "the greatest illusion ever seen in this country." Naturally it is out of the ordinary. A bedroom is erected on the stage open all round, above and beneath. There is nothing in it whatever. Curtains are drawn, Duos presents a suite of doll's furniture to the viear of the audience, folds the articles up in a cloth —one wave of the hands and they have vanished. The curtains are withdrawn, and the bed- room is seen fully furnished with oak wardrobe, washstand, clothes rack and bed, all full size. More- over, the bed is occupied by a lady who arises and steps to the footlights to show that "there is no deception whatever." It is a unique and mystifying performance. Duos is also a superior sleight-of- hand artiste. Johnny Holland -and Co. present a laughable sketch called "The Boat-builder" and Marion Armitage appears as a female Harry Lauder. Mr. Widdopp's orchestra performs in high-class style, and the biograph is also good.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. " CONSUL'S " VISIT.</p>
        <p>Fifty years ago exactly Darwin startled the scientifiC world by propodurling his marvellous theory of man's erolution from the ape To-day it is claimed that Darwin's justification is found in " Consul—the almost man —who will make his first appearance in Warrington at the Palace Theatre on Monday next " Consul," who has delighted and amazed thousands in many parts of England, is not only always garbed as an ordinary human being, but in every other respect he leads the life of one, travelling as an ordinary passenger by rail or \ boat, staying at a first-class hotel, where he takes his seat amongst the other guests at meals :yid comports himself in irreproach- able manner, driving out daily in his carriage enjoying the air and a good cigar at the same time, and after his work at night is over retiring like any ordinary reputable member of inciety to his human bed. The other artistes will be : —Rastus and Banks, coloured entertainers ; Lily Arthur, comedienne and dancer ; M4rgaret Radcliffe, illustrated songstress ; " My Friend Boots" by John Holland and Company; Primrose Comedy Four, harmonising vocalists in song !cena ; Captain Deverenx and troupe of dogs who illustrate a modern drama "The Streets of London."</p>
        <p>TherecV-iiii.re-a —grand picture matinee on Boxing Day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>" A Girl Redeemed From Sin."</p>
        <p>This modern drama, which has attracted good houses at the Royal Court Theatre, tells the touching story of a Lancashire mill lass who, fascinated with the glamour of high life,is induced to leave her humble home and embark upon a downward course. She is cast off by her parents and brought to a state of despera- tion but in the end, after a great struggle and many sensational incidents, everything works together for the best and the curtain is rung down on a scene of contentment. The play is cleverly presented by Mr. Geoffrey Fulton's Company. Miss Olive Fulton gives a powerful portr yal of Nancy Lee, which appeals to the emotions of the audience and Mr. Jo. Monk- house achieves distinction as the bold and dashing Will May. Zr. Phillippe Orr as Robert Denham and Mr. Hugh Carmichael as Ernest Sylveston make the best of unpopular, parts their acting being highly realistic. The tragedy of the play is relieved by an abundance of humour, Miss Ada Oxley as Peggy Grey and Mr. Len Thomas as Jim Jakes making the theatre resound with laughter by the many laughable incidents in which they figure. The aged parents Samuel and Mrs. Lee, are admirably represented by Mr. G. Reynolds and Miss Fanny Olive., The mount- in of the play is very effective. NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. • "CINDERELLA."</p>
        <p>A special Christmas attraction is announced for the fortnight commencing on Monday next. The marvel- lous Pardoes and Miss Lottie Holland will present the gorgeous comic and spectacular fairy pantomime " Cinderella " which is sure to attract crowded houses. The company consists of fifty artistes and is headed by Miss Lottie Holland. The special turns will include —The Marvellous Pardoes, described as the world's champion gymnasts ; Miss Nellie Rose, in her speciality dances ; Zenith, the famous animal impersonator • Edengale Trio of Continental Dancers. Five tons of beautiful new sceLery has been specially painted by Mr. George Singleton and there will be an electric coach and pair of splendid ponies. Matinees are announced for Boxing Day, December 26t1i, Friday, January Ist, and Saturday, January 2nd.</p>
        <p>'LINSEED COMPOUND with warm water in an excellent gargle for sore throat.—Advt.</p>
        <p>MY DEMI CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>I wish you all a most happy Christmas. I expect when you read these lines you will all be enjoying your holidays and the good things which this season of the year brings. Now I want to draw your special attention to a letter I have received from a member. He wrjtes to ask if I will insert this letter, ad- dressed to his cousins of the "Children's Cor- ner." I have very great pleasure in so doing, and I must thank the writer for his kind thought. I hope my young relatives will put into practice the suggestions made. You will feel more than repaid for any little self-denial that you may make. I have just received a let- ter saying that a large parcel of clothing had been sent me for the poor. I most heartily thank the children for their kindness. Now I must finish for this week, and hope you will all have a very happy time. With love, UNCLE NED. Many thanks to Florence Den l for a very pretty Christmas Card. •</p>
        <p>Weiodine to C. A. Blissit and V. Russell. -</p>
        <p>Dear Cousins,—ln our last Saturday's "Cor- ner," in the story of the "Poor Children's Christmas," you will have noticed the Christ- mas parcel that brings good cheer. Did the thought strike you that there will be lots of homes where such a parcel would be a bless- ing, but where that blessing will not come, unless, dear Cousins, it is sent by us? Sup- pose each one of us begs of our parents some of the 'good things which are being stored up in the cupboard for Christmas. We could help with our own pence. Think what a help a parcel of groceries would be in some of the poorest homes—for one parcel from each of us would not be much sacrifice, if any, to some of you; and think what help and pleasure it would give to those who do not know whether they are going to have food enough, even on Christmas Day. There will be no difficulty in finding such a family. There are always half- starved-looking children roaming the streets whom you could question. Trusting, Cousins, you will do your very best to make, at least, one home brighter at Christmastime, I remain, your affectionate Cousin, B. F.</p>
        <p>TUEES The night is cold gnd dark; the snow and rain, ,Mingled, are falling fast. It is in vain The orphan holds the tattered shawl so tight; The wind is cold and pitiless to-night. Her hands are clasped, and tears are in her eyes, Her feet are bare; and yet she feebly tries To raise her voice and sing a Christmas song; Sorrow is in her heart, joy on her tongue. She sings of "Peace on earth, goodwill to , men," And wonders, "What is peace?" yet singe again. Hungry and homeless, weary, wet, and cold, Her Christmas passes—'tis a tale oft told. Here is another scene of Christmas night. " It is a home of wealth; 'tis warm and bright; Green wreaths surround the pictures on the wall, And mistletoe entwines the statues tall. Ladies, whose costly gems their charms en- hance, Whirl in the giddy mazes of the dance; The sparkling winecup passes freely round, And laughter mingles with the music's sound_ No thought of those who wander in the cold, No thought of that sweet history of old, No thought of aught, save riot and display—' 'Tis thus they spend their holy Christmas Day- 'Tis in a cheerful room, where children three Are laughing o'er their gifts with noisy glee; One has a top, and one a bat and ball, But one, the least, displays no gift at all. "Come hither, dear," her motheiertes, "and say Where is the doll your father gave to-day?" The child looked down, and answered, sad and low, "I was so sorry for her, you know." "For whom, and why?" "The singer/in the street, Who has no gifts, no shoes upon her feet; I gave my doll to her." And here we find True Christmas spirit, generous and kind. See this small, humble cot; how fares it here?' The signs of want and poverty appear; The wind blows in, the fire is burning low, And through the thatch is dripping melted</p>
        <p>snow. An aged man sits in an old armchair, ?is face is worn, and streaked with grey his hair, Yet on his brow is peace. Before him lies God's Holy Book, on which he feastiliis eyes. He has true Christmas joy. Though lone and poor,</p>
        <p>_ He has no care; his hope of heaven is sure. No crowned king is half so rich as he, Whose riches last to all eternity. GLADYS KENDALL.</p>
