      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <p>-t----.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—Thanks so much for your lovely box of chocolate. I am delighted with it, and so are my friends. We have had a very sharp frost this morning, so it makes it feel like winter. I hope this warm skirt, which I am sending you, will help to keep some poor child warm this winter. It is so hard to think of them starving for want of clothes and good food while we have plenty, and are enjoying ourselves. I have been busy making book- markers, so I am sending you one for a keep- sake. I am very anxious to become one of your nieces. Dear Uncle Ned, I shall be very glad to have one of your pocket mirrors. Wish- ing you a very happy Christmas,</p>
        <p>CONSTANCE A. BLISSIT. [I am very pleased indeed to welcome you as a member. The skirt has already been given to the child of a most deserving man. HP has had no work for a very long time, and they are in real want of food, not to mention clothing. 'Re was most grateful for the gift. GRANNIES YULETIDE.</p>
        <p>It was Christmas Eve. The snow had left /44. falling, after it had made our village white; 1 tlie chimes were playing, and the carol singers v..ere going about singing, "God rest you, merry gentlemen," and other sweet old songs. Inside our cottage at Green Corner all was I brightness and peace. The iron pot (with the pudding in it) had been taken off the blazing fire, and the tea-kettle swung on—for darling Gran. loved a cup of tea; the kitchen was green with holly, and Granny sat in her high-backed chair with her knitting on her lap, and my little sisters, Rose and Daisy, had drawn their stools close to her. I, Violet, the eldest girl, sat facing Gran., and read to her of the coming of the Prince of Peace from the book on my lap. Before I say more, I must tell you that father, who is a market gardener and florist, had gone to market with mother, so we and Granny kept house. When I had finished the chapter, grandmother told us this little tale. She said:—" When I was a girl, I lived at Peach Farm, in South Africa, as a servant, and one Christmas Eve my mistress brought home a black baby she'd found lying in its dead mother's arms under a tree. She called it my, Christmas-box, but I didn't like it, and made up my mind to give notice. And I should have done so, too, if I hadn't gone to bed on Christmas Eve and had a beautiful dream. I seemed to see the Good Shepherd carrying a lamb in His arms, and He turned its face to me, and. 10, it was black, and He said, Lovest thou Me?' And when I answered, Yea, Lord,' He said, 'Feed My lamb,' and vanished. Then I remembered that God cared for all His children—that there were dark ones as well as white ones; and I took the child as my Christmas-box, and gave it my old doll, and the little thing took to me, and when I married and went away it clung to my gown and cried, 'Maggie, take Dinah! Take Dinah!' Of course, I could not. I was married on Christmas Day, children_; my little black Christmas-box was my bridesmaid. She wore a white frock and a red sash. So the queerest, dearest Christmas-box I ever had was a tiny waif and stray." 'KATE LILIAN COATES.</p>
      </div>
