      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>WINTER TALE.</head>
        <p>A WINTER TALE.</p>
        <p>Whilst the last generation was flourishing, there dwelt, near Boston, an opulent widow. lady, who once afforded a queer mani- festation of the odd compound of incompat- ibles called "human nature." It was Christ- mas Eve of one of those old-fashioned winters which were so "bitter cold." The old lady put on an extra shawl, and as she hugged her shivering frame, she said to her faithful negro servant: "It is a terribly cold night, Saila; I am afraid poor Mrs. Green must be suffering. Take the barrow, Scip,, fill it full of wood, pile on a good load, and tell the poor woman to keep herself warm and comfortable. But, before you go, Scip, put some more wood en the fire, and make me a nice glass of flip." These last orders were duly obeyed, and the old lady was thoroughly warmed both inside and out, And now the trusty Scipio was about to depart on his errand of mercy, when his considerate mistress interposed again: "Stop, Scip; you need not go now, the weather has moderated." G. KENDALL.</p>
        <p>_+___</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>CRICKET AND FOOTBALL-WHICH /</head>
        <p>CRICKET AND FOOTBALL-WHICH /</p>
        <p>PREFER, AND WHY.</p>
        <p>I prefer football, because you can have more fun,. for whilst you are waiting for your inn- ings in cricket the time is gone, and then you can't have them at all. Another thing is, that football does not cost so much—you have only to buy the ball; whilst you have to buy in cricket bats, ball, stumps, and bails. Foot- ball is nice for cold weather, and a healthy exercise, as there is no standing still, like in cricket. There are some papers full of football news and great matches against expert foot.- bailers. C. B. Fry is one of the greatest foot- ballers in England. A magazine all about sports is named after him.</p>
        <p>RICHARD KENDALL.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l am writing ,an eFcay on football, as I prefer it to cricket. We foot- ballers are very lucky to have such fire weather, and the ground nice and moist. After it has been raining, and the boys go out playing football, sometimes they kipper), to slip, and very likely introduce the seat of their trousers to the grass. Once I was playing football, and was knocked down and accidentally kicked underneath the chin, but that is not so bad as some have. I like football best because there is more life in it. ERNEST LEWIS-</p>
        <p>A.: "How do you know that Maier hats coma in for a fortune?"</p>
        <p>B.: "Why, formerly people always said ha was crazy; now they say he's original." .</p>
        <p>Eva: "Does your new dress fit you well?" Vera: "Oh, splendidly. I can hardly move or breathe in it."</p>
        <p>-+-</p>
        <p>A school teacher lately put. the question, "What is the highest form of animal life?" "The giraffe!" responded a bright member of the class.</p>
        <p>"I would like my bill paid," eaid a tailor to an impecunious customer. _</p>
        <p>"Do you not owe anyone anything?" asked the debtor. "No, sir, I am thankful to say I do not?'</p>
        <p>"Then you can afford to wait, was the answer, as the customer walked out of the shop with a self-important air.</p>
      </div>
