      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>[FROII OUR LONDON CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>Of the measures which, in spite. of a protracted and strenuous session, have necessarily been dropped for want of time, the most important are the Housing and Town Planning Bill and the Irish Land Bill. Mr. Asquith announced on December 9th, that the Government will do its best to secure the passage of the Irish Land Bill into law as soon as may be. The enormous amount of time and attention given to the Housing and Town Planning Bill during twenty-three sittings of the Standing Committee need not all be thrown away. The course which the Government proposes to adopt is to introduce it again at the earliest possible moment after the re-assembling of Parliament, and to curtail the Committee stage within as narrow dimensions as is reasonably possible so as to get the Bill to the House of Lords at a very early date next session. The cbief place in the week's work bad been occupied by the Miners' Eight Hours Bill and the Port of London Bill. Indian affairs are generally regarded by Parliament as the province of a few specialists, but Lord Morley's statement on the of India was awaited with keen expecta- tion. IJOL ough all the business, some of it more or less exciting and some of it humdr urn, which Parlia- ment has been winding up, the Lit eral Party, at any rate, has never been able to put out of their minds for a moment "the dominating issues in politics " as Mr. Asquith has accurately described it —" the dominating issue because in the long run it absorbs and overshadows every other." That issue, of course, is the conflict between the caprice of an irresponsible Chamber aml the will ot the representative Chamber, acting upon a direct mandate from the nation. THE GREAT AND GOVERNING ISSUE. The Government cannot consent to accept the dictation of the House of Lords as to the date or manner of dissolution. Nor will they concede to the Opposition the advantage which would be given away by a premature disclosure of the tactics by which they propose to restrict the veto of the House of Lords. But it will not shiink from grappling with " the great and governing issue " that lies before it. In " simplification of issues and concentration of pur- pose "is to be found the key to success. Nothing would be gained by thrusting to the front, for the sake of enlarging the area of conflict, an indefinite number of contentious proposals. A. self-denying ordinance which would restrict the Government for the remainder of this Parliament to a colourless programme containing only non-amtro- versial measures likely to pass the Lords, would be as Mr. Asquith has said, a confession of humiliation. The course of events has given a certain amount of guidance as to the direction which this process of simplification and concentration must take. Finance stands out with unmistakable pre-eminence as the first and weightiest claim upon Parliament during the next session. It is largely through a bold administra- tion of finance that the attrck of Tariff Reform can most effective], be met. And, as the Prime Minister has suggested in words of pregnant significance, "Finance is an instrument of great potency and of great pliability, and it might be found to be, in some directions at any rate, a partial solvent of what, under our existing constitutional conditions, might otherwise be insoluble problems."</p>
        <p>;Tv'ernmt,p,</p>
        <p>WHERE WILL THE HOUSE OF LORDS STOP? Lord St. Aldwyn, who spoke on the subject of the House of-Lords at the conference of Gloucester repre- sentatives of the National Union of Coaservative and Constitutional Associations last Monday, declared that be did not anticipate any collision botween the two Houses on the question of nexi; year's Budget. What- ever financial proposals the Government might make, he did not far a moment suppose that the House of Lords would 'depart from its customary course of dealing with Budgets sent up to it from the Commons, merely because it happened to disapprove of any new taxation that might be propo2ed. He thought the Lords would never allow the finances of the country into confusion, and deprive the Government of the means for carrying on its administrgtion, merely because it disapproved of any new measures of taxation proposed." There is always the weight of long expelience and a balanced judg- ment behind Lr,rd St. Aldwyn's utterances. But it may be questioned whether he really understands the spirit of the present House of Lords. There is a levity about its decisions which is capable of upsetting any cAculations. The Prime Minister has truly said that it is impossible to forecast the fate of any legislation presented to the Uppr House, because this is dependent entirely upon the caprice, except that any measure which challenges a monopoly is assured of rude handling. TIIE EIGHT HOURS BILL.</p>
        <p>Still, it is possible to read by various signs that Lord St. Aldwyn, in spite of his avowed consiction that the Lords would never be guilty of unconstitu- tional practice in the matter of the Midget, is not without misgivings about the past and future conduct of the Chamber which he now adorns. He admits that the relations between the House of Lords and the House of Commons urgently demand adjustment ; that the claim of the hereditary Chamber to force a dissolution whenever it chooses is intolerable. While it would be unfair to attribute the earnestness with -which he argued for the second reading of the Miners' Eight Hours' Bill entirely to tactical considerations, the fear that the Lords might heedlessly put the last finishing touch to the comulati we proof of their indiffer- ence and hostility to the wishes of the people lent a distinctive colour to his pleading. LORD LANSDOWNE'S MOTIVE.</p>
        <p>No one could be in any doubt as to the motive which inspired Lord liinsdowne's sesent to the second reading; It was "tactics, tactics, and yet again, tactics. He asserted that the Bill was an interference with the price of coal. He asserted that '' any inter- ference with the price of coal was about as dangerous a step as any Government could contemplate." And then he proceeded to announce that he, "for one," could not vote against the second leading of the Bill. And why? Out of three reasons which he alleged, one was that the House of Lords " might be repre- sented" as pinning itself to obstinate refusal of a measure which, on humanitarian ground, deserved its favourable consideration. Another reason was that, if the price of coal did rise at any time, the Govern- ment could be saddled with the responsibility,bccause they had introduced this Bill. But Lord Lansdowne did not tell the House that If, immediately after throw out a measure desired by a million workers,they might raise such a storm about their ears as he did not like to think of. Possibly another vicit reason for his vote might be the recollection that a measure of this kind once formed part of a programme promul- gated with the object of bringing a Unionist Govern- ment into power. A PLAY UPON WORDS.</p>
        <p>It may be that Lord Lansdowne Witfi quite sincere in his argument that the Bill was a tneasuro of Pro- tection. Economic questions have no attraction for his aristocratic mind, so that he may really be involved in the ridiculous confusion which puts in the same category the protection —in the ordinary or the common sense —that. a Government ought to give</p>
        <p>against harmful conditions of life and the econ„omic Protection that consists in the unfair subsidisation of an industry by a taiiff levied upon foreign goods. But Mr. Balfour knows better. He is the author of an economic pamphlet, once exposed for sale on the bookstalls at the ridiculously low price of a shilling. Yet he went even further than I.ord Lansdowne. Ho actually put forward the gigantically absurd con- tention that the Eiglit Hours Dill was worse than Protection- because Protection brcught in some revenue, though by a costly process, while the Bill would produce none. Of course, the same argument applies just as well to any taxation for the purposes of education or any other beneficial object. To endow - sahoola is to place a burden on the community, just as Protection dces; but Protection raises revenue, and education does not. THE COAL CONSUMERS' LEAGUE. One of the most striking features of the debate in the House of Lords upon the Eight flours Bill was the challenge flung out to Lord Newton, as Chairman of the Coal Consumers' League, by Lcn d St. David; and Lord Newton's failure to reply. Lord St. David said that, personally, he should estimate the increase in price, from the operation of the Bill, as at the o ut- side twopence or threepence' a ton. No responsible person had asserted that the permanent rise in twice would be more than two or three shillings a ton." But the Coal Consumers' League went About at by-elections foretelling a rise of five shillings a ton. D.d Lord Newton, as Chairman of the League, endorse that estimate, or did he think it indefensible ? Lard Newton's reply was silence.</p>
        <p>COAGULTNE," TENASITINE.' Cements for mending all things.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON SENSATION.</p>
        <p>ACUTE PILES AND ULCERATION CURED BY ZAM-BUK</p>
        <p>Winter's cold and wet were directly responsible for the long and acute suffering from piles and ulceration which made Mrs. Susannah Flughston's life a living ' misery until Zam-Buk completely cured her. As will be gathered from her story told at her home, 7, Regent-street, Warrington, to a " Warrington Examiner " reporter, Mrs. Hughston's sufferings and cure were both extraordinary. • "It was during last winter that I began to suffer from piles and ulceration," she said. " I believe tbe complaints started as the result of a chill I got when out in the wet and culd weather. The piles became so very painful that I could neither sit nor remain standing. The ulcers broke out all over my body, nearly driving me mad with the awful pain ; in, fact, many a time I yearned for any escape from the misery I was daily enduring. " I tried all kinds of doctors' ointments and medicines as well as numerous cheap salves, none of ' which did me any good. While suffering very acutely one night I suddenly remembered that my mother had derived great benefit from Zam-I3uk, and always kept a stock of this balm in her home. I sent for a box, and applied the balm that night before going to bed. " Any words of mine are not sufficient to describe the way the burning pain was eased through Zatn- Buk. Regular dressings with Zam•Buk not only banished the piles completely, but healed every ulcer on my body, growing a new covering of healthy skin in every case. lam now quite well and strong again, and have no pain or suffering at all, thanks only to Zam-Buk.</p>
      </div>
