      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Warrington v. Wigan.</head>
        <p>Warrington v. Wigan.</p>
        <p>Warrington made their second successive visit to Wigan on Saturday, this time in League football. Fine weather prevailed, and although there was not such a great crowd P 9 at the previous week's cup-tie, there were quite 12,000 people present. Both teams were handicapped by the calls on players for the Test match at London, Warrington being short of Dickenson end Brooks, and Wigan of Jenkins and Thomas. Mr. Kennedy, the appointed referee, did not appear and the whistle WAS taken by Mr. Tillotson, one of the touch- judges, who did his work satisfactorily. The teams were : _ _</p>
        <p>„ . Warrington : Tilloy, back ; Trebearne, Taylor, Cart- wright and Fish, three-quarter backs ; Jenkins and Lloyd, half backs ; Dowell, Thomas, P. Boardman, A. R. Boardman, Chesters and Hewitt, forwards. Wigan : Gunn, back ; Leytharn, Sharrock. Todd and Miller, three-quarter backs ; Gleave and Jones, half backs ; Itarnsdale, Whittaker, Johnson, Cheetham, Blears and Barton, forwards. Referee, Mr. S. Tillotson (Salford) ; touch-judges, Messrs. S. Gregory (Runcorn) and M. Green (High- field). Warriugton started strongly and Fish was early prominent in an attempt to round Miller. The visitors continued to be aggressive and had hard luck a few moments later, when Fish, taking a penalty shot, bad the misfortune to hit the cross-bar. Gleave and Sharrock improved Wigan's position, and after further progress had been made by Leythain, the visitors were penalized and a fruitless shot at goal was the result. Jones broke away, but his pass went astray, and in turn Warrington again got within measurable distance of the home goal, where Fish tried hard to get through, but failed to escape the vigilance of Miller. Warrington would not be denied, and after Fish had raced splendidly along touch, Gunn essayed to clear, but his kick travelled to Tilley, who steadied himself for a drop and the ball went sailing over the bar. The Wigan forwards showed up well on resuming, and combined play between Todd and Miller lead up to a try to the latter. Gunn failed at goal, so that when ends changed Wigan led by a try (three points) to a dropped goal (two points. Warrington fell away somewhat in the second half and the home forwards controlled matters. A round of passing ended in Todd scoring at the corner. Still forcing home the attack the Wigan forwards swept down repeatedly on the Warrington line, and after Tilley had fly-kicked in try- ing to repel one rush the ball was taken over by Barton and Sharrock failed at goal. Warrington improved slightly after this, and Taylor made a most praise- worthy effort to get through, whilst Treberno was also held up inches inside. There was, however, little sting in the attack, and the Wigan forwards rushing op again, more asserting Blease got over and this time Gunn added the goal. Warrington made a flue burst in the closing stages of the game, and this time Fish was successful in scoring a try, but the goal kid( was a failure. Result : Wigan, one goal four tries (14 poi ntr.) ; Warrington, one goal one try (five points).</p>
      </div>
