      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>RESULTS TO DATE.</head>
        <p>RESULTS TO DATE.</p>
        <p>—Goals—</p>
        <p>P. W. D. L. For Agst. Pt Heathside Parochial Wyelifts 10... 7 . 0... 3 ... 18... 10... 14 St. James' Ar pl ey-street 8 ... 4 ... 2 ... 2 ... 14 ... 16 ... 10 Beamont St.Barnabas' 8 ... 4 ... 0 ... 4 ... 15 ... 17 ... 8 Silver street 9 ... 3 ... 1 ... 5 ... 18 ... 22 ... 7 St. Mary's Trinity Hamilton st 9 ... 1 ... 3 ... 5 ... 12 ... 24 ... 5 Christ Church 10 ... 1 ... 3 ... 6 ... 13 ... 41 ... 5 St. Alban's ll 0 ... 2 ... 9 ... 1 ... 39 ... 2 Christ Church boys paid a visit to the Silver-street ground and were heavily defeated by eight goals to one. Nurse played a sterling game at centre forward for the winners, always being dangerous and keeping his front line together splendidly. The losers "got off their mark" well but failed to " stay the distance." St. James' entertained St. Barnabas' and after a scrappy game ran out winners by 5-3. Both sets of forwards played poorly, and would never have scored if the respective goalkeepers bad not been very kind to them. Crozier for St. Barnabas', and Munn for St. James', both at left back, played good games, and could not be blamed for any of the goals. St.Alban's met the stalwarts of ParochiaLand suffered the usual fate of those who venture to try conclusions with that brigade. The other unbeaten team were matched against St. Mary's, and ran out easy winners by 5-0. The for- wards of the winning team backed up by three good halves, were always aggressive, and though Potter and Dombavand played well at full-back they were unable to keep back the tide of defeat. The St. Mary's for- wards, though clever, lacked weight, and could not get through the defence set up by Hughes and his fellow backs. The losers were without five of their regular players.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>TIIIRD ROUND LIVERPOOL JUNIOR CUP.</head>
        <p>TIIIRD ROUND LIVERPOOL JUNIOR CUP.</p>
        <p>lands' Recreation after defeating Bickerstaffe Mission by three goals to one, and Runcorn Town by six goals to one, meet Bluudellsands this afternoon on the Recreation Grounds, Gorsey-lane. Rick-off 2.30. Admission 3d., boys Id. Rylands' team will be as follows :—Goal, Booth ; full backs, Glover, Roberts ; half-backs, Darbyshire, Bains, Newton ; forwards, Lawless, Warren, Spencer, Lucock, Derbyshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>CHRISTMAS POSTING.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS POSTING.</p>
        <p>Details of the Arrangements at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The public are urged to take advantage of the system of advance posting of Christmas letters and cards which will remain in force at the Head Post Office and the Market Place Branch Office until next Monday night. The arrangement is now universal and shorn of the restrictions of former years and it is hoped that in this way the enormous pressure incident to the Christmas season will in some measure be relieved. There will not be any special boxes for local posting, as it is found that the public can scarcely be expected discriminate, from a postal point of view, what corres- pondence is local and what is outside the local area. The town letter boxes will be cleared finally at 6 p.m. for the night mail on the 24th inst., and the foal collection at the head office will be 9.30 p.m. It is hoped in view of the Christmas pressure that on the 21st, 22nd, 23rd and 24th, the public will post as early as possible for the night mails. It will not be necessary to have extra premises this year, as the space provided in the new office together with the temporary occupation of the old premises will provide the necessary extra room required for dealing with Christmas work. special relief horse coaches in addition to the regular motor coaches will Tan between Manchester, Liverpool, Chester and Warrington respectively. In addition extra horse vehicles, motor cars, and mail vans will be provided for town and rural district work. Extra staff will be employed both on the indoor work and on collection, and delivery duties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>LATEST TIME OF POSTING.</head>
        <p>LATEST TIME OF POSTING.</p>
        <p>On the 24th December (Christmas Eve) the last collection of letters for despatch by the night mails will be made from the town letter boxes at C p.m„ after which time no letters will be dealt with until the following night, except those intended for local delivery the next morning, for which letters only, a collection will be made at 8 p.m, The above times of posting also apply to the Town Offices for both letters and parcels. It should be borne in mind, therefore, to prevent disappointment, that the Head Office in Springfield-street, is the only place where letters and parcels may be posted after 6 p.m. for the night mail of the 21th, and that the box at that office will close at 9.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>FRODSIIAM, lIELEBY AYT) r.vatm. On the night of the 21th December, the last collection for the night mail will be made at 7 p.m. from the Sub-office, after %bleb time no letters or parcels,except those intended for Local delivery tvill be dealt with until the nest night.</p>
        <p>LYAIM ONLY. The tnual supplementary collection made from the letter box and the railway station at 9 p.m. will be suspended on Christmas Eve.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>By " Chralist."</p>
        <p>Warrington and Wigan are about tiisd .of mating each other, though the feeling of itiFty is probably more pronounced on the Warrington side. Two visits to Wigan on successive Saturdays, and two defeats in consequence, do not whet the appetite for more. So far this season, Wigau have beaten Warrington three times. If fate should bring the clubs together once more in the Northern Union Cup, I hope Warrington will have choice of ground. Then per- haps they will reverse the order of things.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, the Warrington men played just as badly as they did well the previous week. It was not Wigan's superiority which accounted for the score of 13-5 but Warrington's spiritless display. The forwards, for once, were quite out of the hunt, and the regularity-with which the ball came out on the Wigan side of the scrum was monotonous. Nothing seemed to spur the Warrington vanguard to a fraction of the energy displayed the week before and the result was that the backs were occupied mostly in defensive work. Tilley had a lot to do, and even he failed to respond in the usual effctive fashion. Two weak attempts to clear on his part gave Wigan their last two tries. Cartwright was another weak spot. This young player is not fulfilling his early promise of making a class centie. The rest of the men were good, and if they could have got possession oftener might easily have taken a big pelt in the scoring. Fish got his usual Wigan try, and that was the only redeeming feature.</p>
        <p>Brooks and Dickenson both worthily r.pheld the honour of the club in the test match at London, in fact the half back worked the scrums for Thomas just as well as he usually fills the outside position. He had the pleasure of scoring a try and kicking two goals, the last of which equalised the game on the call of time. It was a brilliant exhibition of football, with the honours on the home side. The Kangaroos would have bean beaten but for the wonderful goal- kicking of Messenger who sent the ball over the bar on five occasions. Each side scored 22 points, but the NIL team collected six tries against the Australians' four. Next time the selectors had better include a goal-kicker, for certainly Btooks has few pretensions or qualifications in this direction.</p>
        <p>The defeat at Wigan has not, fortunately, made much difference to Warrington's position in the League, but they have a lot of leeway to make up and I hope solid progress will be reported during the next few weeks. As a matter of fact, the Warrington men should in the next six engagements right off the reel They .rneei Broughton Rangers (home) to-day, Leigh (home), W idnes (away), Runcorn (home), Ebbw Vale (home), and St. Helens (home, still to be arranged). If anything like form is maintained there should not b more than four League defeats debited to Warrington risht to the end of the season.</p>
        <p>The South-West Lancashire League up-to-date is as follows :</p>
        <p>For Agst. P. W. L. D. G. P. G. T. P. Pis. Wigan 4 3 1 0 ... 8 13 '55 ... 7 6 32 ... 6 St. Helens 4 3 1 0 ... 811 49... 5 8 34... 6 Leigh Runcorn 5 3 2 0... 9 9 43... 7 7 35... 6 Widnes Vilatringt'n 5 1 4 0 ... 5 5 25 ... 514 52 ... 2</p>
        <p>The League table, up to date is as follows : For Agst. Per P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. 'T. P. eentage Wigan l4 13 1 0 53 69 323 18 16- 81 92.85 Oldham ...14 13 1 0 43 59 263 15 15 75 92.85 Halifax l2 11 1 0 29 47 199 8 15 61 91366 WaketrldTl4 10 3 1 34 63 257 23 21 109 75310 Runcorn l2 9 3 0 16 33 131 17 14 76 7500 Hunslet l3 8 4 1 22 34 146 18 26 114 6510 Batley l4 9 4 1 22 32 140 20 24 112 67.35 Ebbw Vale 15 9 5 1 23 45 181 26 31 145 6333 H'ddersfrldla 9 5 2 25 44 182 27 29 141 62.50 Keighley 14 8 5 1 38 47 217 18 28 120 60.71 Merthyr Tl 3 7 5 1 21 29 129 32 21 136 5769 Salford l4 S 6 0 31 30 153 24 30 138 57.34 Dewsbury-13 7 6 0 27 30 144 21 27 129 53'84 Leeds l5 8 7 0 23 43 190 25 30 140 5333 Hull 17 8 8 1 41 51 250 27 42 189 5000 St. Helens 13 6 6 1 27 34 156 31 36 170 50000 Leigh l4 7 7 0 24 27 129 26 21 124 50310 Swinton —l6 8 8 0 25 29 137 19 32 134 5000 Hull K.ll 13 6 G 1 33 33 192 31 34 164 50•13 13roughton13 6 6 1 23 31 149 23 34 143 500 Warring'n 13 5 7 1 26 25 127 20 :27 121 42.30 Bradford 15 6 9 0 28 37 167 35 42 196 4000 Barrow l2 4 8 0 17 18 85 29 41 181 33.33 Treherbert 12 4 8 0 13 13 65 11 35 127 3333 Mid-Rh'da 11 3 8 0 10 16 68 25 22 145 2727 Widnes —.12 2 9 1 15 19 87 23 31 139 20•83 ' York l4 211 1 24 25 126 37 59 251 17'85 Barry 13 2 11 0 7 14 56 39 72 204 15.38 RochdaleHl3 1 11 1 9 15 63 31 36 170 1153 Abetdare 12 111 0 16 22 98 44 62 274 833 Bramley —l2 111 0 14 14 70 38 67 286 8.33</p>
        <p>One of the keenest and most exciting struggles seen at Warringion for a lcng time was that on Saturday between Warrington "A " and Wigan "A." The rivalry between the two for the Combination Championship has lasted for some years, and it was never keener than now. Warrington "A " won at Wigan in the last five minutes on October 17, and the the visitors were determined to wipe out the stain, if possible, on Saturday. At half-tima they looked like succeeding with ease, for they hao pl.d up 11 points to Warrington's2 points, mainly through four fine goals from the foot of Charlie Creevey. Warrington had missed more chances than usual, and their star seemed to be set. But in the second half they pulled themselves together, and a tly by Bradshaw was improved by Mclntyre from the touch line. Flushed with success the homesters scattered the IVigan men and Forster dashed over near the goal. There was breathless excitement when Mclntyre took 11)e kick which would give Wariington the lead or leave Wigan stilt ahead. The young full back failed at the easy one as hp had done earlier in the game, and the struggle le-commenced. Both sides strained every nerve, and great was the joy when Mclntyre made amends by kicking a penalty goal. Creevey had two chanches to win the game,hut Fortune would not smile again, and Warrington won by a single point-12-11.</p>
        <p>The match between England and Wales will take place at Broughton on Monday. December 28th, Warrington will have two men engaged on each side. The following are the chosen teams : Wales.—Paddison (Merthyr), Thomas (Halifax), Llewellyn (Oldham), Jenkins (Wigan), Williams (Halifax), Thomas (Wigan), Hopkins (Aberdare), Ruddick (Broughton Range s), Foley (Ebbw Vale), Davies (Merthyr), Rcos (Salford), O'Neil, and Dowell (Warrington). England.—Gifford (Barrow), Batten (nunslet), Dickenson (Warrington), Lomas (Salford), Tyson (Oldham), Brook (WarringtBn), Hilton (Halifax), Jukes, Higson (Hunslet), A. Smith (Oldham), Longworth (Oldham), Mann (Bradford), and Robinson (Halifax). Reserves.—Clarkson (Leigh),Taylor (Warrington), Miller (Wigan), liolroyd (Huddersfield), Silcock (Wigan), and Padbury (Runcorn). Referee : W. M'Cutcheon (Oldham).</p>
        <p>To-day's fixture should prove an attractive one, for Broughton Rangers always give Warrington a good game. The locals have the defeat at Broughton to wipe out, and with the team available which trounced Oldham they should do the job in handsome style. The team is : Tilley, full back ; Fib, Dickenson, Taylor and Treharne, three-quarters ; Brooks aud Jenkins, half-hacks ; O'Nei 1, Dowell, Thomas, Cheaters, A. Boardman and P. Boardman, forwards.</p>
        <p>The final for the Lancashire Cup between Wigan and Oldham takes place to-day at Broughton, and I fancy the Wiganers will just wta, though they have never done much in cup-ties. The match may cause the abandonment of the Broughton and Warrington " A " team fixture, for which the Warrington team has be= chosen as follows : Mclntyre, full back Landor, Jordan, Cartwright and Bradshaw, three' quarters ; Lees and Lloyd, half-backs ; Belton" Ifrrner, Harrison, Gibbs, Het‘itt and ltradantwo forwards.</p>
      </div>
