        <p>QUESTIONS FOR TARIFF REFORMERS.</p>
        <p>X.—lf we pay for Imports in money, how is it that we import more gold and silver than we export P</p>
        <p>Protectionists believe that imports spell loss—that they are paid for in money, which money goes to pay wages to foreign workmen. If this were true we should constantly be exporting gold and silver. As a matter of fact, we have no gold or silver mines worth mentioning,and on balance we are always importing the precious metals. The Board of Trade returns show : IMPORTS END EXPORTS OP GOLD AND SILVER IN THE TEN yEThits, IS9S-1907. Imports... X 479,400,000 Exports... ... 432,000,000</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>W. it WAIT'S &amp; CO. LTD COMPTON HOUSE, 14VERPOOL.</head>
        <p>W. it WAIT'S &amp; CO. LTD COMPTON HOUSE, 14VERPOOL.</p>
        <p>_ '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Excess of Imports £47,400,000</head>
        <p>Excess of Imports £47,400,000</p>
        <p>Thus in the ten years IDB-1907 we imported £11,400,000 worth more of the precious metals than we exported, which is conclusive proof that imports are not paid for in money. They are, of course, paid for by exports of British goods or services—i.e. by British labour. L. G. C. NI.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>LETTERS TO TIIE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO TIIE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>Wkisky at the Workhouse. Sir,—ln reference to your account of the Board of Guardians meeting in Saturday's issue, Mr. Bent is reported to say that the whisky shewn in the Private Fund Account was consumed by the inmates. I wish to inform the ratepayers that such is not the case. The inmates did not consume any of it. As Mr. Bent made the statement before half the members had assembled and [ the Chairman ruling the disscussion out of order, it is ' not fair that such an erroneous statement should go unchallenged and as a Guardian I feel in duty bound to contradict it.—Your's fathfully,</p>
        <p>W. C. BROOKS,</p>
        <p>10, Fennel-street, Vi'arrington. December 15th, 1008.</p>
        <p>Tariff Reformers and Trade Returns</p>
        <p>Sir,—l observe from the Board of Trade's latest returns that Britain, despite strikes, lockouts and* trade I depression has emerged once again triumphant in commerce. For the past nine months the returns are as follows : Imports £370,084,000 Exports £285,063.000 As our greatest competitors in trade are the United States and Germany, and the population of these two great nations is each considerably larger than ours, the first named being-double, and therefore her productive power should be relatively larger,how comes it that her exports during the same period were only £252,503,000, and that Germany's were also less ? The value of these two nations' imports was also less than ours. I shall be pleased if our local advocates of Tariff Reform will kindly explain if they ran how it is, that if our Fiscal system is so foolish as they represent it to be, that year in and year out, we are always ahead of the protected nations.—Yours faithfully, E. ENGLAND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Children's Cot.</head>
        <p>The Children's Cot.</p>
        <p>Si,—Would you kindly allow me to express through the medium of your paper the sincere thanks of the Board of Management to the scholars attending the Day schools in NVarrington for the splendid start they have made towards raising the funds necessary to endow the Children of Warriagton Cot No. 1. I can assure them that whatever sum, however small, may have been given by any child, it is recognised as a practical proof of the interest takqn in the Children's Ward by ' the children themselves. There must have been a certain amount of self-denial exercised to give anything and I personally am more than grateful that the sug- gestion I ventured to make some time ago has been adopted with such enthusiasm. I have heard of many instances of the keenness of children to help and there is a touch of true feeling in the mind of a child who brings a single halfpenny with the simply apology "I should have brought more but father is not working," or another one bringing its money box containing nine. pence "because mother was in the Hospital now." I should also like to thank the Committee and in fact the whole of the teachers for the trouble they have taken in organising the collection of the money. I know it has been to them a labour of love and a matter of con- gratulation to all concerned that every school in the borough has furnished its quota to the total sum realised.—Yours faithfully, JAMES HENRY SHE rfIURST. Briery Croft, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Wesleyans and Dr. Clifford.</head>
        <p>Wesleyans and Dr. Clifford.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l read the letter of "Wesleyan "in last Satur- day's " Examiner" and whilst deprecating with him any attack upon Dr. John Clifford's personal character I think he misses the purport of the comment by the Editor of the " Record." The quoted article does not question Dr. Clifford's personal character but solely discountenances his attitude with regard to the Educa- tion strife when he advocates income tax resistance. The Editor of the Record believes in a" better way" and so do I. I think to ignore the law by adoption of unconstitutional means deplorable and decidedly bad taste. It creates a precedent for everyone with a grievance "against the Government " to perpetrate disorder grad annoyance. Of course Dr. John Clifford is not infallible—he commits errors of judgment—and I think him guilty in this respect. If everyone who had strong feelings against a certain law resorted to passive resistance in one way or another what " fun" there would be, to be sure. The bulk of enlightened public opinion is against Dr. Clifford in this respect. To associate his name with Grayson and Licensed Victuallers is to couple his "indiscretions" with theirs and not his general character. At the same time it is possible for Victor Grayson and Licensed Victuallers to be good men, and they are not all bad by any means. Really, Dr. Clifford should consider the Government in the Education wrangle. They have done their level best to settle the vexed question, and deserve consideration. Income-tax resistance apart, however, John Clifford has done yeoman work for Christ and Righteousness, and I join hands with" Wesleyan " in deploring lack of men of similiar attainments and ability in the various branches of his church. There is plenty of room for good men but for some reason or other they seem to hold hack.—Yours</p>
        <p>BAPTIST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Mr. W. P. Rylands and Tariff Reform.</head>
        <p>Mr. W. P. Rylands and Tariff Reform.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l read with interest Mr. W. P. Rylands' numer- ous addresses on Fiscal Reform. From my point of view it seems a pity such abundant energy is not reserved for a worthier cause. These addresses are necessarily of a very scrappy and incomplete character And Mr. Itylands has no opportunity of developing the arguments upon which his somewhat sweeping state- ments are based. flow for example is it possible for Mr. Rylands to develop a reasoned argument before a street corner audience who have assembled to hear the reasons why a member of a particular trade should be returned to help to rsanage the municipal affairs of our town ? Mr. Rylands appears to consider Municipal Elections as preliminary canters for the greater Parlia- mentary race. While Mr. Rylands has his eye on the tariff walls, with which other countries have encumbered , themselves, the municipal electors are thinking about the whitewashing of their own walls and the paving and lighting of their own back streets ; while Mr. Rylands has his mind on imperial issues the electors are thinking about setts, bridges, and like matters. Mr. Rylands has given us a number of thoughtful articles on Socialism with which as a whole I generally agree. The only point at which I would join issue with him is where after shewing that the Socialistic cure is useless, he triumphantly holds up his own medicine labelled " Tariff Reform" as the great cure-all. What I would like to suggest to Mr. Rylands is that he should give to the public in a similar set of articles his explanation and defence of the particular kind of Tariff Reform he advocates. The great difficulty those who believe in Free Trade have in dealing with the" first constructive plank of Unionist policy " is to know what that policy really is. Ido not mean in minute detail, but in lead- ing principles, and is it to be applied to food, raw material, or manufactures ? if Mr. R3lands thinks well of this proposal and adopts it, I feel sure the Warrington presi will give full facilities to any who may differ frora hira if they should desire to point out wherein his arguments appear unconvincing and faulty. CIIARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Penketh, December 15th, 1908.</p>
        <p>Employers and Compulsory Service_ Sir,—Mr. Haldane in his speech at Rochdale expressed his surprise ,at the unanimity with which local employers agreed that compulsory service would cause far more dislocation than his own system by which some serve, many shirk. But why this surprise ? What do you expect from an employer when he is faced by these two alternatives ; either to let his men off for two, if not for three years, or to let them off for fifteen days for several successive Nears ? So the trap is cunningly our War laid, and th Minister rubs cheanntds a employershe eeilss cji.rathita, andehda s another nail into the coffin of compulsory service. But suppose the employers of Rochdale had had the choice of these alternatives: "We offer you two systems. Under one system every young man in your employ- ment at the age of eighteen (the number so called out would be only stout 2.7 per cent.) shall, if fit, undergo four months military training,and after that period shall go to camp for a fortnight each year for not three or four</p>
        <p>_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, December 19, 1908.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>years, and shall then be freed from further training. THE EDUCATION QUESTION. Under the other system we shall expect you to allow , as many of your men as possible to join the Terri- 1 _______ torials, and as some employers are unpatriotic we hope , that you will contribute more than your own share. INTERESTING DISCUSSION BY LOCAL Of course the short training proposed will not give you a reliable article, but we will put. that right by CLERGY. .... .. . . . --</head>
        <p>years, and shall then be freed from further training. THE EDUCATION QUESTION. Under the other system we shall expect you to allow , as many of your men as possible to join the Terri- 1 _______ torials, and as some employers are unpatriotic we hope , that you will contribute more than your own share. INTERESTING DISCUSSION BY LOCAL Of course the short training proposed will not give you a reliable article, but we will put. that right by CLERGY. .... .. . . . --</p>
        <p>mobilisation in the hour of danger, and a six months' training after war breaks out." What will the sound business man say to these two propositions? Surely although in the first system the training is a recurring factor, on the second only a possibility, and though therefore there is in the first apparently more dislocation to trade, the business man vi ill recognise that the advantage of the second system far outweigh its alleged inconveniences. He will say- 1. I get value for my money because I get a reliable article, which will prevent that terrible dislocation certain to be caused by any national disaster. 11. The burden is distributed, and equal burdens break no backs. The training being regular and systematic, can easily be arranged for. In case of mobilisation, my competitor, who at present discourages Territorills, cannot seize an unfair advantage over me, whose business will be heavily handicapped by the departure of men to a six months' training.' The business man must after all look at these military schemes as insurance against the losses of war, lie must say to himself" What premium shall I have to pay and what is the value of the insurance which I get ? The employer who prefers Mr. Haldane's scheme with its small premium but doubtful insurance, is like a man who, baying combustible materials in his works liable to become dangerous in hot weather, prefers to stint his fire brigade, saying to himself that if the summer should become unusually hot, there will be ample time to make his brigade efficient. Two or three years go by, then suddenly the temperature rises rapidly. At once besets his brigade drilling hard, but. too late. The fire breaks out and his men, insufficiently trained, are unable to beat back the flames. The far-seeing employer should welcome the real insurance of universal training. The premium is small, the fighting value is great. All thinking men recognise that the day of war has not gone by ; that there is need to insure against it. The firm for business meain this country to insure with, is The National Service League, 72, Victoria-street, London, W.—Yours etc., GEORGE CROSFIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>Rector on Advantages of the Dead Bill. ARPLEY-STREET AND THE SECONDARY SCHOOL BEYOND THE PALE,</head>
        <p>Rector on Advantages of the Dead Bill. ARPLEY-STREET AND THE SECONDARY SCHOOL BEYOND THE PALE,</p>
        <p>absei</p>
        <p>-- A conference of the clergy and lay representatives of the Rural Deanery of Winwick was held in Toft's Assembly Itooms,Bridge-street, on Thursday afternoon. The Rev. Canan Penrhyn (Rural Dean) presided over a fair attendance. rhe following appointments were made :—Secretary, Rev. E. C. E. Carleton; treasurer, Mr. E. Draper. Representatives on Diocesan Com- mittees: Finance Association, Rev. Canon Willis and Mr. James Wood; Church Building Society, Rev. J. W. Williams and Mr. Robert Stone; Benefices Augmenta- tion Fund, Rev. R. Smith and Captain Withington ; Standing Committee of Diocesan Conference, Rev. Canon Stevenson, Mr. G. Mather, Mr. W. Thompson; Board of Education, Rev. J. Davies and Colonel J. D. Buckton; Church Aid Society, Rev. Canon Pentlayn and Dr. Mouncey ; Board of Missions, Rev. B. Percival. The most important item for discussion was "The Future of our Church Schools." At a recent meeting of the clergy of the Deanery the following resolution was carried nern-can:—" That this meeting of the chapter considers that a peaceful settle- ment of the education question is only possible on the basis of all-round tolerance and equality, irrespective of creed, and without distinction between denomina- tionalists and undenominationalists, and for this reason cannot accept the terms of the compromise embodied in the Education Bill." The RURAL DEAN stated that he had forwarded copies of the resolution as requested and had received acknowledgments from the Prime Minister, the Bishop of the Diocese, Mr. A. H. Crosfleld, M.P., and Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P. Mr. Seddon told him that he had voted against the Bill. The Rev. .lixtEs DAVIES : But not on the same grounds as you would. The RURAL DEAN, continuing, said the withdrawal of the Bill gave the Government time for some reflection and he hoped they would come to a better mind and see their way to introduce something which would be less destructive than the late measure would have been. The future of the Church schools was hanging in the balance. They had been saved for a time; they would have been taken away from them altogether if the Bill had passed. In the single school areas, with which he had full sympathy, they could have injured the Church's prospects in every way. The Bill would have taken away the right of Churchmen to those schools which they had built for themselves without Govern- ment aid, and it appeared to him it would have been an act of great injustice. It was qnite clear that the Council schools were becoming much more numerous and the Church schools were,to a certain degree, fading. He did not, however, think they were fading so quickly as had often been stated, and he believed the number of schools to be given up would diminish considerably in future years. They were said to be going at the rate of three schools per week but a good many of the schools that had been handed over were not Church schools. The Wesleyans, for instance, had given up most of their schools and allowed them to become Council schools. The weaker schools among the Church schools had gone,and many which no doubt were not suitable for the purposes of teaching had naturally been superseded. They should try to get the character of the Church schools recognized as such. Canon Stevenson had strongly advocated that there should be schools allotted, some to Roman Catholics, some to Churchpeople and some to those who did not wish for any distinctive teaching, and he (the speaker) did not see that there could be any real difficulty in that. It was in the single school areas that the greatest difficulty arose, but he thought the Govern- ment ought to recognize the character of Church schools. Let the schools built with the money of Churchmen remain as Church schools, but let there be a teacher told off to teach those who dissented from Church instruction. Ihe religious difficulty was one which, to his mind,was enormously exagerated. There was really very little difficulty in the schools them- selves. It was very important that they should keep the atmosphere of the Church doctrines in the Church schools and it was for them to hold to them to the utmost of their power, whilst being ready to grant to everybody else the same freedom they wished for them- selves. The RECTOR (Rev. Canon Willis) said he acquiesced in the carrying of the resolution above-mentioned nem CO7l, and he repented of it afterwards. He really was a dissentient but he did not wish it to go forth at the time. He still felt as he did before—that with substantial amendments which he hoped might have been agreed to, he hoped the Bill would pass. He know he was in a minority in the matter. The amend- ments he would like would be that the headmaster should be allowed to teach and that it should be pre- sumed that parents were willing that their children should be taught denominational or undenominationar instruction if there was nothing to the contrary,instead of obliging them to consent. They knew how lax some parents were. He did not think the clergy realised as they should have done that Mr. Runciraan offered to double the grants for the lease of the schools. A great point that influenced him (the Rector) was that every child who attended an elementary school would, if the Bill had passed with such amendment as he had named,have received religious instruction of a kind and, he believed,good as far as it went. There were two kinds of undenorninational instruction—the kind that was represented by Unitarians which they would all differ from, and the kind which taught our Lord's life and moral duties and many other points on which they did not differ. If amendments had been allowed which would have granted a real right of entry—as Mr. Asquith himself said, a genuine and effective right of entry—into the Council schools, it would have been an immense gain. Although it had been said they could not count on assistant teachers in the future, he could only say he bad reliable information that his own assistant teachers would all have volunteered to give denominational instruction. He wished to emphasise the awful result in the future if there was no religious instruction of any kind in the schools. The CHAIRMAN : Nobody advocates that, that I know of. The RECTOR: But the Bill allowed religious instruction, The Rev. J. CARSON (Wargrave) : It seems to me, with all respect to the Rector, that the time is being wasted. The Bill is dead and gone. Is it going to be revived ? The Rector is very properly defending him- self for being in a minority, but. I don't think the Bill is before us for discussion. The RECTOR : lam coming to what is before us. What I want to look at is the future of the country. There are two schools in Warrington at the present time where no religious instruction at all is given. (" Shame.") There is the Secondary school and also the Arpley-street school. Having dealt at some length with this aspect, the Rector said that all children might have received religious instruction if the Bill had passed. Secondly, be believed the Church would have been allowed real facilities for going into the Council schools and following their own children. There were five days of the week in which they could teach in their own schools. He was told by diocesan inspectors that there were parishes in which the clergy never went into their schools. They had been fighting about the right of entry into other schools and yet some clergy did not go into their own schools. It was their bounden duty to go into their schools to see that the teaching was given or to give the teaching. The Rev. J. RYDER (Newton) said the crux of the difficulty with regard to Dissenters was the conscien- tious point that they did not care to pay rates for the teaching of religious instruction with which their con- science was not in harmony. He did not know that they had ever been called upon to do that, because the teaching in the Church of England schools was such to which every Nonconformist minister assented when he was appointed. He believed the difficulty could be solved by allocating the rates paid by Churchpeople, Roman Catholics and Nonconformists to the mainten- ance of their respective types of schools. The Rev. E. E. PETERS said the time had come for the Church of L'nglaial to take up a more offensive attitude and not to rely entirely on the defensive. Ile thought some action should be taken with regard to the Secondary and Arpley-street schools. The Rev. H. A. LESTER said it was a serious matter that the pupil teachers attending the Secondary School received no religious instruction in school hours. If Churchpeople put their backs into the matter they would get some alteration ; they were strong enough to make themselves felt. With regard to the Church schools, the great grievance at the present time was that they did not allow any facilities for the Noncon- formists. He did not think that if they offered facilities, anyone would come in and teach the Noncon- formist children, but he advocated offering them. Then they could afford to have a few Nonconformist assist- ants on the staff of a large school. If they said : "We will give you facilities and we shall not inquire too closely as to the religious views of onr assistant teachers," the Nonconformists would not have a leg to stand upon and they would have more or less pacified them The Rev. J. CARSON said he would rather children did not get scholarships at all than go to the Secondary School and get no religious instruction. He was confident that if they told the people what religious i instruction they gave n the Church schools, they would Ibe more than satisfied with it. As far as be could make out Irons sermons of Nonconformist preachers, reported in the newspapers, they had no Christianity at all. He knew that very well from is particular instance under his own observation. Our Saviour was left out and also His Atoning Sacrifice and the great work He came to do and He was confined merely to being a good religious teacher and setting foith bright doctrines. The Rev. B. PERCIVAL said he could find no instance where Nonconformists had availed themealves of facilities when they had been offered. The RECTOR would not agree with a resolution beginning with an expression of satisfaction at the withdrawal of the Bill, and eventually the following resolution was unanimously passed; Seeing that Mr. Runciman's Bill has been withdrawn, this (Con- ference urges Churchmen to take e,,ery possible step</p>
        <p>to defend and strengthen Church schools in the various districts." Considerable discussion took place with regard to the Absence of religious instruction at the Secondary School and Arpley-street school. The Rev. G. Vein Owss moved: "That a com- mittee be appointed to approach the management of the People's College and Secondary Schdol on behalf of the clergy and lay representatives of Warrington and neigh- bourhood with the object of obtaining religions teaching in those schools." There were here two important schools in Warrington where the Name of Christ apparently was not heard. That should be enough to make them do all they could to reverse the order of things there. The Rev. E. E. PETERS seconded. The Rev. W. BRACECAMP said the matter should have been:discussed when ,the Secondary School was built. He suggested the formation of a Parents' Lea gue In every parish. The Rev. J. DAviris said they had appealed to the Education Committee on more than one occasion with regard to the Secondary School and the clergy of the town offered to teach there, had the right of entry been permitted. The Rev. E. C. E. CARLETON said they must take more care about the formation of the committee of the Secondary School and about the election of members of the Town Council who possibly had to do with these matters. It was, he believed, quite possible for them to get a system of religious teaching in the Secondary School. He suggested that the parents should be approached. There had been great carelessness in the appointments made at the Secondary schooLthere being no security that any of the teachers should Lave their competency tested, as to whether they wereprepared or able to give religious instruction. The pupil teachers who were being educated at the Secondary school and [ who were not receiving religious instruction were being distinctly disqualified from appointments tbey might otherwise obtain. r The Rev. J. 11. G. HOLLINGWORTR asked what religious teaching was meant by the resolution. The Rev. G. VALE OWEN : Simple religious teach- ing, undenominational or denominational. The Rev. T. A. J. RIDPATTI said he did not think they bad the slightest right to support undenominational teaching. By their ordination vows they were bound to support definite Church teaching, He advocated asking for right of entry to the Secondary School. It was ultimately resolved that an instruction be given to the Committee of Church School Managers to take action in the matter of the religious instruction in the Seoondary School and, if possible, in the Arpley- treetschooL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>Infirmary Requirements.</head>
        <p>Infirmary Requirements.</p>
        <p>Sir,—As your readers well know, the extensions at the Infirmary have entailed an enlarged permanent charge upon the Institution for maintenance. An appeal has been issued for extra support from our friends and I am glad to say there are signs that next year's contribution from the Workingmen's Committee will show the appeal hat not been made in vain. For instance, I have just received a letter,a copy of which I enclose, announcing that the workmen of Messrs. the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., have decided to increase their contribution by 50 per cent. Naturally, I hope that this good example will be largely followed by employees of other firms. The same generosity shown all round would free the Board of Management of all anxiety for the future.—Yours Ste., J. H. J. HAMPSON, Hon. Secretary Workingmen's Infirmary Committee. The Pearson and Knowles Coal 6; Iron Co. Ltd., Warrington, 16th December, 1908. Dear Sir,—l am pleased to inform you that the employees of the Iron and Engineering. Depart- ments of these works, have this year Increased their Annual Donation to the Infirmary by 50 per cent. from £lOO to £l5O. This increase has been granted on account of the recent Extension, which will in future demand a much larger sum than formerly for maintenance. Yours truly, for Sick Fund, P. C. PRICE, (Secretary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>Peers and the Licensing Bill. Lordly Shareholders.</head>
        <p>The Peers and the Licensing Bill. Lordly Shareholders.</p>
        <p>Sir,—At the public meeting held on Saturday night at Parr Hall, to protest against the recent action of the House of Lords malority in its treatment of the Government's Licenffng Bill, a question was pat to the Chairman, Mr. Dodds,asking was it a fact, as purported to have been said by Mr. Robert Pierpoint to a meeting of junior Conservatives" that probably not one in a hundred of those Peers had any pecuniary interest in the subject whatever." I may not be quite clear about the exact phrasing, but that is the spirit of the I question as understood by many in the meeting. I forward you a copy of the Good Templars' "Watchword" of December sth with an incomplete list of the Peers who voted against the 2nd reading and their interest in various breweries as far as can be ascertained, if it is of any use to you, or of interest to your readers, you might afford it space in your columns. If Mr. R. Pierpoint did make the statement attributed to him. and his facts on other political questions are as equally accurate as this, one can only piously add, may God help the future intelligence and judgment of those junior Conservatives who may have to rely upon that source of instruction alone. Believe me yours very truly,</p>
        <p>PATRICK O'BRIEN,</p>
        <p>125, Wilderspool road, December 14th. THE BLACK LIST. In the majority against the Licensing Bill in the House of Lords were the following peers whose names appear as shareholders in brewery companies. Some of these peers hold the stock as trustees, either for their families or for others. Peer. Brewery Company. Ardilann Guinness. *Ashcombe Avebury Meux,Watney, Whitbread, Sic. *Barnard Barrymore Cannon. Bath Robinson's B. *Bathurst Whitbread. *Bessborough Lion. *Brownlow Burton Bass *Camden Tadcaster T. *Carnarvon Meux. *Cawder Clanricarde *Clarendon *Clinton *Cobham Cannon. Colville of Culross Darnley *Dartmouth Guinness, Hoare. *Dartrey *De Ramsey Downe Cannon,Manchester,Hoare Faber Fitzwilliam Gainsborough *Halsbury *Harrowby *Hatherton Hindlip *Howe *Huntingdon Iveagh Guinness, Watney. *Kensington Knaresborough Worthington. Lansdowne Bass, Watney. *Latham *Leeds Lilford *Londonderry Gninness. Lovelace Barclay, Watney. Manners Barclay, Hoare. *Manvers Barclay. - *Midleton lnd. Coope. Newcastle . North Cannon. Northumberland Play fair *Portland New Westminstsr. *Banfurly Barclay. 'Ratlamore Revelstoke Whitbread, Watney, Guin- ness, Bristol Georges, Bass. Rothschild Worthington,Watney,Tru- s. man, Guinness, Meta. Roxbrirghe Watney. *St. John of Bletsoe Allsopps, Watney. Stradhrooke New Westminster,Watney. Stratheden Sutherland Bristol Georges. Waldegrave . Cannon. Willoughby de Broke Hoare. Wenlock Watney, Bass. *Wolverton *lndicates Trustee. This list, moreover, is not exhaustive, for only 40 or 50 companies out of 200 are examined, and the debenture holders, who are probably a larger class, are not known. In the list of shareholders examined, 140 peers own between them as much as £2,416,000 worth of stock. It is obvious that the position of a trustee places a p,er in a different position from a private stockholder, and in quoting these names we do not for a moment ' suggest that these peers were influenced by conradera- tioas of private interest. But the list shows to what an extent the class from which the peers are drawn have been induced to invest in companies for the sale of liquor, and demonstrates the hold which the Trade has acquired in high places. It is a statutory rule that no Magistrate shall sit as a licensing Justice who has an interest in the Trade. If th's salutary condition had been applied to the Lords so that no peer should vote against the Licensing Bill wha had interests in the Trade, the majority against the Bill would have been much smaller.— Daily Chronicle."</p>
        <p>'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs &amp; Colds. Relieves Mthma and difßcult breathing. 90., Is. lid.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>PEOPLE'S COLLEGE CONCERT.</head>
        <p>PEOPLE'S COLLEGE CONCERT.</p>
        <p>Another Great Success. In writing of People's College concerts, the same pleasant task invariably falls to one's lot—to record complete success—and this year another signal triumph is to be placed to the credit of the teachers and scholars. Thursday evening saw the Parr Hall crowded in every part with an audience which expressed its delight with unmistakable enthusiasm. On the platform were 350 children whose happy faces showed that to them this was the red letter day of the year and, far from being nervous, all seemed to revel in the parts allotted to them. Children and grown-ups alike found the concert a source of pleasure and the time passed all too quickly. In the history of People's College concerts, Thursday's entertainment will hold a worthy place. As an opening chorus, ' There's a land," with its admirable patriotic sentiments, was most fitting, and the stately tune went with a fine swing. As usual, the choruses formed a conspicuous and highly accept- able feature of the programme. They were sung with precision and a full realization of the spirit of the composers, and were so nicely varied as never to approach monotony. Cowen's ever-welcome setting of Mrs. Heman's " The Pater Land " was interpreted with great expression. One of the gems of the even- ing was "The Minnesinger's Gift " in which the alto part produced a fine effect, the blending of the voices being excellent. As an item in the lighter vein, " Polly-Wolly-Doodle " proved immensely popular. While the chorus remained seated, it was amusing to see a different boy rise in his place to sing each successive verse, and the innovation so took the fancy of the audience that the piece had to be repeated. Appropriately to the Christmas season, the conclud- ing chorus was the beautiful carol "It came upon the midnight clear." Several of the boys distinguished themselves as soloists, and each had the honour of a hearty recall. William T. Wilkinson sang that favourite, "Mary of Argyle "—a difficult composition for a boy to render— very sweetly,and the performance did him great credit. John Muir, equipped as an angler, with rod and line which had hooked a very diminutive flsh,created consider- able merriment with A fishy fishing story." As a memorising feat it was notable, and gestures and accents were quite in keeping with the words. Horace Knowles sang of the valiant deeds of "Andy Mc Elroe." He made a typical " broth of a boy " and sang and comported himself with an easy self-confidence which found great favour. Wilfred E. Rice is deserving of special praise for his solo " Whistling." Attired as an errand boy with basket on his arm, he sang cheerily of the pleasurea of whistling and at the end of each verse he himself broke forth into whistling which was afterwards taken up by the chorus. Reginald Chalmers has an exceptionally good voice and, in the garb of a son of the Emerald Isle, he gave " oft to Philadelphia"</p>
        <p>With conspicuous success. Novelties are always to the fore at People's College concerts and the many original features introduced this year showed that the ingenuity and enterprise of the teachers and scholars are by no means exhausted. Alexander Plumpton and William W. Muir, as ragged newsboys, walked on to the stage proclaiming the sensational news of the day which included a " catas- trofe " in Knutsford-road and something about the doings of the Warrington Town Council. Then the Christmas bells were supposed to ring merrily, causing one of the urchins to soliloquise on the hard lot of those to whom winter only means greater privation. It was a capital item and was cordially received. A con- tingent of girls carrying baskets of flowers sang "Posies," appealing to passers-by to buy their wares, and they also gave a pretty dance. Perhaps the out- standing feature of the evening was the " Cachuca Dance" from "The Gondoliers." It was performed by girls, picturesquely costumed as Contadini, who went through the almost bewildering movements very cleverly to the accompaniment of ear-haunting music sung by the chorus. The variety of colour and gaiety of action combined to produce a kaleidoscopic effect and the audience was quite captivated. A group of girls, whose coon " make-up " was strikingly complete, were heard to good effect in the popular ditty "Ma Curly-headed Baby," and they lavished caresses on dolls while they sang it. " Miss Milligan's girls " was another highly successful item in which girls acquitted themselves with distinction. They were the models of primness when under the mistress's eye, but the moment Miss Milligan had turned ber back they were to be seen indulging in such pastimes as the cake-walk. "The Drum-Major" gave some of the boys an opportunity to shine. Harold Warburton as the pompous drum-major, walked at the head of a band of drummers, and his rendering of the song was spirited and went with a grand swing. Richard Lawton has previously gained laurels as a humorist at these concerts, and on Thurs- day evening he did better than ever. He gave a little sketch with the utmost assurance, narrating his seaside holiday experiences, and convulsed the audience when be told them of what he had to endure through the suffragist activities of his .` missus." Later he scored another splendid success with one of Harry Lauder's favourite songs "The saf test of the family," interspersed with a mass of amusing patter. It was indeed remark- able to see such a diminutive youngster bolding the attention of the large andience with his jokes, and making them laugh heartily. From beginning to end the concert was quite in keep- ing with the high traditions of the school which has for so many years played a prominent part in the I educational life of Warrington. Too much praise cannot be accorded to the teachers and the scholars whose joint endeavours produced such happy results. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Ban., was at his usual post as organist and conductor and the smoothness with which everything passed off must have been a source of great gratification to him. Before the concluding items, Mr. Wmt.tam LONG, one of the school managers, said be was loth to bring them back to this workaday world after the delightful evening they had been spend- ing, but he would ask the audience to remember that the pleasing entertainment they had witnessed had involved a great amount of care and work for which he was sure they would wish to express their gratitude. First he must mention the man at the head, the captain of the echo-el—Mr. Edward Wellington. (Applause.) To him that must have been a very anxious occasion but his heart was SO much in the work that he never seemed to think of the time and trouble that he had to give to it. Then there was Mr. Payton who had been well to the front, and all in that crowded audience could tell that the rosult of his teaching showed plainly that the hearts or the children were with him and that it was a labour of love. The parents also deserved thanks although perhaps they did not look for them. He was sure the mothers' hearts must beat with additional pride that night to see the way in which their children had behaved and the nice appearance they presented on the platform. Of course, the children had to obey orders and he was told that special thanks were due to.some of the teachers for the interest they bad taken in the concert. He had particularly to. mention Miss Brown, Miss Wallington, Miss Bayley, Miss Clay and Mr. Bowen. (Applause.) The children, too, were deserving of praise, and the manner in which they had gone about their work showed that they enjoyed it quite as much as the audience. As an old manager of the school—he had</p>
        <p>For all Chronic Chest Complaints, take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. is. 10., 2s. 9d.—Advt.</p>
        <p>been connected with it between thirty and forty years —it was a source of pride to him to see how the school —bad been conducted during the whole of that time. (Applause.) He wished that all through the country this religious question which had been the bane of education for many years could be put on one side. Let them remember that dogma was not religion. During the whole of the time he had been connected with that school, he bad never heard of parents taking excep- tion to any religious teaching that had been given there. (Applause.) He hoped those who had some voice would make it heard in the land and try to bring about that , happier time when their children could be taught a ' religion without a dogma. (Applause.) Mr. LONG then called for cheers, which were enthusi- astically given, for all the helpers he had named. The concert concluded with the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>TAXATION OF LAND VALUES.</p>
        <p>Conservative Opposition.</p>
        <p>The end of the Liberal Government's term of office in our Local Parliamentary Debating Society came last Thursday evening, at the weekly meeting in the the Conservative Club Assembly Room. They give up Office with a good record. Licensing, Education,Home Rule for Ireland, Old Age Pensions, Nationalisation of Railways and Land Values have all been dealt with and carried safely through some lively discussions and close divisions. The President of the Local Government Board piloted his Right to Work Bill but fell with a minority of six. Two private member's Bills were debated upon and then placed upon the Statute Book. The speeches on Thursday evening on the resumed debate on the Taxation of Land Values were interest- ing and they drew a good muster of members. After several questions had been dealt with Mr. W. J. BURGESS (London University) resumed the debate. He said he opposed the Bill because he did not see that justice would be served merely by taxing those people who were wealthy in the land. We did not specially tax those people who wore wealthy in other things. The Government had instituted a capital tax, and if they were to levy it fairly, they ought to levy it upon capital. The Government made a dis- crimination of quality. The land was the natural heritage of the people and it ought to belong to the people. But the people who owned the land ought not to be taxed when they did not tax people who owned other things. If they taxed the owners of land because they did not consider they had absolute right for that land, they must tax the capitalist as well as the land. They must either tax all capital or none at all. (Hear, hear.) it might not be very difficult to tax all capital and the only solution they could have was to put a higher income tax upon the large incomes received by the landlords and the capitalists. That would be fair for them all. (Hear, hear.) He moved the rejection of the Bill. Mr. R. G. PIERPOINT (East Manchester) in a very racy speech said that the Bill was absolutely "On-English." The Bill did not differentiate between the landowner who looked after, his land or the man who did not recognize his responsibilities. It treated the good landlord just as badly as the man who used the land as an instrument to undermine the people's welfare. (Applause.) Be objected to the Irish Party being allowed to vote upon that question, for when they wanted to nationalise the railways they would not come to vote. It was intolerance for them to come and vote for the 13ill. He seconded the motion before the House. Mr.J4triat AN (President of Board of Trade) said one tax rested upon the income derived from the investinent,and not upon the amount of the investment,which was a right and proper thing. This tax rested upon the value of the land. The man would be taxed upon what he stated was the value of the land for rating, and upon that alone. He maintained that every section of the com- munity would beneflt,very materially by the measure. (Applause). Mr. BAINES (West Derby) moved an amendment : " That this Bill shall not apply to ground rents pur- chased at a capitalised price before this Act came into force." Mr. F. G. TAYLOR (Petersfleld) formally seconded the amendment. The CHIEF SECRETARY POll SCOTLAND (Mr. W. H. Sherburn) said they could not accept the amendment for the reason that the land was bought with a full knowledge of its responsi- bilities. If the responsibilities had been shirked for a long time they could not help that. On a division being taken the result was as follows : For the amendment, 22 ; against, 27 ; majority, 5. Mr. J. MCDERMID (Paisley) moved an amendment standing in the Member for Blackpool's name(Mr. A. Bennett) which was "That this Act shall not apply to agricultural land, which is the raw material for produc- ing the food of the people." Speaking for himself, Mr. McDerraid thought that it anything were to be got out of the taxation of lend values it ought to go to help the local taxation rather than Imperial, because it was locally where the values were made. Mr J. W. VERNON (Maidstone) seconded. The CHIEF SECRETARY FOR SCOTLAND Could not accept the amendment, because the tax was put not on the value of the land, but on independent increment and the passing of that Act could not bring more land for cultivation and therefore it would zot affect the rights of farmers. Mr. Oseontee (Newcastle-on-Tyne), supported the amendment of the member for Blackpool. It was unfair to tax industry. He was greatly in favour of having a tax put upon the site values. On a division being taken a tie was announced, both sides claiming 25 votes. The Speaker decided in favour of the amendment. Mr. NIGHTINGALE (Normanton), said it seemed to him to be an axiom to get everybody else to bear their burdens so long as they did not suffer themselves. The i Bill was a step n the right direction. They should ask for more until they got the earth, which was their's by Divine right. Mr. F. G. TAYLOR (Petersffeld) said it was an inequitable measure. He held that the majority of landowners to-day had bought their land and in so doing they had paid the full value for it. Mr. WYNNE JONES (Foreign Secretary) asked the members to adopt the broad principle of taxing land values. He said the whole of the National Liberal Party were backing the Bill ; and all that was best in the Independent Labour Party. So long as the evil of the land being held up existed, the full benefit of other measures would not be felt. The present system cramped the people. The taxation of land values would do a great deal to solve the housing and unemployed problems. THE CHIEF SECRETARY FOR SCOTLAND in replying to the various criticisms offered, said he quite agreed with the contention that capital had a right to be taxed as well as land. He had still to learn that he could not do one good thing, unless he did another. (Hear, hear.) They proposed to do one thing and they left their friends of the Opposition to do the other. (Applause.) He was hoping that when the Land Values Bill got to the House of Lords the Letder of Opposition would use his influence to get the Hill passed as a non- contentious Bill, for he had last Thursday said it</p>
        <p>was so. Mr. STANSFIELD (Leader of Opposition) : I have been converted. (Loud Laughter.) On a vote being taken for the amendment before the House" that the Bill be read 6 months hence " it was rejected by 29 to 2L Mr. W. H. STANSFIELD, Leader of the Opposition, will form a ministry for the next session. Votes of thanks were heartily accorded to the Speaker and the officials of Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>SMOKING CONCERT AT THE LION HOTEL.</head>
        <p>SMOKING CONCERT AT THE LION HOTEL.</p>
        <p>ucc.eed(</p>
        <p>The rnembeis of our Local Parliament enjoyed pleasant relaxation from their legislative duties on I Wednesday evening, when a smoking concert was held 1 at the Lion Hotel. For the nonce the Speaker cast aside his wig and gown and members of all parties ' fraternised as though political differences were unknown. lhe attendance was not as large as might have been expected, but nevertheless the affair proved highly enjoyable. Mr. Nathan Cook, jun., had arranged a capital musical programme which gave the utmost satisfaction. The first half of the programme included the songs " Two eyes of grey "and " Marching along " given in his best style by Mr. F. Fieldhouse, " Love me and the world is mine" by Mr. W. Kilduff, and " Blow, blow, thou winter wind" by Mr. N. Cook, jun., each item being c-editably interpreted. Mr. Scarisbrick pro- vided a pleasing diversion in the shape of a ventriloqual entertainment and performances on some unique home- made musical instruments. During an interval The SPEAKER. (Mr. C. Plinston) delivered a brief address. He said he was perfectly convinced that although as far as numbers were concerned the last session had not been as conspicuous as the previous one, the society was fulfilling the object for which it was inaugurated—the educating of the younger men of all political parties in political knowledge. It was also answering another valuable purpose, that of train- ing young men to rise in debate and give utterance to their ideas. One of the most notable features during the last few weeks had been the number of young men who had made their maiden speeches, and without being invidious to any party he must say that as Speaker he was very much inclined to think that the rising talent was with the Conservative and Nationalist parties. He might be wrong ; he hoped he was. (Loud laughter.) They were looking at it in the wrong light. (Laughter.) He hoped he was wrong in the sense that at did not show a dearth oi rising ability on the other sides, but that they had not the courage to show what was in them. He thought that in the interests of the society whoever he called upon to form a Government for the next session would feel in honour bound to accept. If the party he had in mind were called upon, they would have some most interesting debates and hd hoped that at the close in April they would he able to fill that room with a convivial gathering and drink "Success to the present Government." (Applause). The second part of the programme was then pro- ceeded with. Mr. W. Kilduff gave a recitation entitled "An Actor's Art," describing in a pleasing</p>
        <p>manner the way in which an actor rose and fell in such a short space of time, and held the audience spell- bound by his dramatic performance. Mr. Fieldhouse sang "The Soldier's Toast," the chorus of which was heartily sung by the company. lie was re-called and gave the little ditty "Sweet little girl." Mr. H. M. Ashton gave readings from Mark Twain, which were thoroughly enjoyed, the account of "The Funeral of Bnck-Fanshaw " caus- ing roars of laughter. Mr. J. W. Willett, sang" Ansa Mine" in which his powers as a tenor singer were pleasingly exemplified, The audience was delighted and accorded a hearty encore, Mr. Willett responding with " Mary of Argyle." Mr. George El. Smith, of " Merrie England "fame, proved that his capabilities as an exponent of the comic element have in no way diminished since his arduous task of a few months ago and he was recalled for a humorous song " Singers and talkers." Mr. N. Cook, jun., gave a song familar te all baritones—" Revenge "—in capital style. Mr. W. H. STaNsFIELD (Leader of the Opposition) in moving a vote of thanks to the Speaker said no-one would doubt that Mr.Plinston was the right man in the right place. With regard to the problems with which the incoming Government would have to deal, if his party to office, they would try to bring in some contentious measures upon which there would be sure to be great opposition from all sides of the House, and not Bills like the one for the Taxation of Land Values with which there was so much agreement. The Rev. C. HARVEY COOK, in seconding, said that some people might think it out of place for a "rev. gentleman" to be at such a meeting and in such a place, but he was very pleased to be there. Although he did not indulge in intoxicants,he drank ginger beer. and such like, which did not, he was glad to say, get into his head. (Loud laughter.) As a temperance man hs could really see no objection to attending at an hotel to spend such a convivial evening and he was sotry to think some members of the Society had stayed away on that account. (Applause.) He would be very glad if the Speaker would accept office for the neat session, as he was sure a substitute could ‘not be found to equal Mr. Plinston. (Applause. The vote was heartily accorded. The SPEAKER in reply said he had been thinking that he would rather someone else took the position and gave him a rest, as he found he was rather lacking in the art of oratory through want of practice. As he was a member of the Debating Society 25 years ago and took part in the debates at that time, he felt he would like- to continue in the line of fire in which he had been brought up. (Applause.) But he would leave himself in the bands of the House. (Applause.) The SPEAKER then moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Lee for the kind way in which he had entertained the society. Mr. OSBORNE (hon. secretary) seconded, and said it had hurt him very much to think that a number of the members bad absented themselves owing to the meeting taking place at the hotel. He thought that the cause of temperance was much more harmed by the action of such people in refusing to come to such a beautiful room as the one that Mr. Lee had placed at the Society's disposal, then the teetotal party conceived. The vote was passed with acclamation. Mr. J. F. AWBERY (Chief Secretary for Ireland) remarked that the Irish were satisfied, as they had now gob Home Rule. A vote of thanks having been passed to the artistes, the meeting closed with the singing of " God save the King."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>MR. BIRRELL AND THE LORDS. "I Repeat What I Said at Warrington." _</head>
        <p>MR. BIRRELL AND THE LORDS. "I Repeat What I Said at Warrington." _</p>
        <p>Speaking at Nottingham last night week Mr. Birrell again referred to the House of Lords and the Licensing Bill. He said: "We have brought the question to a clear issue, and weans not going to turn back. (Cheers.) No member ot the House of Lords need suppose that this bill would ever be dropped. We have behind us a vast body of opinion—in fact, everybody intimately acquainted with the social life of the people. We have great force against us, and we are in Tor a fight. Do you think we do not know the influence of the taproom? I repeat what I said at Warrington, the whole moral forces of a nation are behind us. Whether they are or whether they are not, we mean to go on , fighting." (Cheers.) That brought him to the question of the House of Lords, which thought fit to slay the bill before it had been removed from the table of the House. That was the advantage of a chamber of revision, of a cool and collected body of men, not slaves to their constituents, but men who represented themselves and their own interests. (Laughter.) He did not think the House of Lords ever did a more foolish thing in all their lives than to accept this Licensing Bill as the one on which. to flout the determination and the expressed vote of the popular chamber. (Cheers.) It marked an extra- ordinary folly, which he owned he hardly expected the house to show (a Voice : " Reform them"). Well, the House of Lords were about to reform themselves, but he saw little in their suggestion which met the case. Under present conditions legislation was impossible. It was all very well to say "have a revolution," but they could not have a revolution every month. He gathered from what he had heard that evening that they did not want an immediate dissolution. It could not possibly be,because they thought they would be beaten. (Laughter.) They wanted the Government to go on, to do the best it could in the face of terrible odds, until such times as the best opportunity arose for taking the opinion of the country. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>NEXT GREAT FIGHT.</head>
        <p>THE NEXT GREAT FIGHT.</p>
        <p>At a Libera—Zee-tirlg-i-nWii-iVistOl—on Wednesday night a letter from Mr. Birrell to his constituents was read, in which the right hon. gentleman said :—" We shall have a great fight next time against the House of Lords and for Free Trade, and although the Govern- ment has already achieved many remarkable things, including the passing of the Old Age Pensions Act, we have been and are, thwarted in our desire to do more. It will be for the electorate to decide by whom it wishes to be governed —by those who represent electors or by those who represent themselves."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Special services were held at this church on Sunday the occasion being the annual choir festival. The church is fortunate in possessing such an enthusiastic and capable choir to lead its services and the large congregations-which assembled on Sunday were much ;repressed with the thoroughly adequate manner in which the music was rendered. At the morning service the anthem was "Judge me, 0 God "and Mrs. A. Booth sang " 0 had I Jubal's Lyre " with pleasing effect. In the evening the choir interpreted the anthem " Hark, bark my soul"and Miss Mabel Henshall and Mrs.A.Booth rendered solo parts. Mr. Frank Hickman was at the organ. The preacher at the morning service was the Rev. E. Blanshard Keeling who gave a powerful dis- course on "The grace of life." In the evening the Rev. Thomas Miller spoke on " Recreation and religion." St. Paul's advice to Timothy, " Exercise thyself unto godliness, for bodily exercise is profitable for a little, but godliness is profitable for all things," was the text. It was admitted that gymnastics were profitable for some things, but their beneficial influence was limited by advancing age and the grave. Christianity recog- nised the claims of the body. It was the instrument of the soul. By a mistranslation Paul had been repre- sented as describing the body as " vile," which WAS contrary to his general teaching. As the Temple of God, it was worthy of honour; it was sanctified by ,the indwelling Spirit ; the Resurrection manifested its share in the Redemption. The customs of Greece and Rome as described in Lord Lytton's "Last Days of Pompeii "were mentioned as being examples of excessive care for physical beauty. Plato, in his Crito, and especially in his Republic, drew some of his most valuable moral instruction from the gymnastic exercises prescribed as essential to education in his day. Recreation taught self-control. Games ought to be played in a gentle- manly fashion. Everything which would improve one's health should be practised ; the outlook upon life- greatly depending upon physical fitness or unfitness. The preacher did not agree with IL G. Wells in "First and Last Things " that professionalism should be impossible in a perfect social state. It was necessary to be as earnest in the attainment of godliness as in athletic evercises. Asceticism was a one-sided religious life. Self-control taught by games must be completed in self-dedication to Christ. Happiness which was they aim of our pleasures was only realized inChristian service. It was necessary to master the passions and appetites of the flesh and this victory could only be obtained by maintaining bodily health and spiritual vigour. Godli- ness was the result of exercise ; it was the growth and not a gift. The loftier ranges of Christian experience could only be reached when the whole natuee cleaves to God. By every wise kind of discipline our good desires must become resolutions and our resolutions must be translated into holy deed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>Warrington. Woman's Suicide.</head>
        <p>Warrington. Woman's Suicide.</p>
        <p>A verdict of "-Suicide whilst temporarily insane was returned at an inquest held at Grappenhall yesterday, by Mr. H. C. Yates on the body of Annie Balshaw, married woman, Knutsford-road, Warring- ton, which was found in the Bridgewater Canal at Grappenhall at 3.30 on Thursday afternoon. Deceased, who had been very depressed during the past fortnight, was seen in the neighbourhood of the canal about 2.45 but no one actually saw her jump into the water.</p>
