        <p>Warrington Phliomathic Society. SENSATIONAL ARREST. A meeting of the above society was held in Gregory's Cafe, Buttermarket-street, on Wednesday evening. Ur. H. H. Taylor delivered a lecture upon "Evolution ' tenarge Against Mr. Louis Henshall. which was illustrated by diagrams and many specimens of various forms of creation. The lecture was listened Mr. Louis Henshall, managing director for to with great interest by a large number of naembers. Messrs. R. A. Naylor, Ltd., timber merchants, Howley, A vote of thanks to the lecturer was proposed by Mr. D. Gladding and heartily passed. was arrested last night oa a warrant taken out by a Musical Successes. member of the firm chargiug him with stealing a sum The following pupils of Miss Mind Jennings, A.V.C.V., 140, Wilderspool-road, passed very success- of money of between £7O and .ZB3. Mr. Hen shall fully Victoria College, Manchester Ceutre. Miss has been with the fir.n for twenty-eight years. Clarice Daly, 9, St. Aristin's-lane, has gained the maximum number of marks (100) for theory and is The Chief Constable (Air. If. Nicholls), arrested Mr. duly elected Bronze Medallist of the College ; Ist (8 years at age), 133, Henshall at 8.35 grade piano, Sylvester Hall at hi a residence at Stockton Heath, Sankey-street, 93 marks first class honours out of a assisted by Sergeant Stuart, of the Warrington Police, possible 100 ; Bessie If. Jones, 123, Lovely-lane, 93 and Sergeant Barlow, of the Cheshire Constabulary. marks first class with honours ; 2nd grade, Lily Penketh, Stockton Heath, 88 marks first class honours_; Mr. Henshall was conveyed in a cab to the Pclice 3rd grade, Mabel Houghton, 11, Walker-street, 75 . marks first class. Station and will be brought before the magistrates .to- Unique Event at Messrs. J. &amp; W. Dutto day. Mr. TS. 's.Steel has been engaged for the defence. A unique and pleasing event, and one which will long he remembered as a red letter day in the minds of hundreds of Warrington children took place last Saturday afternoon at Messrs. J. and W. Dutton's, Sankey-street, when about 403 of the poorer children of the town, gathered from the various schools, were invited to view the Christmas display of toys, &amp;c. The whole of the basement had been transformed into a veritable fairyland with an immense Christmas tree laden with toys in the centre,and to still further convey the idea of Santa Claus, one of the staff was arrayed in a costume to represent Father Christmas, and after the visitors had enjoyed themselves thoroughly for nearly three hours, he presented each one on leasing with a toy. The presence of Mr. Dutton, who indi- vidually welcomed hie little guests and devoted him- self throughout the afternoon to their enjoyment, added greatly to their pleasure, and his generosity and thoughtfulness will not soon be forgotten. Co-operative Lecture. Another of the series of lectures arranged by the Educational Committee of the Co-operative Society, was delivered in Bold-street Wesleyan schoolroom on Tuesday by Councillor JanaeaJohnston, of Manchester, who took for his subject "Belgium, and the way in which the workers of that country use the profits made by co-operative methods."—Mr. James Ball presided over a large attendance, and spoke on the advantages of the co-operative movement, which he said could be hugely extended upon the very same principles that existed in limited companies. The lecturer said there would be no remedy for unemployment in this country until the land laws were entirely altered, and until the system of unlimited companies was done away with by a system of co-operation. In Belgium this was recog- nized as the root of unemployment, and they were working there with co-operative methods in order to do away with it. —Councillor Johnston gave numerous instances of the suocess of co-operative undertakings in Germany, showing how the profits were utilized for the benefit of the community. The lecture was fully illustrated by limelight views, which proved very interesting and instructive. Several selections were given during the evening by the Co-operative Male , Voice Choir. PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER nig CLOCK F3RBEST BOOTS IN LANCASIIIRE.—Advt. Jolly Anglers' Society. On Saturday evening this Society held its annual dinner at the " Bowling Green House," Liverpool- road, the headquarters of the Society. There were Ipresent the President of the Society (Mr. Frederick T. Ihe • nine) and Messrs. Samuel Jenks and William E. Clare (vice-presidents.) About 40 members and friends sat down to a sumptuous dinner, which bud been excellently prepared by Mc. and Mrs. Campbell, and justice was done to the same by all present. After dinner there was a smoking concert, when the President made some encourag ing remarks in connection with the Society. The prizes were distributed by the President to the winners, who were as follows :—Messrs. W. Melling, E. Lucy, C. Nurse, W. Boyle, W. Bate, W. White, W. Rooke, and W. H. Gandy. The toast of " The Jolly Anglers' Society" was next proposed by Mr. W. Robinson and was responded to by Mr. Aquila Cook. Songs by Messrs. W. White, C. Nurse, J. All cock and J. Gorst were beautifully rendered by the respec- tive artistes and highly appreciated by those present. The humorous portion of the programme was in the capable hands of Mr. John Oultrain whose excellent songs and recitations, coupled with his most amusing attitudes, greatly amused the company and met with loud applause. Numerous toasts were proposed, honoured, and responded to. Votes of thanks to Mr. and airs. Campbell for the excellent manner in which they had catered for the occasion ; to Mr. W. White for the capable manner in which he had carried out the duties of the secretary through his unavoidable absence, and to the artistes concluded a most success- ful and enjoyable evening. Mr. J. Gorst was the accompanist. BRASS AND COPPER HEARTTI SCITES, see window display for new and artistic patterns.—J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Adrt. A Respected Foreman. At the Warrington Cemetery on Saturday the funeral took place of the late Air. John Rosterne, patten- maker at the works of Pearson and Knowles. The deceased had been engaged with the firm for the last 40 years and was greatly respected, as was manifested by the large number of workpeople who assembled to witness the last rites. The Rev. E. C. E. Carleton officiated. The Engineering Department was repre- sented in all its branches,the procession being headed by the patten-makers cud moulders. The chief mourners were Mr. Harry Rosterne (son), Mr. R. Cooper (brother-in-law), and Messrs. Walter and Arthur Rosterne and James Cooper (nephews.) The deceased was carried to his last resting place by his fellow shop mates, viz., Messrs. A. Rigby, C. Little- wood, J. Boyle, J. Robinson, J. Hardman, and J. Knight. The Engineering Department staff was ' represented by Messrs. Thomas Atherton (manager), E. Massey (assistant manager)' G. L. Brimelow (chief draughtsman), Jonathan Atherton, J. Hope, T. Gilmore, A. Gilmore, T. Elwell, J. Lee, S. Allen, J. Hewitt, W. Scott, W. Bennett, W. Crawshaw, W. Hewitt, E. Hatton, J. Pennington, W. Morris, and G. Phillips; whilst the offices were represented by Messrs. J. Gibson, H. Rigby, J. Burn,H. Corst, E. Itimmer, W. Dennett, IV. K. Woods, K. J. Cuppage, F. Sharatt and S Partridge. Floral tributes were sent by the following, " His loving son and daughters," Mrs. William Baste] ne and family, Mr. and Mrs. Walter I Rosterue, Mr. and Mrs. It. Cooper, " His affectionate i nephew, Arthur," Mrs. Alfred Rosterne. Messrs. H. S. and IV. J. Bleakly, and the employs in the pattern shop, moulding department, fitting and turning shop, foremen engineering department, the engineering department, the engineering °flee staff, and Mr. Jonathan Atherton and family. LINSEED COMPOUND' (Aniseed, Senega, Squill Ay.), for Coughs. Of Chemists only.—Advt. Tree Planting at Wycliffe. The pleasures of tree planting were decidedly marred on Tuesday afternoon by the heavy downpour of rain, but a fair number of the hardier gpirits of the Beautiful Warrington Society turned up to witness the planting of seven trees in front of Wycliffe Church- Bewsey:street. Amongst those present were : The Revs. Canon Stevenson, John Yonge, and C. Harvey, Cook, Aldermen Smethurst and Bennett, Mr. E. J. Hall, Mr. John Fairhurst, Mr. G. Wallington, Mr. G. 11. Smith (secretary), Miss Porritt and Miss Corney.— Canon Sterenson opened the proceedings by saying that they were there to further the work of beautifying the good old town of Warrington. The Society had long had their eyes on that spot and they hoped to see at before long one of the beenty spots of Warrington. The society was indebted to the kind friends in the congregation who had provided the trees for that part of the town. The Rev. John Yonge welcomed the members of the Beautiful Warrington Society and said they could congratulate themselves upon having Canon Stevenson with them. He just received a letter from Mr. Armitnee saying that he he came in town in order to attend the tree planting but the downpour of rain had" put him entirely out of court." He was sorry not to pay his respects to Canon Stevenson.—Mr. George Wallington stated that it was the society's intension to plant the trees in that spot last year, but no 'trees were to be had, owing to the demand for them in town and country.— The ceremony was then proceeded with on the Bewseys street side of the church. Canon Stevenson was the first to plant a tree, and others were planted by Rev. John Yonge, Miss Porritt, Miss Corney and Alderman Smethurst, the Rev. Harvey Cook and Mr. R. Travis. Four other trees were subsequently planted on the Edgeworth-street side of the church. Alderman Smethurst remarked that it would always give him pleasure to look upon those trees when passing that way.—The party then proceeded to Church-street when trees were planted in front of the hoardings opposite Messrs. Ryland's works, by Canon Stevenson, the Rev. C. Harvey Cook, Alderman Bennett and Mr. G. H. Smith.</p>
